(function(){window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES=window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES||{};window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES["advanced-liberian-law"]={"M1:Separation of Powers and Constitutional Interpretation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 1</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: SEPARATION OF POWERS AND CONSTITUTIONAL INTERPRETATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitutional Law</td><td>Separation of Powers and Constitutional Interpretation</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain separation of powers and constitutional interpretation using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Constitutional interpretation determines the legal meaning of a constitutional provision. Institutional legitimacy concerns why an actor may exercise a particular power. A desirable outcome does not itself supply constitutional authority.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Supremacy</td><td>Priority of constitutional requirements over inconsistent subordinate rules</td></tr><tr><td>Interpretation</td><td>Reasoned determination of legal meaning</td></tr><tr><td>Institutional competence</td><td>Authority assigned to an institution for a particular function</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Start with the disputed action and the constitutional language allocating that function. Read related provisions together rather than isolating a convenient phrase. Text, structure, institutional purpose and precedent can support different interpretations; explain which approach resolves the actual ambiguity. A comparison with another constitution is an argument, not a source of Liberian institutional power.</p><p>Distinguish a rule about who decides from a rule about what may be decided. A legislature may possess a subject-matter power while remaining constrained by rights and procedure. Likewise, judicial review is not a licence to redesign every policy. An advanced answer evaluates the strongest institutional counterargument and the consequences of the proposed interpretation without substituting policy preference for law.</p><p>A structural argument needs a limiting principle. If the ministry may override a statute for efficiency, ask what would prevent the same reasoning from displacing every legislative constraint. Conversely, if every administrative detail required a new Act, explain how lawful delegation could function. Compare these consequences, then return to the actual constitutional and statutory language. Institutional convenience can support an interpretation only within a defensible account of the text.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a ministry announces that its circular overrides a statute and bars judicial challenge. A weak response calls the circular unfair. A stronger analysis identifies the hierarchy of norms, the claimed delegated power and the constitutional judicial function. If the circular merely implements an authorised technical detail, the analysis differs from one that contradicts the Act. Obtain the full instrument before deciding.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Articles 2, 3, 11, 20 and 65–66 of the Constitution. Select one published Supreme Court constitutional decision; record its issue, holding and remedial limits.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Separation of Powers and Constitutional Interpretation”, start by separating supremacy from interpretation. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Constitutional interpretation determines the legal meaning of a constitutional provision. Institutional legitimacy concerns why an actor may exercise a particular power. A desirable outcome does not itself supply constitutional authority.</li><li>Separate supremacy from interpretation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Supremacy</td><td>Priority of constitutional requirements over inconsistent subordinate rules</td></tr><tr><td>Interpretation</td><td>Reasoned determination of legal meaning</td></tr><tr><td>Institutional competence</td><td>Authority assigned to an institution for a particular function</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Supremacy</summary><p>Priority of constitutional requirements over inconsistent subordinate rules</p></details><details><summary>Interpretation</summary><p>Reasoned determination of legal meaning</p></details><details><summary>Institutional competence</summary><p>Authority assigned to an institution for a particular function</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A circular contradicts its enabling Act. Which inquiry comes first?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Assume every circular amends legislation</li><li>Choose whichever text is shorter</li><li>Identify its legal authority and the hierarchy of norms</li><li>Count supportive comments online</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Validity depends on legal authority and superior constraints.</p></details><p><strong>Why compare the disputed clause with related constitutional provisions?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Related provisions have no legal relevance</li><li>Every provision gives identical powers</li><li>A comparison replaces reading the text</li><li>The constitutional structure can clarify the allocation of power</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> A provision operates within the constitutional structure.</p></details><p><strong>How should a foreign constitutional decision be used here?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>As a reasoned comparison whose local applicability must be justified</li><li>As an automatic amendment to Liberia’s Constitution</li><li>As binding merely because it is recent</li><li>As proof that no local research is needed</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Comparative reasoning cannot replace applicable Liberian authority.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Supremacy and Interpretation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of supremacy and interpretation. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned constitutional law research task: Write a 900-word competing-interpretations note on the circular, with a hierarchy diagram, two plausible readings and a defended conclusion. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 900-word competing-interpretations note on the circular, with a hierarchy diagram, two plausible readings and a defended conclusion.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M1:Constitutional Review, Standing and Remedial Design": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 1</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONSTITUTIONAL REVIEW, STANDING AND REMEDIAL DESIGN</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitutional Law</td><td>Constitutional Review, Standing and Remedial Design</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain constitutional review, standing and remedial design using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A constitutional remedy is a legally available response to an established constitutional defect. Access to review, the merits of a claim and the scope of relief are distinct stages of analysis.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standing</td><td>Entitlement to invoke a particular legal process</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>Whether the legal claim is substantively established</td></tr><tr><td>Remedial fit</td><td>Connection between the proven defect and the requested legal response</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the claimant’s affected interest, the challenged public act and the competent route. Verify standing, jurisdiction and any applicable procedural requirements in Liberian sources. A general concern about government does not answer every access question. Conversely, uncertainty about standing should be analysed rather than used to ignore a serious rights issue.</p><p>Match each requested order to a particular defect. A new hearing addresses procedural unfairness; setting aside an instrument addresses invalidity; compensation requires its own foundation. Explain the effects on third parties and the remaining discretion of the responsible institution. A carefully limited order can vindicate legality without prescribing the final policy outcome. Discuss practical implementation and what evidence would be needed to justify broader relief.</p><p>Develop a remedy ladder. Identify the minimum action needed to correct the defect, the additional facts required for broader relief and the institutional consequences of each order. If a lawful hearing could produce the same result, explain why procedural vindication still matters. Distinguish an effective remedy from a guarantee of the claimant’s preferred outcome; the former addresses the right established, while the latter may exceed what the record supports.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a public board cancels a training provider’s licence without supplying reasons. The provider asks a court to award a permanent licence and compensation immediately. Separate a challenge to the process from proof of substantive entitlement. The board may have safety evidence that requires a lawful hearing, while the provider still needs an effective response to an unexplained cancellation.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Articles 2, 3, 11, 20 and 65–66 of the Constitution. Select one published Supreme Court constitutional decision; record its issue, holding and remedial limits.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Constitutional Review, Standing and Remedial Design”, start by separating standing from merits. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A constitutional remedy is a legally available response to an established constitutional defect. Access to review, the merits of a claim and the scope of relief are distinct stages of analysis.</li><li>Separate standing from merits.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standing</td><td>Entitlement to invoke a particular legal process</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>Whether the legal claim is substantively established</td></tr><tr><td>Remedial fit</td><td>Connection between the proven defect and the requested legal response</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Standing</summary><p>Entitlement to invoke a particular legal process</p></details><details><summary>Merits</summary><p>Whether the legal claim is substantively established</p></details><details><summary>Remedial fit</summary><p>Connection between the proven defect and the requested legal response</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A licence cancellation lacks a hearing. What does that alone establish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An unconditional right to every licence</li><li>A guaranteed damages amount</li><li>The abolition of safety regulation</li><li>A procedural issue requiring analysis, not automatic permanent entitlement</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> A procedural defect and substantive eligibility are different questions.</p></details><p><strong>Why investigate the claimant’s route and standing separately from the merits?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A strong allegation does not by itself resolve access and jurisdiction</li><li>Standing means the claimant must physically stand</li><li>Merits always create jurisdiction</li><li>All public disputes follow one identical route</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Review requires a competent process as well as an arguable claim.</p></details><p><strong>Which proposed order needs its own legal and evidential foundation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A list of missing documents</li><li>Compensation for proven loss</li><li>A request to read the governing Act</li><li>A heading identifying the claimant</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Compensatory relief cannot be assumed from disagreement with a decision.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Standing and Merits.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of standing and merits. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned constitutional law research task: Draft a 1,000-word constitutional remedies memorandum with primary citations, a procedural research checklist and narrowly worded alternative orders. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a 1,000-word constitutional remedies memorandum with primary citations, a procedural research checklist and narrowly worded alternative orders.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M2:Offence Elements, Mental States and Evidential Inference": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 2</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: OFFENCE ELEMENTS, MENTAL STATES AND EVIDENTIAL INFERENCE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Law</td><td>Offence Elements, Mental States and Evidential Inference</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain offence elements, mental states and evidential inference using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An offence is constituted by the elements specified in applicable criminal law. A mental element concerns the required state of mind; an evidential inference is a reasoned conclusion drawn from proved facts, not a substitute for the legal requirement.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Element</td><td>A legally required component of the offence</td></tr><tr><td>Inference</td><td>A conclusion supported by evidence rather than directly observed</td></tr><tr><td>Temporal concurrence</td><td>The legally relevant relationship between conduct and required mental state</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Break the offence into conduct, circumstances, results and the mental requirement attached to each relevant component. Do not assume that every offence requires the same intention. Where a result is required, connect the alleged conduct to that result under the governing causal rule. An unpopular defendant is still entitled to element-by-element proof.</p><p>Separate what evidence shows from what it merely permits a fact-finder to infer. Possession of an item may support competing accounts about knowledge or control. Analyse timing: knowledge acquired after an event does not automatically establish the earlier state of mind. Consider the evidential basis for innocent explanations, and identify the prosecution burden without inventing numerical probabilities for proof.</p><p>Compare the prosecution and defence through the same element grid. For each contested inference, state the evidence, the alternative explanation and the reason one account may be stronger. Avoid reversing the burden by requiring the courier to prove innocence merely because the situation is suspicious. The legal conclusion should identify the precise element affected by each uncertainty, rather than treating all doubt as one undifferentiated weakness.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a Monrovia courier carries a sealed parcel containing stolen goods. Messages show delivery instructions but not knowledge of the contents. A weak analysis equates possession with guilt. A strong one identifies the charged offence, examines control and the required mental element, compares suspicious circumstances with ordinary courier practice and identifies evidence that could distinguish the explanations.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the applicable offence and general responsibility provisions in the Penal Law. Check amendments and verify the particular conduct, mental element, participation rule and defence before using a comparative model.</p><p><a href=\"https://assets.tobaccocontrollaws.org/uploads/legislation/Liberia/Liberia-Penal-Law-native.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Penal Law, Title 26 — primary statute reproduction (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Offence Elements, Mental States and Evidential Inference”, start by separating element from inference. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An offence is constituted by the elements specified in applicable criminal law. A mental element concerns the required state of mind; an evidential inference is a reasoned conclusion drawn from proved facts, not a substitute for the legal requirement.</li><li>Separate element from inference.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Element</td><td>A legally required component of the offence</td></tr><tr><td>Inference</td><td>A conclusion supported by evidence rather than directly observed</td></tr><tr><td>Temporal concurrence</td><td>The legally relevant relationship between conduct and required mental state</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Element</summary><p>A legally required component of the offence</p></details><details><summary>Inference</summary><p>A conclusion supported by evidence rather than directly observed</p></details><details><summary>Temporal concurrence</summary><p>The legally relevant relationship between conduct and required mental state</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>The courier learns the parcel is stolen only after delivery. Why does timing matter?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Later knowledge proves all earlier intentions</li><li>Time never affects responsibility</li><li>Delivery cancels the offence definition</li><li>The required mental state must be linked to the legally relevant conduct</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> An offence requires the prescribed relationship between its elements.</p></details><p><strong>What does an elements matrix prevent?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Treating a suspicious narrative as proof of every required component</li><li>The use of primary statutes</li><li>Consideration of defences</li><li>Reasoned factual inference</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Each element needs its own legal and evidential analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Which description best treats the sealed parcel evidence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It removes the burden of proof</li><li>It may support competing inferences that must be tested</li><li>It conclusively proves knowledge in every case</li><li>It is always irrelevant</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Evidence must be evaluated against the actual mental and conduct requirements.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Element and Inference.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of element and inference. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned criminal law research task: Prepare a charge-elements matrix and a 900-word analysis of the courier, including an alternative explanation and five targeted evidence requests. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a charge-elements matrix and a 900-word analysis of the courier, including an alternative explanation and five targeted evidence requests.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M2:Attempt, Conspiracy and Individual Participation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 2</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ATTEMPT, CONSPIRACY AND INDIVIDUAL PARTICIPATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Law</td><td>Attempt, Conspiracy and Individual Participation</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain attempt, conspiracy and individual participation using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Inchoate liability addresses legally defined preparatory or incomplete wrongdoing. Participation liability concerns an individual’s contribution to an offence. Agreement, assistance, intention and completed harm are separate matters requiring their own legal foundations.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Attempt</td><td>Incomplete offending meeting the applicable statutory threshold</td></tr><tr><td>Agreement</td><td>A shared commitment, where legally required, distinct from parallel thought</td></tr><tr><td>Individual attribution</td><td>Linking a particular person to legally relevant conduct and fault</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Draw a chronology for each person. Identify the alleged agreement, the proposed offence, acts taken and any asserted withdrawal. Mere association should not be silently converted into an agreement or intentional assistance. Verify the local statutory threshold for attempt rather than importing a foreign phrase as the test.</p><p>Collective events create a risk of guilt by proximity. Attribute communications and actions individually and examine their meaning in context. A message may be joking, conditional or directed toward lawful activity. If the prosecution alleges a common plan, test both its existence and the defendant’s connection to it. Explain how an asserted abandonment affects liability under the applicable provisions; it does not automatically erase completed conduct.</p><p>Distinguish an agreement’s content from the consequences later attributed to it. A person may agree to one act while disputing knowledge of a further offence. Research whether the applicable participation provision reaches the additional conduct and what mental requirement it prescribes. The group’s final action cannot retrospectively prove every earlier participant’s scope of agreement; timing, communications and individual conduct remain central.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: three people discuss taking a company generator, but one objects and leaves before another enters the yard. The objection, planning messages and later assistance must be assessed separately. A weak answer calls everyone a conspirator because they met together. A strong answer distinguishes discussion, agreement, preparation and completed taking, reserving conclusions until the offence and participation provisions are verified.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the applicable offence and general responsibility provisions in the Penal Law. Check amendments and verify the particular conduct, mental element, participation rule and defence before using a comparative model.</p><p><a href=\"https://assets.tobaccocontrollaws.org/uploads/legislation/Liberia/Liberia-Penal-Law-native.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Penal Law, Title 26 — primary statute reproduction (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Attempt, Conspiracy and Individual Participation”, start by separating attempt from agreement. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Inchoate liability addresses legally defined preparatory or incomplete wrongdoing. Participation liability concerns an individual’s contribution to an offence. Agreement, assistance, intention and completed harm are separate matters requiring their own legal foundations.</li><li>Separate attempt from agreement.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Attempt</td><td>Incomplete offending meeting the applicable statutory threshold</td></tr><tr><td>Agreement</td><td>A shared commitment, where legally required, distinct from parallel thought</td></tr><tr><td>Individual attribution</td><td>Linking a particular person to legally relevant conduct and fault</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Attempt</summary><p>Incomplete offending meeting the applicable statutory threshold</p></details><details><summary>Agreement</summary><p>A shared commitment, where legally required, distinct from parallel thought</p></details><details><summary>Individual attribution</summary><p>Linking a particular person to legally relevant conduct and fault</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Three people are present during planning. What must be established individually?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The relevant agreement, conduct and fault required for each person’s liability</li><li>The same liability solely from shared location</li><li>Automatic guilt for leaving early</li><li>Collective wealth</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Association does not replace the prescribed participation requirements.</p></details><p><strong>Why should a learner avoid treating any preparation as an attempt?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Attempt has no statutory elements</li><li>The applicable law defines the threshold between preparation and punishable attempt</li><li>Preparation is never evidence</li><li>All planned acts are completed crimes</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> The threshold must be sourced and applied to the actual acts.</p></details><p><strong>What is the sound treatment of a disputed withdrawal message?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Ignore it because the group is unpopular</li><li>Rewrite it to strengthen the defence</li><li>Examine authenticity, timing, meaning and the applicable legal effect</li><li>Assume it erases all prior conduct</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Withdrawal raises factual and legal questions requiring separate analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Attempt and Agreement.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of attempt and agreement. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned criminal law research task: Write a 1,000-word comparative responsibility opinion with one chronology per person and a separate analysis of the alleged withdrawal. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,000-word comparative responsibility opinion with one chronology per person and a separate analysis of the alleged withdrawal.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M3:Liberty, Bail and Procedural Safeguards": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 3</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LIBERTY, BAIL AND PROCEDURAL SAFEGUARDS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Procedure</td><td>Liberty, Bail and Procedural Safeguards</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain liberty, bail and procedural safeguards using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Pre-trial restraint is a procedural measure, not a finding of guilt. Bail analysis concerns the legally available conditions of release and the reasons relevant to them; punishment presupposes a different legal foundation.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Custody record</td><td>Dated evidence of detention and related decisions</td></tr><tr><td>Bail condition</td><td>Legally authorised requirement attached to release</td></tr><tr><td>Presumption of innocence</td><td>Protection against treating accusation as established guilt</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Create a custody timeline: arrest, stated grounds, access to counsel, first appearance, applications and orders. Distinguish a person’s allegation about detention from an authenticated order or custody record. Constitutional safeguards and procedural legislation must be read together, including qualifications in the text.</p><p>A reasoned application identifies the relevant legal test and connects proposed conditions to supported risks. Generic assertions that everyone accused is dangerous do not assess the particular record. Equally, a right to seek release does not predetermine its result. Consider the practical effect of proposed conditions and the information needed to test necessity or proportionality where those concepts are legally applicable. Never import a foreign custody deadline into a Liberian answer.</p><p>Consider the difference between a risk assertion and a workable response. If interference with a named witness is alleged, identify evidence of access and communication, then evaluate whether a lawful condition could address that concern. If the proposed condition cannot realistically be met, discuss its practical effect rather than describing it as neutral. The application should show both its evidential basis and its relationship to the governing release framework.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a student is arrested after a disputed warehouse incident. The application offers a verified address and reporting arrangements; the opposition alleges witness interference without identifying a source. A balanced analysis investigates the allegation, the governing release provisions and workable conditions. It does not promise release or treat the allegation as a conviction.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Constitution Article 21 with the Criminal Procedure Law and current court rules. Verify custody, access to counsel, bail and pre-trial procedures against the actual provisions.</p><p><a href=\"https://natlex.ilo.org/dyn/natlex2/natlex2/files/download/42875/LBR42875.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Criminal Procedure Law, Title 2 — primary code reproduction hosted by ILO (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Liberty, Bail and Procedural Safeguards”, start by separating custody record from bail condition. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Pre-trial restraint is a procedural measure, not a finding of guilt. Bail analysis concerns the legally available conditions of release and the reasons relevant to them; punishment presupposes a different legal foundation.</li><li>Separate custody record from bail condition.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Custody record</td><td>Dated evidence of detention and related decisions</td></tr><tr><td>Bail condition</td><td>Legally authorised requirement attached to release</td></tr><tr><td>Presumption of innocence</td><td>Protection against treating accusation as established guilt</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Custody record</summary><p>Dated evidence of detention and related decisions</p></details><details><summary>Bail condition</summary><p>Legally authorised requirement attached to release</p></details><details><summary>Presumption of innocence</summary><p>Protection against treating accusation as established guilt</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why distinguish a release decision from conviction?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The procedural question does not determine guilt</li><li>Bail automatically dismisses the charge</li><li>An accusation proves the offence</li><li>Release conditions replace trial evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Pre-trial decisions and final criminal responsibility serve different functions.</p></details><p><strong>An opposition alleges witness interference without a source. What is appropriate?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Invent a witness statement</li><li>Identify the evidential gap and seek particulars before assessing its weight</li><li>Treat the allegation as a final finding</li><li>Dismiss every possible risk automatically</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Reasons should be evaluated against supported facts.</p></details><p><strong>What makes a proposed release condition analytically useful?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its similarity to a foreign television case</li><li>A promise that no trial will occur</li><li>A lawful and practical connection to an identified relevant risk</li><li>Its maximum inconvenience alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Conditions need a legal foundation and a reasoned purpose.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Custody record and Bail condition.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of custody record and bail condition. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned criminal procedure research task: Prepare a fictional bail memorandum, custody chronology and proposed conditions linked to the evidence; verify every procedural deadline you mention. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional bail memorandum, custody chronology and proposed conditions linked to the evidence; verify every procedural deadline you mention.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M3:Charging, Disclosure and Pre-Trial Case Management": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 3</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CHARGING, DISCLOSURE AND PRE-TRIAL CASE MANAGEMENT</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Procedure</td><td>Charging, Disclosure and Pre-Trial Case Management</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain charging, disclosure and pre-trial case management using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A charge defines the alleged offence; disclosure concerns access to material through the applicable process. Case management organises issues and hearings while preserving the parties’ procedural rights.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Particulars</td><td>Specific allegations needed to identify the case to be answered</td></tr><tr><td>Disclosure schedule</td><td>Structured account of material sought, held and outstanding</td></tr><tr><td>Procedural prejudice</td><td>Concrete disadvantage affecting the ability to use a fair process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Compare the charge with the statutory elements and particulars. A charge sheet is not evidence proving the alleged event. Identify what the defence needs to understand and answer, and research the local route for requesting records, particulars or other relief. Do not assume that a foreign discovery regime applies.</p><p>Build a disclosure schedule stating each item, custodian, relevance, requested action and status. Preserve the distinction between material not yet obtained and material said not to exist. Where a record is sensitive, evaluate lawful protection and fair access rather than assuming either unrestricted publication or complete secrecy. Draft applications around the specific procedural prejudice and the available remedy; explain why a narrower order would or would not address it.</p><p>A disclosure request should explain materiality without pretending to know the missing record’s contents. State what issue it could illuminate, why the available extract is inadequate and how the proposed order would permit fair preparation. If the record no longer exists, distinguish a preservation failure, an evidential inference and a procedural remedy. Each response needs its own applicable authority and cannot be assumed from the fact of absence alone.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a theft charge refers to CCTV, but only a still image has been supplied and the recording’s date is disputed. The defence should identify the missing original, surrounding footage and extraction record. The prosecution’s explanation and retention policy matter too. An effective case plan requests identifiable material and a competent ruling, rather than claiming every missing item automatically ends the case.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Constitution Article 21 with the Criminal Procedure Law and current court rules. Verify custody, access to counsel, bail and pre-trial procedures against the actual provisions.</p><p><a href=\"https://natlex.ilo.org/dyn/natlex2/natlex2/files/download/42875/LBR42875.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Criminal Procedure Law, Title 2 — primary code reproduction hosted by ILO (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Charging, Disclosure and Pre-Trial Case Management”, start by separating particulars from disclosure schedule. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A charge defines the alleged offence; disclosure concerns access to material through the applicable process. Case management organises issues and hearings while preserving the parties’ procedural rights.</li><li>Separate particulars from disclosure schedule.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Particulars</td><td>Specific allegations needed to identify the case to be answered</td></tr><tr><td>Disclosure schedule</td><td>Structured account of material sought, held and outstanding</td></tr><tr><td>Procedural prejudice</td><td>Concrete disadvantage affecting the ability to use a fair process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Particulars</summary><p>Specific allegations needed to identify the case to be answered</p></details><details><summary>Disclosure schedule</summary><p>Structured account of material sought, held and outstanding</p></details><details><summary>Procedural prejudice</summary><p>Concrete disadvantage affecting the ability to use a fair process</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A charge sheet alleges theft. What is its evidential status?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It shifts every burden automatically</li><li>It states the accusation and does not itself prove its elements</li><li>It is a final conviction</li><li>It authenticates every prosecution exhibit</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Accusation and proof must remain separate.</p></details><p><strong>Why request the original CCTV recording and extraction record?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Video records are never evidence</li><li>Originals automatically establish guilt</li><li>They can clarify completeness, timing and provenance</li><li>A still image is always fabricated</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Reliability depends partly on context and provenance.</p></details><p><strong>What should a request for sensitive records address?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic public release of every record</li><li>Permanent secrecy regardless of fairness</li><li>Invented witness identities</li><li>Relevance, lawful access and proportionate protective arrangements</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Fair access and legitimate protection require a reasoned local procedure.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Particulars and Disclosure schedule.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of particulars and disclosure schedule. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned criminal procedure research task: Draft a disclosure schedule and 1,000-word pre-trial application addressing the CCTV record, confidentiality and alternative relief. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a disclosure schedule and 1,000-word pre-trial application addressing the CCTV record, confidentiality and alternative relief.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M4:Jurisdiction, Service and Strategic Forum Analysis": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 4</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: JURISDICTION, SERVICE AND STRATEGIC FORUM ANALYSIS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil Procedure</td><td>Jurisdiction, Service and Strategic Forum Analysis</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain jurisdiction, service and strategic forum analysis using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Jurisdiction concerns legal authority to adjudicate. Venue concerns an authorised location. Service provides notice through the legally prescribed mechanism; actual awareness does not necessarily cure every procedural defect.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Subject-matter jurisdiction</td><td>Authority over the type of dispute</td></tr><tr><td>Venue</td><td>Permitted geographical place for proceedings</td></tr><tr><td>Service</td><td>Legally prescribed delivery of process with required proof</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with the cause of action, parties, territorial connections and requested relief. A convenient courthouse is not automatically competent. Distinguish subject-matter authority, authority over the relevant parties and any specialised statutory route. Record what source establishes each proposition rather than relying on the forum named by a claimant.</p><p>Analyse service as a sequence of authorised steps and proof, including the document served, recipient, method and return. A jurisdiction objection may have its own procedural requirements; investigate them before drafting an appearance. Strategic analysis must remain lawful: compare efficiency, access and enforceability without selecting a forum simply to evade another party’s rights. Explain how an uncertain classification would change the route.</p><p>Develop a branching forum opinion: if the claim is classified one way, identify the source authorising the first route; if classified another way, identify the alternative and its consequences. Explain which missing fact decides the branch. This is more useful than listing every court. A procedural recommendation should also account for recognition of service, response obligations and preservation of objections under the verified local rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a Buchanan supplier sues a Monrovia buyer over equipment delivered in Kakata and sends a photograph of the claim to an employee. The learner must identify the competent court and permitted service procedure from current local sources. Neither the delivery town nor the photographed document alone resolves all forum and notice questions.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Civil Procedure Law, Judiciary Law and relevant court rules. Verify forum, service, interlocutory relief and appeal procedures without copying foreign monetary limits or deadlines.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Jurisdiction, Service and Strategic Forum Analysis”, start by separating subject-matter jurisdiction from venue. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Jurisdiction concerns legal authority to adjudicate. Venue concerns an authorised location. Service provides notice through the legally prescribed mechanism; actual awareness does not necessarily cure every procedural defect.</li><li>Separate subject-matter jurisdiction from venue.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Subject-matter jurisdiction</td><td>Authority over the type of dispute</td></tr><tr><td>Venue</td><td>Permitted geographical place for proceedings</td></tr><tr><td>Service</td><td>Legally prescribed delivery of process with required proof</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Subject-matter jurisdiction</summary><p>Authority over the type of dispute</p></details><details><summary>Venue</summary><p>Permitted geographical place for proceedings</p></details><details><summary>Service</summary><p>Legally prescribed delivery of process with required proof</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A courthouse is nearest to the claimant. What further question is essential?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether travel is unpleasant</li><li>Whether law gives it authority over the dispute and parties</li><li>Whether its sign is visible</li><li>Whether the claimant prefers its décor</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Convenience alone does not establish jurisdiction.</p></details><p><strong>A defendant saw a claim photograph. What should the analysis avoid?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Reading court rules</li><li>Checking proof of delivery</li><li>Assuming awareness automatically satisfies every service requirement</li><li>Investigating the recipient</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The prescribed legal procedure must be verified.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate subject-matter jurisdiction from venue?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Venue always creates substantive rights</li><li>Every court has identical competence</li><li>A venue objection proves the claim false</li><li>They answer different authority and location questions</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Authority over a dispute and its permitted location are distinct.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Subject-matter jurisdiction and Venue.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of subject-matter jurisdiction and venue. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned civil procedure research task: Prepare a forum-and-service opinion with a source for each jurisdictional proposition and a checklist of documents needed to evaluate the attempted service. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a forum-and-service opinion with a source for each jurisdictional proposition and a checklist of documents needed to evaluate the attempted service.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M4:Interlocutory Relief, Injunctions and Proportional Orders": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 4</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INTERLOCUTORY RELIEF, INJUNCTIONS AND PROPORTIONAL ORDERS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil Procedure</td><td>Interlocutory Relief, Injunctions and Proportional Orders</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain interlocutory relief, injunctions and proportional orders using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Interlocutory relief addresses a problem before final determination. An injunction directs or restrains conduct where legally available; it is not a substitute for proving the entire claim at trial.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interlocutory</td><td>Operating before final determination</td></tr><tr><td>Preservation</td><td>Maintaining an asset or evidence pending resolution</td></tr><tr><td>Specificity</td><td>Sufficiently clear identification of required or prohibited conduct</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the threatened act, urgency, evidence and exact order sought. Then verify the applicable local test and procedural route. Distinguish preservation from final disposition: temporarily securing disputed goods differs from permanently transferring ownership. An affidavit should separate personal knowledge, documents and attributed information.</p><p>Consider whether the proposed order is workable, sufficiently specific and no broader than its lawful purpose requires. Address affected third parties and possible alternatives, such as preserving records or maintaining a defined status quo. Discuss any applicable undertaking or security requirement only after checking local authority. An advanced application openly confronts evidence that weakens urgency and explains why ordinary final relief may be insufficient.</p><p>Test the proposed order as if you had to comply with it. Identify the asset, conduct, duration and person bound. Ambiguity can create enforcement disputes even where the claimant has a strong underlying case. Compare the cost of refusing relief with the cost of an order later found unwarranted, to the extent those considerations are legally relevant. Explain why the chosen scope preserves rather than prejudges the disputed entitlement.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a disputed generator will be moved tomorrow, but the owner’s identity and the sale agreement are contested. A learner proposes banning all trade by the defendant. A stronger draft targets the identified asset, provides the source of the urgency claim and considers a preservation arrangement. It leaves title for a competent decision on the full record.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Civil Procedure Law, Judiciary Law and relevant court rules. Verify forum, service, interlocutory relief and appeal procedures without copying foreign monetary limits or deadlines.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Interlocutory Relief, Injunctions and Proportional Orders”, start by separating interlocutory from preservation. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Interlocutory relief addresses a problem before final determination. An injunction directs or restrains conduct where legally available; it is not a substitute for proving the entire claim at trial.</li><li>Separate interlocutory from preservation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interlocutory</td><td>Operating before final determination</td></tr><tr><td>Preservation</td><td>Maintaining an asset or evidence pending resolution</td></tr><tr><td>Specificity</td><td>Sufficiently clear identification of required or prohibited conduct</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Interlocutory</summary><p>Operating before final determination</p></details><details><summary>Preservation</summary><p>Maintaining an asset or evidence pending resolution</p></details><details><summary>Specificity</summary><p>Sufficiently clear identification of required or prohibited conduct</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What distinguishes preservation of a generator from final transfer of ownership?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Preservation creates a new statute</li><li>Ownership never requires proof</li><li>Preservation maintains the position pending a decision rather than deciding title</li><li>Both automatically decide the entire case</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Interim and final relief have different effects.</p></details><p><strong>An urgency affidavit repeats an unnamed rumour. What is needed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>More dramatic adjectives</li><li>Removal of all dates</li><li>An invented personal observation</li><li>Clear attribution and supporting evidence for the threatened act</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Urgency must be supported by a candid evidential account.</p></details><p><strong>Why address a narrower alternative to banning all business activity?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The order should fit the problem and its lawful basis</li><li>The broadest order is always mandatory</li><li>Courts cannot identify assets</li><li>Third parties never matter</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Remedial design should consider scope and practical effects.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Interlocutory and Preservation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of interlocutory and preservation. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned civil procedure research task: Draft a preservation application, evidence schedule and two alternative orders, then explain in 800 words why your preferred order fits the verified test. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a preservation application, evidence schedule and two alternative orders, then explain in 800 words why your preferred order fits the verified test.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M4:Public Law and Litigation Research File": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 4 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Public Law and Litigation Research File</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional public board cancels a licence and commences proceedings against the provider. Construct a record that separates constitutional review, civil procedure and criminal safeguards.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A 1,200-word research memorandum with a narrowly framed question, verified primary authorities and one adverse argument per issue</li><li>A source-status log, procedural chronology and forum map distinguishing civil and criminal routes</li><li>Two alternative relief sections explaining scope, authority and factual assumptions</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M5:Contract Interpretation, Risk Allocation and Exclusion Terms": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 5</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONTRACT INTERPRETATION, RISK ALLOCATION AND EXCLUSION TERMS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law of Contracts</td><td>Contract Interpretation, Risk Allocation and Exclusion Terms</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain contract interpretation, risk allocation and exclusion terms using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Interpretation determines the meaning of an agreement under the governing law. Incorporation asks whether a term became part of the agreement. An exclusion term seeks to limit responsibility; its existence, scope and enforceability are separate questions.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incorporation</td><td>Whether a term entered the agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Construction</td><td>Determination of a term’s meaning in context</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion clause</td><td>Term seeking to exclude or limit specified responsibility</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Collect the complete document set: offer, acceptance, standard terms, schedules, later variations and communications. Identify the point of agreement and competing versions. A clause printed on a later invoice may raise a different issue from a signed schedule supplied before assent. Do not jump from a signature to the conclusion that every possible limitation is effective.</p><p>Read the disputed term within the commercial arrangement. Ask what event activates it, which loss it covers and what other provisions qualify it. Distinguish an interpretive ambiguity from an enforceability objection. A balanced risk analysis considers each party’s best construction, any mandatory local rule and the evidence needed to resolve uncertainty. Draft a clearer alternative that allocates a defined risk without promising immunity the law does not permit.</p><p>Use a four-stage clause audit: agreement, meaning, legal control and remedy. A clause may fail at one stage even if it survives another. For example, clear wording cannot answer whether the buyer ever agreed to it, while proof of agreement cannot defeat a mandatory statutory limitation. State the stage at which each party’s argument operates and avoid allowing a conclusion on one issue to silently determine all the others.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a solar supplier’s signed contract promises installation, while a later invoice says “no liability whatsoever.” Equipment arrives damaged. The customer argues the limitation was never agreed; the supplier relies on its usual terms. Analyse timing, incorporation, scope and applicable controls separately, then distinguish damage in transit from negligent installation.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use applicable Liberian commercial legislation and published contract decisions. Identify the transaction type and verify the governing rule for interpretation, vitiating factors and remedies.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Contract Interpretation, Risk Allocation and Exclusion Terms”, start by separating incorporation from construction. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Interpretation determines the meaning of an agreement under the governing law. Incorporation asks whether a term became part of the agreement. An exclusion term seeks to limit responsibility; its existence, scope and enforceability are separate questions.</li><li>Separate incorporation from construction.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incorporation</td><td>Whether a term entered the agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Construction</td><td>Determination of a term’s meaning in context</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion clause</td><td>Term seeking to exclude or limit specified responsibility</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Incorporation</summary><p>Whether a term entered the agreement</p></details><details><summary>Construction</summary><p>Determination of a term’s meaning in context</p></details><details><summary>Exclusion clause</summary><p>Term seeking to exclude or limit specified responsibility</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A limitation appears only on a later invoice. What is the first issue?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether all limitations are criminal</li><li>Whether the invoice automatically rewrites the contract</li><li>Whether it was incorporated into the agreement</li><li>Whether the font is modern</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Timing and assent matter to incorporation.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate meaning from enforceability?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Clear wording defeats every statute</li><li>Only unclear clauses can be reviewed</li><li>Enforceability determines the paper size</li><li>A clearly worded clause may still face applicable legal controls</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Interpretation and legal validity are distinct inquiries.</p></details><p><strong>What should a risk-allocation opinion do with competing clause interpretations?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Compare both against the text, transaction and governing authority</li><li>Choose the supplier automatically</li><li>Ignore the contract schedules</li><li>Replace local law with a video summary</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A balanced opinion tests the strongest readings.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Incorporation and Construction.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of incorporation and construction. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned law of contracts research task: Write a 1,100-word contract-risk opinion and redraft the exclusion term with precise scope, assumptions and legal limits. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,100-word contract-risk opinion and redraft the exclusion term with precise scope, assumptions and legal limits.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M5:Misrepresentation, Duress and Transactional Vulnerability": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 5</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: MISREPRESENTATION, DURESS AND TRANSACTIONAL VULNERABILITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law of Contracts</td><td>Misrepresentation, Duress and Transactional Vulnerability</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain misrepresentation, duress and transactional vulnerability using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A vitiating factor is a legally recognised defect affecting an agreement or its consequences. Misrepresentation concerns a relevant false representation; duress concerns legally improper pressure. A poor bargain alone is not necessarily either.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Representation</td><td>Statement considered for its effect on another party’s decision</td></tr><tr><td>Reliance</td><td>Decision-making connection to the alleged representation</td></tr><tr><td>Duress</td><td>Pressure meeting the governing legal threshold for an improper constraint</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Construct a pre-contract chronology and identify the precise statement or pressure alleged. Separate fact, opinion, promise and silence, and investigate the local rule applicable to each. Analyse reliance and causation rather than assuming any inaccurate statement determined the decision. Preserve the distinction between a representation and a contractual term.</p><p>For pressure, identify the threatened act, available alternatives, timing and connection to assent. Do not treat ordinary hard negotiation as automatically unlawful, or assume economic need excuses every abusive demand. Investigate the governing doctrine and remedy, including any relevant restrictions on undoing the transaction. An advanced answer considers affirmation, third-party effects and the feasibility of restoring exchanged benefits where the local law makes them relevant.</p><p>Analyse remedial election through the transaction’s chronology. A party may seek to undo an agreement, enforce a promise or recover a legally recognised loss, but those positions can interact. Identify the factual and legal conditions of each route and any effect of later conduct. Do not assume a party may both retain every benefit and recover every possible remedy; justify the proposed combination under the applicable framework.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a distributor claims exclusive rights that have expired, then threatens to withhold goods unless a new agreement is signed immediately. The buyer had already paid a deposit. Evaluate the false statement and pressure separately. Identify missing licensing records, the delivery obligation and any realistic alternative supply. The learner should not label every commercial threat “duress” without a sourced test.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use applicable Liberian commercial legislation and published contract decisions. Identify the transaction type and verify the governing rule for interpretation, vitiating factors and remedies.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Misrepresentation, Duress and Transactional Vulnerability”, start by separating representation from reliance. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A vitiating factor is a legally recognised defect affecting an agreement or its consequences. Misrepresentation concerns a relevant false representation; duress concerns legally improper pressure. A poor bargain alone is not necessarily either.</li><li>Separate representation from reliance.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Representation</td><td>Statement considered for its effect on another party’s decision</td></tr><tr><td>Reliance</td><td>Decision-making connection to the alleged representation</td></tr><tr><td>Duress</td><td>Pressure meeting the governing legal threshold for an improper constraint</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Representation</summary><p>Statement considered for its effect on another party’s decision</p></details><details><summary>Reliance</summary><p>Decision-making connection to the alleged representation</p></details><details><summary>Duress</summary><p>Pressure meeting the governing legal threshold for an improper constraint</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A licence statement was false but unknown to the buyer when contracting. What issue arises?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic reliance in every case</li><li>Proof that all terms are void</li><li>The abolition of contract law</li><li>Whether the statement actually influenced the relevant decision</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The alleged representation must connect to the decision under the applicable rule.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish hard bargaining from legally actionable pressure?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The doctrine requires more than dissatisfaction with the bargain</li><li>Every urgent demand is duress</li><li>Need always makes contracts invalid</li><li>Commercial threats can never matter</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The legal threshold and context must be established.</p></details><p><strong>A transaction is to be unwound after goods were resold. What must be examined?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the paper contract was folded</li><li>The available remedy, restoration and relevant third-party effects</li><li>Only the original price</li><li>An assumed automatic reversal of all later transactions</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Remedies can raise issues distinct from establishing the defect.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Representation and Reliance.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of representation and reliance. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned law of contracts research task: Prepare a 1,100-word avoidance-and-remedies memorandum with a reliance timeline, pressure analysis and alternatives to unwinding the agreement. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a 1,100-word avoidance-and-remedies memorandum with a reliance timeline, pressure analysis and alternatives to unwinding the agreement.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M6:Vicarious Liability and Organisational Risk": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 6</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: VICARIOUS LIABILITY AND ORGANISATIONAL RISK</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law of Torts</td><td>Vicarious Liability and Organisational Risk</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain vicarious liability and organisational risk using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Vicarious liability attributes responsibility for another person’s wrongdoing under a recognised legal rule. It differs from an organisation’s own negligent selection, supervision or system design.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary wrong</td><td>The underlying civil wrong alleged against the actor</td></tr><tr><td>Vicarious liability</td><td>Responsibility attributed for another’s wrong under an applicable rule</td></tr><tr><td>Direct liability</td><td>Responsibility based on the defendant’s own wrongful conduct</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the primary wrong before considering attribution. Establish who did what, the legal relationship and how the activity connects to the work entrusted. A uniform or company vehicle may be relevant evidence but does not itself resolve the governing relationship or scope test. Examine contracts and actual working arrangements.</p><p>Keep personal liability, organisational direct fault and attributed liability in separate columns. A claimant may advance more than one route, but each needs its own authority and elements. Consider policy arguments about enterprise risk critically: they can explain a doctrine without proving that Liberia recognises a particular foreign formulation. Analyse available defences and avoid counting the same loss twice when comparing potential defendants.</p><p>Compare the incentives behind direct and attributed responsibility without treating policy as a substitute for law. A safer dispatch system concerns the enterprise’s own conduct; attribution concerns when another person’s wrong is legally connected to it. The same evidence may be relevant to both, but the reasoning differs. Explain what would change if the rider’s detour were authorised, incidental to delivery or wholly unrelated to the entrusted activity.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a delivery rider injures a pedestrian while making a private detour in a company-branded vehicle. The dispatch records, working arrangement and reason for the journey matter. A weak answer makes branding conclusive. A stronger one examines the applicable attribution rule and separately asks whether the company itself created an unsafe dispatch system.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate Liberian civil-liability decisions and applicable legislation. Treat foreign tort tests as comparative; verify the local basis for attribution, defences and damages.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Vicarious Liability and Organisational Risk”, start by separating primary wrong from vicarious liability. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Vicarious liability attributes responsibility for another person’s wrongdoing under a recognised legal rule. It differs from an organisation’s own negligent selection, supervision or system design.</li><li>Separate primary wrong from vicarious liability.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary wrong</td><td>The underlying civil wrong alleged against the actor</td></tr><tr><td>Vicarious liability</td><td>Responsibility attributed for another’s wrong under an applicable rule</td></tr><tr><td>Direct liability</td><td>Responsibility based on the defendant’s own wrongful conduct</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Primary wrong</summary><p>The underlying civil wrong alleged against the actor</p></details><details><summary>Vicarious liability</summary><p>Responsibility attributed for another’s wrong under an applicable rule</p></details><details><summary>Direct liability</summary><p>Responsibility based on the defendant’s own wrongful conduct</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why establish the rider’s underlying wrong first?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Branding alone creates every tort</li><li>Employers are automatically criminally guilty</li><li>A company cannot face any direct claim</li><li>Attribution does not replace analysis of the primary civil wrong</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The routes of responsibility must be established separately.</p></details><p><strong>The rider wears company clothing. What is the sound inference?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It is relevant context but does not conclusively resolve the legal relationship</li><li>It proves all scope requirements</li><li>It makes the pedestrian liable</li><li>It eliminates the need for records</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Actual arrangements and the governing test remain important.</p></details><p><strong>Which claim concerns direct organisational fault?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The company’s logo colour</li><li>An unsafe dispatch policy attributable to the company itself</li><li>A claim solely based on another actor’s status</li><li>The rider’s surname</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Direct fault concerns the organisation’s own conduct.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Primary wrong and Vicarious liability.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of primary wrong and vicarious liability. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned law of torts research task: Produce a 1,000-word liability map distinguishing the rider’s conduct, organisational fault, attribution and evidential gaps. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Produce a 1,000-word liability map distinguishing the rider’s conduct, organisational fault, attribution and evidential gaps.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M6:Defamation, Reputation and Proof of Civil Loss": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 6</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DEFAMATION, REPUTATION AND PROOF OF CIVIL LOSS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law of Torts</td><td>Defamation, Reputation and Proof of Civil Loss</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain defamation, reputation and proof of civil loss using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Defamation concerns legally actionable injury to reputation through communication. Criticism, factual allegation, opinion, publication, applicable defences and compensable loss must be distinguished under the governing law.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Publication</td><td>Communication to the relevant audience under the applicable rule</td></tr><tr><td>Opinion</td><td>Evaluative statement whose treatment depends on context and law</td></tr><tr><td>Causal loss</td><td>Loss shown to result from the legally relevant wrong</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Preserve the exact words and the context in which an audience received them. Identify the person allegedly referred to, the communication and its factual or evaluative character. An edited screenshot may omit qualifications or surrounding exchanges. A learner should not treat every offensive remark as a proved defamatory statement.</p><p>Research the local elements and defences before assigning burdens. Evaluate the difference between truth of an allegation, an honestly expressed opinion and a legally protected occasion where relevant. Analyse evidence of reputational and financial effects without inventing damages. Compare correction, apology, removal and monetary relief only through their available legal foundations. An advanced opinion addresses expression interests and the strongest adverse argument rather than silencing all criticism.</p><p>Construct a meaning-and-loss map. First state the meaning reasonably conveyed in the complete context, then identify the legal issue it raises and finally trace the asserted injury. A statement can be reputationally serious while a particular lost contract remains causally unexplained. Conversely, absence of a proved financial loss does not by itself settle every possible cause of action or remedy; the local framework must determine the consequences.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a public post says a named contractor “stole the school’s money,” while attached accounts show a disputed advance payment. The contractor loses a bid but the reason is unclear. Preserve the complete post and accounts, separate allegation from proven finding, and investigate audience understanding, possible defences and the causal link to loss.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate Liberian civil-liability decisions and applicable legislation. Treat foreign tort tests as comparative; verify the local basis for attribution, defences and damages.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Defamation, Reputation and Proof of Civil Loss”, start by separating publication from opinion. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Defamation concerns legally actionable injury to reputation through communication. Criticism, factual allegation, opinion, publication, applicable defences and compensable loss must be distinguished under the governing law.</li><li>Separate publication from opinion.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Publication</td><td>Communication to the relevant audience under the applicable rule</td></tr><tr><td>Opinion</td><td>Evaluative statement whose treatment depends on context and law</td></tr><tr><td>Causal loss</td><td>Loss shown to result from the legally relevant wrong</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Publication</summary><p>Communication to the relevant audience under the applicable rule</p></details><details><summary>Opinion</summary><p>Evaluative statement whose treatment depends on context and law</p></details><details><summary>Causal loss</summary><p>Loss shown to result from the legally relevant wrong</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why preserve the complete post rather than one sentence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Meaning and possible defences can depend on context</li><li>A screenshot always proves every allegation</li><li>Context never affects communication</li><li>Editing makes evidence more reliable</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The audience’s understanding must be assessed on the actual record.</p></details><p><strong>A contractor loses a bid after a post. What remains to be investigated?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An assumed criminal conviction</li><li>Whether the publication caused the loss and what other factors mattered</li><li>Automatic proof of every claimed amount</li><li>Whether every bidder read the post</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Sequence alone does not establish the causal explanation.</p></details><p><strong>Which distinction is essential before declaring a criticism defamatory?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the platform is fashionable</li><li>Whether the claimant dislikes criticism</li><li>Factual allegation, evaluative opinion and applicable legal protection</li><li>The writer’s preferred font</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Different forms and contexts of communication can receive different legal treatment.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Publication and Opinion.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of publication and opinion. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned law of torts research task: Write a 1,000-word reputational-risk opinion and evidence schedule, including two lawful alternatives to an overbroad speech restriction. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,000-word reputational-risk opinion and evidence schedule, including two lawful alternatives to an overbroad speech restriction.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M7:Title, Conveyances and the Limits of Documentary Proof": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 7</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TITLE, CONVEYANCES AND THE LIMITS OF DOCUMENTARY PROOF</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Property Law</td><td>Title, Conveyances and the Limits of Documentary Proof</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain title, conveyances and the limits of documentary proof using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A conveyance is a transaction or instrument transferring an identified legal interest. Documentary title evidence must be distinguished from possession, physical boundaries and the transferor’s actual authority.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conveyance</td><td>Transfer of a specified interest through a legally recognised process</td></tr><tr><td>Chain of title</td><td>Sequence of asserted interests and transfers</td></tr><tr><td>Possession</td><td>Factual control, distinct from proof of legal entitlement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with a chain-of-interest diagram. Identify each claimed transfer, the parties’ capacity, the described parcel or asset and relevant formalities. A deed may be genuine as a document yet ineffective to convey an interest the grantor did not possess. Conversely, an incomplete file is not proof that no interest exists.</p><p>Test the deed against underlying records and competing claims. Separate authenticity, execution, registration, priority and substantive entitlement. Each asks a different question and may require different evidence. Advanced due diligence states what the documents establish, what remains unverified and how the uncertainty affects the proposed transaction. Avoid guaranteeing good title from a single stamped copy; explain the appropriate local searches and professional review.</p><p>A title report should identify the difference between a factual gap and a legal defect. A missing copy may be recoverable through a lawful search, while a transfer by a person without the relevant interest raises a substantive problem. Classify the risk and explain the next inquiry capable of resolving it. Avoid treating every uncertainty as either harmless or fatal before examining the complete chain and governing law.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a buyer receives a scanned deed naming a seller whose claimed authority comes from a deceased relative. The boundaries overlap a neighbour’s survey. The scan, succession authority and parcel identity require separate investigation. Registration or a signature should not be treated as an answer to every underlying entitlement issue.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Land Rights Act with applicable conveyancing legislation and published property decisions. Verify the legal nature of the interest, transfer authority and any formal requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Title, Conveyances and the Limits of Documentary Proof”, start by separating conveyance from chain of title. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A conveyance is a transaction or instrument transferring an identified legal interest. Documentary title evidence must be distinguished from possession, physical boundaries and the transferor’s actual authority.</li><li>Separate conveyance from chain of title.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conveyance</td><td>Transfer of a specified interest through a legally recognised process</td></tr><tr><td>Chain of title</td><td>Sequence of asserted interests and transfers</td></tr><tr><td>Possession</td><td>Factual control, distinct from proof of legal entitlement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Conveyance</summary><p>Transfer of a specified interest through a legally recognised process</p></details><details><summary>Chain of title</summary><p>Sequence of asserted interests and transfers</p></details><details><summary>Possession</summary><p>Factual control, distinct from proof of legal entitlement</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A deed is authentic but the seller lacks the claimed interest. What follows analytically?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Authenticity does not by itself establish authority to convey that interest</li><li>Every authentic paper guarantees title</li><li>A scan creates ownership automatically</li><li>Possession and title become identical</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Document integrity and substantive entitlement are different issues.</p></details><p><strong>Why compare the deed description with survey and neighbouring records?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Descriptions have no legal role</li><li>Parcel identity and overlap may affect what is claimed to be transferred</li><li>All surveys conclusively settle title</li><li>Neighbours determine every rule informally</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> A transfer must relate to an identifiable interest and property.</p></details><p><strong>Which conclusion is appropriately cautious after limited due diligence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Declare every missing paper a forgery</li><li>Ignore succession questions</li><li>State verified findings, outstanding searches and the effect of unresolved risks</li><li>Guarantee good title from one stamp</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> A recommendation should reflect the actual evidential limits.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Conveyance and Chain of title.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of conveyance and chain of title. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned property law research task: Create a property due-diligence report with a chain diagram, document-status matrix and conditional recommendation on the fictional sale. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a property due-diligence report with a chain diagram, document-status matrix and conditional recommendation on the fictional sale.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M7:Easements, Licences and Co-Ownership Disputes": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 7</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: EASEMENTS, LICENCES AND CO-OWNERSHIP DISPUTES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Property Law</td><td>Easements, Licences and Co-Ownership Disputes</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain easements, licences and co-ownership disputes using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A property interest may bind others in ways a personal permission does not. An easement, licence and co-ownership arrangement must be classified under applicable law rather than inferred merely from longstanding use.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Easement</td><td>A legally recognised right over another’s land where the local law provides it</td></tr><tr><td>Licence</td><td>Permission whose scope and legal effect depend on its basis</td></tr><tr><td>Co-ownership</td><td>Concurrent interests requiring analysis of each holder’s rights and authority</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the claimed right, benefiting party, burdened property and legal basis. Permission to cross land for one event is not automatically a permanent transferable interest. Investigate the creating instrument, duration, conditions and the local rules governing creation or recognition. Comparative categories are research tools, not a substitute for Liberian authority.</p><p>For shared ownership, separate management authority from each person’s underlying interest. Ask who may consent to a lease, transfer, improvement or settlement. A majority vote may be relevant but does not silently dispose of every minority entitlement. Develop options that address access and use while respecting the verified rights. A strong opinion explains how a change in classification would change remedies and affected parties.</p><p>Evaluate the proposed access arrangement against future changes. Would the claimed right continue if the benefiting property were sold, the use intensified or the permission were withdrawn? Those questions expose the practical significance of classification. Do not answer them from a label alone. Identify the creating instrument and local recognition requirements, then explain which terms or evidence would support a limited settlement without overstating permanent rights.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a family allows a neighbour to cross a path, then one family member signs a permanent access agreement after a boundary dispute. Others deny consent. Analyse the earlier permission, the new instrument and the signer’s authority separately. Do not turn repeated courtesy into a guaranteed permanent right or assume one relative can bind every owner.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Land Rights Act with applicable conveyancing legislation and published property decisions. Verify the legal nature of the interest, transfer authority and any formal requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Easements, Licences and Co-Ownership Disputes”, start by separating easement from licence. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A property interest may bind others in ways a personal permission does not. An easement, licence and co-ownership arrangement must be classified under applicable law rather than inferred merely from longstanding use.</li><li>Separate easement from licence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Easement</td><td>A legally recognised right over another’s land where the local law provides it</td></tr><tr><td>Licence</td><td>Permission whose scope and legal effect depend on its basis</td></tr><tr><td>Co-ownership</td><td>Concurrent interests requiring analysis of each holder’s rights and authority</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Easement</summary><p>A legally recognised right over another’s land where the local law provides it</p></details><details><summary>Licence</summary><p>Permission whose scope and legal effect depend on its basis</p></details><details><summary>Co-ownership</summary><p>Concurrent interests requiring analysis of each holder’s rights and authority</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A neighbour used a path with permission. What must be researched before claiming an easement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only the neighbour’s confidence</li><li>The local requirements for creation or recognition of the asserted right</li><li>Whether the path is attractive</li><li>Whether every repeated use creates ownership</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Use, permission and a binding property interest are not identical.</p></details><p><strong>One co-owner signs a permanent access agreement. What is a central issue?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the signature uses blue ink</li><li>Whether the neighbour is popular</li><li>The scope of that person’s authority to affect the shared interests</li><li>Automatic power to bind everyone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Shared interests require a legally grounded authority analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Why draft settlement terms preserving disputed title?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Settlement always eliminates every legal interest</li><li>Access and title are the same issue</li><li>No agreement can address use</li><li>Practical access can be arranged without falsely conceding unresolved ownership</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> A carefully limited agreement can separate use from contested entitlement.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Easement and Licence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of easement and licence. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned property law research task: Write a 1,000-word access-rights opinion with a classification table and two settlement terms that preserve unresolved ownership claims. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,000-word access-rights opinion with a classification table and two settlement terms that preserve unresolved ownership claims.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M8:Land Categories, Customary Rights and Competing Claims": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 8</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LAND CATEGORIES, CUSTOMARY RIGHTS AND COMPETING CLAIMS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land Law</td><td>Land Categories, Customary Rights and Competing Claims</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain land categories, customary rights and competing claims using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Land classification concerns the legal category governing a parcel. Recognition of customary land rights is distinct from boundary proof, internal governance and authority to transact on behalf of a community.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land category</td><td>Statutory classification affecting applicable rights and powers</td></tr><tr><td>Customary interest</td><td>Interest requiring analysis under the governing customary-land framework</td></tr><tr><td>Representation</td><td>Authority to act for the relevant rights holders</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the claimed category using the Act rather than a buyer’s label. Record the community, claimed boundaries, existing uses, documentary history and overlapping interests. A community may have legally significant interests even where the file does not resemble an individual urban deed. Do not assume absence of a familiar document means absence of rights.</p><p>Separate recognition from representation. Evidence of community occupation does not establish that a particular spokesperson may sell land alone. Investigate decision-making requirements and participation of affected groups under applicable law. An advanced analysis treats competing claims fairly, distinguishes facts from legal classification and identifies the process for resolving uncertainty. Development benefits do not automatically cure defective authority or participation.</p><p>A community-land analysis should not collapse collective rights into the personal property of a spokesperson. Identify how the community is constituted for the relevant legal purpose, how decisions are authorised and what interests require separate attention. Compare the development proposal with existing use, including seasonal and shared access. State which claims are supported, which are contested and how the governing process addresses that contestation.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: an investor describes a rural area as vacant because no individual seller produces a deed. Residents use it seasonally and identify community boundaries. A local leader offers to sign a sale. The learner must investigate classification, users’ interests and lawful collective authority, rather than accepting either the investor’s vacancy claim or one person’s signature as decisive.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Land Rights Act 2018 and current Liberia Land Authority materials. Distinguish land categories, community governance, surveying and the legal authority for a transaction.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Land Categories, Customary Rights and Competing Claims”, start by separating land category from customary interest. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Land classification concerns the legal category governing a parcel. Recognition of customary land rights is distinct from boundary proof, internal governance and authority to transact on behalf of a community.</li><li>Separate land category from customary interest.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land category</td><td>Statutory classification affecting applicable rights and powers</td></tr><tr><td>Customary interest</td><td>Interest requiring analysis under the governing customary-land framework</td></tr><tr><td>Representation</td><td>Authority to act for the relevant rights holders</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Land category</summary><p>Statutory classification affecting applicable rights and powers</p></details><details><summary>Customary interest</summary><p>Interest requiring analysis under the governing customary-land framework</p></details><details><summary>Representation</summary><p>Authority to act for the relevant rights holders</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>No individual deed is produced for seasonally used community land. What is the sound response?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Treat seasonal use as legally impossible</li><li>Investigate the statutory category and community interests</li><li>Declare the land ownerless immediately</li><li>Assume the investor owns it</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> The applicable framework may recognise interests beyond an individual deed.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish community recognition from a leader’s power to sell?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Recognition abolishes participation</li><li>A signature resolves all boundaries</li><li>Existence of rights does not establish one person’s transaction authority</li><li>Every leader owns all community land personally</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Representation and substantive rights require separate analysis.</p></details><p><strong>An investor promises jobs. What legal issue remains?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Benefits automatically transfer title</li><li>Jobs replace every statutory requirement</li><li>Only the publicity plan matters</li><li>Whether the transaction has lawful authority and required participation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Development value does not itself establish transaction validity.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Land category and Customary interest.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of land category and customary interest. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned land law research task: Prepare a community-land issues register and 1,000-word balanced opinion identifying classification evidence, participation requirements and unresolved claims. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a community-land issues register and 1,000-word balanced opinion identifying classification evidence, participation requirements and unresolved claims.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M8:Surveys, Community Consent and Transaction Sequencing": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 8</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: SURVEYS, COMMUNITY CONSENT AND TRANSACTION SEQUENCING</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land Law</td><td>Surveys, Community Consent and Transaction Sequencing</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain surveys, community consent and transaction sequencing using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A survey addresses physical identification and measurement. Consent addresses authorised agreement. Neither automatically proves the other, and neither alone establishes the complete legal validity of a land transaction.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Technical identification and measurement of land</td></tr><tr><td>Informed participation</td><td>Meaningful involvement based on the actual proposed decision</td></tr><tr><td>Condition precedent</td><td>Event required before an agreed step or obligation takes effect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Plan due diligence in stages: identify the category and interested persons, obtain records, clarify boundaries, verify representation and examine the proposed instrument. Record objections rather than editing them out of the file. A technically accurate map can still depict land the proposed seller lacks authority to transfer.</p><p>Treat consultation as a process with records, not a photograph of a meeting. Ask who received information, in what language, who participated, what was proposed and what decision was actually authorised under the applicable framework. Distinguish preliminary discussion from final transaction approval. A defensible recommendation states conditions precedent and stop points: unresolved overlap or authority can justify further investigation before funds or signatures are committed.</p><p>Audit the transaction by version. For each material document, record the date, parcel description, proposed rights and approving body or persons. A survey, consultation record and final lease can refer to different proposals even when they share a project name. A defensible completion recommendation reconciles those versions and identifies any renewed approval or factual inquiry required by the applicable framework before the actual terms are implemented.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a developer obtains a survey and photographs a meeting attended by some residents. A later agreement adds a larger parcel and excludes a water-access path. The photographed meeting does not prove consent to the revised terms. Compare versions, participation records and the survey, then identify who can authorise the actual transaction.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Land Rights Act 2018 and current Liberia Land Authority materials. Distinguish land categories, community governance, surveying and the legal authority for a transaction.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Surveys, Community Consent and Transaction Sequencing”, start by separating survey from informed participation. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A survey addresses physical identification and measurement. Consent addresses authorised agreement. Neither automatically proves the other, and neither alone establishes the complete legal validity of a land transaction.</li><li>Separate survey from informed participation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Technical identification and measurement of land</td></tr><tr><td>Informed participation</td><td>Meaningful involvement based on the actual proposed decision</td></tr><tr><td>Condition precedent</td><td>Event required before an agreed step or obligation takes effect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Survey</summary><p>Technical identification and measurement of land</p></details><details><summary>Informed participation</summary><p>Meaningful involvement based on the actual proposed decision</p></details><details><summary>Condition precedent</summary><p>Event required before an agreed step or obligation takes effect</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A survey is accurate. What does it not by itself establish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The surveyor’s stated observations</li><li>The map’s scale</li><li>The proposed seller’s legal authority or required consent</li><li>The parcel’s measured dimensions</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Technical identification and transaction authority are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>A revised agreement enlarges the parcel after consultation. What must be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether earlier photographs automatically approve every change</li><li>Only the filename</li><li>Whether the developer prefers the revision</li><li>Whether the actual revised transaction received the required authority and participation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Consent must relate to the decision and terms actually proposed.</p></details><p><strong>Why keep an objections register?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It preserves unresolved issues that may affect rights, boundaries or consent</li><li>It automatically proves every objection correct</li><li>It replaces the governing Act</li><li>It permits deletion of contrary records</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A transparent file distinguishes allegations from resolved findings.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Survey and Informed participation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of survey and informed participation. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned land law research task: Draft a staged land-transaction checklist, version comparison and 900-word recommendation stating conditions that must be resolved before completion. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a staged land-transaction checklist, version comparison and 900-word recommendation stating conditions that must be resolved before completion.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M8:Private Rights and Community Land Clinic": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 8 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Private Rights and Community Land Clinic</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional cooperative purchases equipment and proposes a lease over disputed community land. A damaged shipment and overlapping survey create different claims.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A contract-risk and tort-liability matrix distinguishing parties, conduct and remedies</li><li>A land-category, boundary and representation report with a version comparison and participation questions</li><li>A 1,400-word balanced opinion and a plain-language 400-word explainer preserving unresolved rights</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M9:Administrative Discretion, Reasons and Lawful Decision-Making": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 9</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ADMINISTRATIVE DISCRETION, REASONS AND LAWFUL DECISION-MAKING</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Administrative Law</td><td>Administrative Discretion, Reasons and Lawful Decision-Making</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain administrative discretion, reasons and lawful decision-making using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Discretion is a legally conferred choice within defined limits. A reasoned decision identifies the legal power, relevant facts and explanation connecting them to the outcome; it is not simply an announcement of preference.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Discretion</td><td>Lawfully conferred choice subject to legal limits</td></tr><tr><td>Fettering</td><td>Improperly treating a policy as eliminating a discretion where the law requires it</td></tr><tr><td>Reasons</td><td>Explanation connecting material findings, authority and outcome</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the decision-maker and enabling provision. Ask which considerations the law requires or permits and whether the person exercising the power is authorised. Distinguish disagreement with the result from an error about power, process or relevant considerations. A policy can guide consistent decisions without lawfully replacing a statutory discretion in every case.</p><p>Analyse reasons as an account of the route to the decision. They should expose the material findings and legal basis, allowing affected persons and a reviewing body to understand the outcome. The content and enforceability of any duty to give reasons must be locally sourced. An advanced answer also addresses administrative capacity, consistency and the danger of converting review into an unsupported merits appeal.</p><p>Separate three explanations for an adverse outcome: a lawful choice within discretion, a factual misunderstanding and use of an irrelevant or unauthorised criterion. They may require different records and legal responses. An advanced review memorandum should identify which explanation the available material supports and what the decision-maker’s reasons leave unresolved. Do not convert an evidential gap into a finding of bad faith without a proper basis.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: an agency rejects every licence application from one district under an unpublished policy, despite an Act requiring assessment of specified criteria. A learner must test the policy’s authority, the relevant criteria and the process used. The fact that uniform refusals are efficient does not answer whether the statute permits them.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read constitutional protections with the enabling Act for the chosen public body. For the procurement case, use current PPCC legal documents, including the instruments listed in 2026.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.ppcc.gov.lr/publications/document-type/legal-documents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Current procurement legal documents, including 2026 instruments — PPCC Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Administrative Discretion, Reasons and Lawful Decision-Making”, start by separating discretion from fettering. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Discretion is a legally conferred choice within defined limits. A reasoned decision identifies the legal power, relevant facts and explanation connecting them to the outcome; it is not simply an announcement of preference.</li><li>Separate discretion from fettering.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Discretion</td><td>Lawfully conferred choice subject to legal limits</td></tr><tr><td>Fettering</td><td>Improperly treating a policy as eliminating a discretion where the law requires it</td></tr><tr><td>Reasons</td><td>Explanation connecting material findings, authority and outcome</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Discretion</summary><p>Lawfully conferred choice subject to legal limits</p></details><details><summary>Fettering</summary><p>Improperly treating a policy as eliminating a discretion where the law requires it</p></details><details><summary>Reasons</summary><p>Explanation connecting material findings, authority and outcome</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>An agency uses a blanket unpublished policy. Which question matters?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the agency has many staff</li><li>Whether applicants dislike waiting</li><li>Whether the enabling law permits that policy and requires individual assessment</li><li>Whether uniform refusals are easy</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Administrative efficiency does not establish statutory authority.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate legality review from merits disagreement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Reviewers may rewrite every policy without authority</li><li>Reasons are never relevant</li><li>Every refusal proves corruption</li><li>An undesirable result is not automatically a legal defect</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Review must identify an applicable legal ground.</p></details><p><strong>What should reasons reveal?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The route from material findings and legal power to the decision</li><li>Only the official’s job title</li><li>A slogan about efficiency</li><li>The applicant’s political opinions alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Reasons make the basis of the outcome intelligible.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Discretion and Fettering.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of discretion and fettering. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned administrative law research task: Write a 1,000-word administrative legality opinion with a decision-tree diagram and a draft request for the relevant record. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,000-word administrative legality opinion with a decision-tree diagram and a draft request for the relevant record.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M9:Public Procurement, Concessions and Accountability Review": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 9</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PUBLIC PROCUREMENT, CONCESSIONS AND ACCOUNTABILITY REVIEW</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Administrative Law</td><td>Public Procurement, Concessions and Accountability Review</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain public procurement, concessions and accountability review using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Procurement regulates public purchasing; a concession can involve a distinct grant or arrangement governed by its own applicable framework. Evaluation, award, contract performance and review are different stages.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Evaluation criteria</td><td>Published measures used to assess offers under the governing procedure</td></tr><tr><td>Conflict of interest</td><td>An interest requiring assessment against the applicable impartiality rules</td></tr><tr><td>Review route</td><td>Legally competent process for challenging the relevant procurement act</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the current instrument, procuring entity, procedure and published evaluation criteria. Build a document chronology from invitation through evaluation and decision. A learner should not use an old procurement summary without checking the PPCC’s current legal materials and commencement or transition provisions.</p><p>Separate procurement legality from whether the selected product seems attractive. Undisclosed criteria, conflicts and unequal access to information raise different issues requiring their own evidence. A complaint must use the competent route and verified timing. Discuss remedies in relation to stage and public interests: preserving a process, reconsidering an evaluation and resolving contract-performance disputes are not interchangeable. Explain the limits of the available file rather than declaring an award invalid from a rumour.</p><p>Analyse procurement accountability at the point of the alleged defect. An evaluation error may call for a different response from a delivery failure after a lawful award. Ask what can still be corrected, whose interests are affected and what the competent review body is authorised to do. A broad demand to cancel everything should be justified against narrower options and the governing procedure, not assumed to follow from any irregularity.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a school tender publishes price and technical criteria, then evaluators add an undisclosed local-office requirement. One evaluator has a family connection to a bidder. Analyse the criterion change and conflict separately, verify the applicable current procedure and identify documents necessary for a competent review.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read constitutional protections with the enabling Act for the chosen public body. For the procurement case, use current PPCC legal documents, including the instruments listed in 2026.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.ppcc.gov.lr/publications/document-type/legal-documents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Current procurement legal documents, including 2026 instruments — PPCC Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Public Procurement, Concessions and Accountability Review”, start by separating evaluation criteria from conflict of interest. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Procurement regulates public purchasing; a concession can involve a distinct grant or arrangement governed by its own applicable framework. Evaluation, award, contract performance and review are different stages.</li><li>Separate evaluation criteria from conflict of interest.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Evaluation criteria</td><td>Published measures used to assess offers under the governing procedure</td></tr><tr><td>Conflict of interest</td><td>An interest requiring assessment against the applicable impartiality rules</td></tr><tr><td>Review route</td><td>Legally competent process for challenging the relevant procurement act</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Evaluation criteria</summary><p>Published measures used to assess offers under the governing procedure</p></details><details><summary>Conflict of interest</summary><p>An interest requiring assessment against the applicable impartiality rules</p></details><details><summary>Review route</summary><p>Legally competent process for challenging the relevant procurement act</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Evaluators add a criterion after bids arrive. What should be examined?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the criterion sounds useful alone</li><li>Whether all bidders own identical offices</li><li>Only the final contract price</li><li>Consistency with the published process and current governing instruments</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Procurement analysis depends on the authorised procedure and fair application.</p></details><p><strong>Why check commencement and transition provisions in new procurement instruments?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The applicable rules can depend on when and how the process began</li><li>New titles automatically apply retroactively to every event</li><li>Older transactions have no rules</li><li>A website listing alone resolves every transitional issue</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Temporal application must be verified.</p></details><p><strong>An evaluator has a bidder connection. What is a sound response?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Publish unverified personal accusations</li><li>Investigate the relationship, disclosure and applicable conflict safeguards</li><li>Declare every connected bid automatically criminal</li><li>Ignore it because price is low</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> The conflict issue needs evidence and the governing standard.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Evaluation criteria and Conflict of interest.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of evaluation criteria and conflict of interest. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned administrative law research task: Prepare a procurement review file with a chronology, criteria comparison, conflict inquiry and 1,100-word stage-specific remedies opinion. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a procurement review file with a chronology, criteria comparison, conflict inquiry and 1,100-word stage-specific remedies opinion.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M10:Sale of Goods, Delivery Risk and Commercial Remedies": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 10</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: SALE OF GOODS, DELIVERY RISK AND COMMERCIAL REMEDIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Commercial Law</td><td>Sale of Goods, Delivery Risk and Commercial Remedies</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain sale of goods, delivery risk and commercial remedies using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A sale-of-goods dispute can involve conformity, delivery, risk allocation, acceptance and remedies. Physical possession, ownership and responsibility for transit loss need not change at the same moment.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conformity</td><td>Whether goods meet legally relevant agreed or required standards</td></tr><tr><td>Risk</td><td>Allocation of responsibility for specified loss</td></tr><tr><td>Acceptance</td><td>Legally relevant treatment of goods under the governing framework</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Map the transaction from order to delivery and inspection. Identify the goods, agreed standard, delivery point, carrier arrangement and communications about defects. A delivery signature may acknowledge receipt without resolving every conformity issue. Distinguish damage occurring in transit from goods that were already defective.</p><p>Read express terms alongside applicable mandatory and default rules. Analyse notice, examination and available relief using local authority rather than assuming a foreign rejection period. Quantify alleged loss with a transparent calculation and consider mitigation or substitute performance where legally relevant. The strongest opinion evaluates both the buyer’s remedy and the seller’s response, and explains which missing fact would change the risk allocation.</p><p>Test the commercial explanation of each contractual term. A delivery point may allocate logistical responsibility, but its effect on legal risk depends on the agreement and applicable default or mandatory rules. A conformity complaint may survive physical receipt, while later conduct may affect the available response. Compare the competing timelines and explain which event changes each party’s asserted right; avoid using one moment of delivery as the answer to every issue.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a Buchanan clinic orders refrigerators, signs a delivery receipt and later finds that some cannot maintain the specified temperature. The seller blames the carrier and says signing waived all claims. Inspect the agreed specifications, delivery terms, testing record and actual wording of the receipt. Receipt, conformity and remedial rights require separate analysis.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the applicable Liberian Commercial Code provisions on the chosen goods or payment transaction. Verify current amendments and distinguish statutory requirements from comparative video examples.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Sale of Goods, Delivery Risk and Commercial Remedies”, start by separating conformity from risk. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A sale-of-goods dispute can involve conformity, delivery, risk allocation, acceptance and remedies. Physical possession, ownership and responsibility for transit loss need not change at the same moment.</li><li>Separate conformity from risk.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conformity</td><td>Whether goods meet legally relevant agreed or required standards</td></tr><tr><td>Risk</td><td>Allocation of responsibility for specified loss</td></tr><tr><td>Acceptance</td><td>Legally relevant treatment of goods under the governing framework</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Conformity</summary><p>Whether goods meet legally relevant agreed or required standards</p></details><details><summary>Risk</summary><p>Allocation of responsibility for specified loss</p></details><details><summary>Acceptance</summary><p>Legally relevant treatment of goods under the governing framework</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A buyer signs for delivery. What should not be assumed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That goods arrived physically</li><li>That the signature is relevant evidence</li><li>That the delivery record should be preserved</li><li>That receipt alone resolves every conformity and remedy issue</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The legal effect depends on terms, law and context.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish transit damage from an original defect?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Different facts may engage different obligations and risk allocations</li><li>The distinction never matters</li><li>Carriers always bear every loss</li><li>Sellers are never responsible for quality</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The cause and timing of loss affect the legal analysis.</p></details><p><strong>What supports a defensible loss calculation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The buyer’s preferred compensation alone</li><li>A stated legal basis, actual records and transparent assumptions</li><li>An arbitrary round figure</li><li>Multiplying the price without reasons</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Remedial quantification needs legal and evidential support.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Conformity and Risk.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of conformity and risk. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned commercial law research task: Write a commercial remedies memorandum with a delivery chronology, risk matrix and documented alternative-loss calculations. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a commercial remedies memorandum with a delivery chronology, risk matrix and documented alternative-loss calculations.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M10:Negotiable Instruments and Payment-Chain Liability": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 10</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: NEGOTIABLE INSTRUMENTS AND PAYMENT-CHAIN LIABILITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Commercial Law</td><td>Negotiable Instruments and Payment-Chain Liability</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain negotiable instruments and payment-chain liability using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A negotiable instrument is a legally defined payment instrument with rules concerning issue, transfer and enforcement. The underlying debt and rights arising from the instrument must be analysed separately.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Endorsement</td><td>Signature or act relevant to transfer under the governing instrument rules</td></tr><tr><td>Underlying obligation</td><td>Debt or transaction distinct from the payment instrument</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement entitlement</td><td>Legal basis on which a particular claimant may demand payment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the instrument and applicable statutory requirements before using terms such as holder or endorsement. Map issuer, payee, transferees and any collecting or paying institution. Preserve the original where available and record signatures, alterations, dates and the chain of possession. A copy may prove some facts without resolving every enforcement requirement.</p><p>Distinguish a defence arising from the underlying transaction from a defect in the instrument or transfer. Whether a transferee can enforce despite a defence depends on the applicable local provisions and facts; do not import a foreign protected-holder rule automatically. Analyse forgery, authority and notice separately. A careful opinion identifies who claims against whom and on what legal basis rather than assuming that every participant shares the same liability.</p><p>Use one arrow for each asserted legal claim in the payment chain. Label it with the obligation, claimant, defendant and disputed requirement. This reveals why an underlying sale defence may affect one relationship differently from a signature defect affecting another. The diagram does not decide the result; it organises the local statutory inquiry and prevents an unsupported assumption that every participant can enforce or resist payment on the same basis.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a supplier receives a cheque, transfers it to another business and later disputes defective goods with the buyer. A signature is contested. The learner must chart the chain and investigate instrument validity, transfer and the asserted defence. The goods dispute and signature issue cannot be collapsed into a general claim that “the cheque is bad.”</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the applicable Liberian Commercial Code provisions on the chosen goods or payment transaction. Verify current amendments and distinguish statutory requirements from comparative video examples.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Negotiable Instruments and Payment-Chain Liability”, start by separating endorsement from underlying obligation. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A negotiable instrument is a legally defined payment instrument with rules concerning issue, transfer and enforcement. The underlying debt and rights arising from the instrument must be analysed separately.</li><li>Separate endorsement from underlying obligation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Endorsement</td><td>Signature or act relevant to transfer under the governing instrument rules</td></tr><tr><td>Underlying obligation</td><td>Debt or transaction distinct from the payment instrument</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement entitlement</td><td>Legal basis on which a particular claimant may demand payment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Endorsement</summary><p>Signature or act relevant to transfer under the governing instrument rules</p></details><details><summary>Underlying obligation</summary><p>Debt or transaction distinct from the payment instrument</p></details><details><summary>Enforcement entitlement</summary><p>Legal basis on which a particular claimant may demand payment</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why chart each transfer of a cheque?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Enforcement rights and potential liabilities depend on the relevant chain</li><li>Possession always proves every right</li><li>Every transferee is the original buyer</li><li>Transfer cancels the governing statute</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The parties’ roles and acts must be identified.</p></details><p><strong>A signature is disputed. What requires investigation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The transferee’s popularity</li><li>Authenticity, authority and their effect under the instrument rules</li><li>Only the paper’s colour</li><li>Automatic validity because money is owed</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Signature issues are distinct from the existence of an underlying debt.</p></details><p><strong>What must precede reliance on a foreign protected-holder doctrine?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Deleting the transfer history</li><li>Treating all defences as impossible</li><li>Verification of the applicable Liberian provisions and qualifying facts</li><li>Assuming every common-law country is identical</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Local law determines the instrument’s legal consequences.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Endorsement and Underlying obligation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of endorsement and underlying obligation. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned commercial law research task: Prepare a payment-chain diagram and 1,000-word liability opinion addressing the contested signature and separate underlying goods dispute. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a payment-chain diagram and 1,000-word liability opinion addressing the contested signature and separate underlying goods dispute.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M11:Directors, Conflicts and Corporate Decision Records": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 11</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DIRECTORS, CONFLICTS AND CORPORATE DECISION RECORDS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Business and Corporate Law</td><td>Directors, Conflicts and Corporate Decision Records</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain directors, conflicts and corporate decision records using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Corporate authority concerns power to act for an entity. Directors’ duties regulate the exercise of their role. Approval of a transaction and compliance with duties are related but not identical questions.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authority</td><td>Power to commit the entity to the relevant act</td></tr><tr><td>Conflict disclosure</td><td>Candid identification of an interest under the applicable duty</td></tr><tr><td>Contemporaneous record</td><td>Record accurately made at the relevant time rather than retrospectively fabricated</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the entity, governing instrument, transaction and decision-maker. Separate the company’s legal capacity from an individual officer’s delegated authority. A board minute should record the actual decision and relevant interests, not create a fictional approval after the event. Verify any statutory or constitutional approval requirements.</p><p>For a conflicted transaction, identify the interest, disclosure, participation and decision process, then examine the applicable legal consequences. Do not assume that a majority vote cures every breach or that a poor result proves disloyalty. Evaluate information available at the time and distinguish business judgment from self-dealing. A reasoned governance opinion proposes prospective controls without altering historical records.</p><p>Evaluate process and substance independently. A transaction can be commercially attractive yet involve an undisclosed interest, and a properly informed authorised decision can still produce a loss. Identify what the governing duty requires and which evidence bears on it. Proposed controls should improve future decision-making through timely disclosure, appropriate approval and accurate records, rather than sanitising the historical file or promising that procedure guarantees profitable results.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a director awards a printing contract to a business owned by a relative and later asks staff to backdate minutes. The learner must assess authority, the conflict and the integrity of the record separately. A competitive price may be relevant but cannot by itself establish that every required governance step occurred.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate current Liberian Associations Law and applicable local business requirements. Distinguish entity type, directors’ powers, duties, partnership arrangements and dissolution procedure.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Directors, Conflicts and Corporate Decision Records”, start by separating authority from conflict disclosure. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Corporate authority concerns power to act for an entity. Directors’ duties regulate the exercise of their role. Approval of a transaction and compliance with duties are related but not identical questions.</li><li>Separate authority from conflict disclosure.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authority</td><td>Power to commit the entity to the relevant act</td></tr><tr><td>Conflict disclosure</td><td>Candid identification of an interest under the applicable duty</td></tr><tr><td>Contemporaneous record</td><td>Record accurately made at the relevant time rather than retrospectively fabricated</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Authority</summary><p>Power to commit the entity to the relevant act</p></details><details><summary>Conflict disclosure</summary><p>Candid identification of an interest under the applicable duty</p></details><details><summary>Contemporaneous record</summary><p>Record accurately made at the relevant time rather than retrospectively fabricated</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A favourable price is offered by a director’s relative. What still matters?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The applicable conflict, disclosure and approval requirements</li><li>Price automatically cures every governance defect</li><li>Relatives can never transact with companies under any law</li><li>The company’s stationery alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Transaction merit and duty compliance require separate analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish entity capacity from an officer’s authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The distinction abolishes contracts</li><li>A company’s ability to transact does not prove every person can bind it</li><li>Every employee has unlimited authority</li><li>Capacity means only cash availability</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Different legal questions govern the entity and its representatives.</p></details><p><strong>Staff are asked to backdate minutes. What should an accurate file do?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Delete the request</li><li>Assume all directors attended</li><li>Preserve the actual chronology and identify the missing approval record</li><li>Invent contemporaneous approval</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Record integrity is essential to responsible governance analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Authority and Conflict disclosure.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of authority and conflict disclosure. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned business and corporate law research task: Write a governance opinion and draft a prospective conflict-disclosure form and minute template; keep the historical chronology accurate. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a governance opinion and draft a prospective conflict-disclosure form and minute template; keep the historical chronology accurate.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M11:Partnership Dissolution and Continuing Business Obligations": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 11</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PARTNERSHIP DISSOLUTION AND CONTINUING BUSINESS OBLIGATIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Business and Corporate Law</td><td>Partnership Dissolution and Continuing Business Obligations</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain partnership dissolution and continuing business obligations using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Dissolution changes the legal relationship or status of a partnership under the governing framework. Winding up addresses remaining affairs. Ending cooperation between partners does not automatically extinguish obligations owed to third parties.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dissolution</td><td>Change or ending of the partnership relationship under applicable law</td></tr><tr><td>Winding up</td><td>Dealing with remaining business affairs after the relevant ending event</td></tr><tr><td>Third-party obligation</td><td>Responsibility owed outside the partners’ internal arrangement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Collect the partnership agreement, authority arrangements, creditor records and communications about departure. Distinguish internal allocation of responsibility from an external creditor’s rights. A private agreement that one partner will pay a debt does not necessarily release others without the legally required basis.</p><p>Build a closing schedule covering assets, liabilities, records, disputed accounts and notice. Analyse whether continuing acts are authorised for winding up or instead create a new undertaking. Investigate local rules for termination, authority and third-party reliance. A sound opinion addresses both fair internal settlement and lawful protection of creditors, without inventing a universal order of distributions.</p><p>A dissolution memorandum should distinguish existing debts, obligations incurred in winding up and new trading commitments. Identify the actor’s authority and relevant third-party knowledge at each stage. The same business name can obscure changes in the relationship without proving that the external party accepted them. Recommend clear communication and lawful closing steps, while reserving any contested release or allocation question for the applicable agreement and legal rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: two partners end a training business, but one continues ordering supplies using the old name. Their internal email assigns all debts to the departing partner. The supplier has not agreed to that arrangement. Examine notice, authority and existing obligations separately from the internal settlement.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate current Liberian Associations Law and applicable local business requirements. Distinguish entity type, directors’ powers, duties, partnership arrangements and dissolution procedure.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Partnership Dissolution and Continuing Business Obligations”, start by separating dissolution from winding up. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Dissolution changes the legal relationship or status of a partnership under the governing framework. Winding up addresses remaining affairs. Ending cooperation between partners does not automatically extinguish obligations owed to third parties.</li><li>Separate dissolution from winding up.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dissolution</td><td>Change or ending of the partnership relationship under applicable law</td></tr><tr><td>Winding up</td><td>Dealing with remaining business affairs after the relevant ending event</td></tr><tr><td>Third-party obligation</td><td>Responsibility owed outside the partners’ internal arrangement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Dissolution</summary><p>Change or ending of the partnership relationship under applicable law</p></details><details><summary>Winding up</summary><p>Dealing with remaining business affairs after the relevant ending event</p></details><details><summary>Third-party obligation</summary><p>Responsibility owed outside the partners’ internal arrangement</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Partners privately allocate a debt to one person. What must be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That all debts vanish at dissolution</li><li>Whether and how that affects the creditor’s external rights</li><li>Automatic release of every other person</li><li>Whether an email has a logo</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Internal allocation and external release are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>One partner orders new goods after the split. Which issue matters?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic unlimited authority forever</li><li>No possible obligation after any departure</li><li>Continuing authority and the supplier’s relevant notice or reliance</li><li>Only the goods’ colour</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Post-dissolution acts require a legal and factual authority analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish dissolution from winding up?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They always mean all assets disappear</li><li>Winding up is unrelated to creditors</li><li>Dissolution proves debts were paid</li><li>Ending the relationship may leave affairs and obligations to resolve</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The end event and completion of remaining affairs are different stages.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Dissolution and Winding up.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of dissolution and winding up. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned business and corporate law research task: Prepare a partnership exit memorandum, creditor-notice plan and closing-account schedule, identifying unresolved authority and liability questions. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a partnership exit memorandum, creditor-notice plan and closing-account schedule, identifying unresolved authority and liability questions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M12:Treaties, Consent and Domestic Legal Effect": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 12</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TREATIES, CONSENT AND DOMESTIC LEGAL EFFECT</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>International Law</td><td>Treaties, Consent and Domestic Legal Effect</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain treaties, consent and domestic legal effect using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A treaty creates international obligations under the applicable international framework. Party status, scope and domestic implementation are separate questions; signing a document is not always equivalent to becoming bound by all of it.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ratification</td><td>International act expressing consent to be bound where the treaty framework uses it</td></tr><tr><td>Reservation</td><td>Statement purporting to exclude or modify a treaty provision’s legal effect within the applicable rules</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic implementation</td><td>Measures giving an international obligation effect within the national legal system</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the instrument, parties, relevant dates and subject matter. Check entry into force generally and for the state concerned, alongside reservations, declarations and any relevant subsequent action. A website headline that a state supports an instrument is not a substitute for the depositary record.</p><p>Separate international obligation from a remedy in a domestic court. Investigate the constitutional and legislative basis for local application and the procedural route for the claimant. Do not assume that a treaty provision automatically supplies a domestic cause of action. A rigorous opinion identifies the level at which each proposition operates and candidly states any implementation gap. Comparative enforcement examples may illuminate options without establishing Liberian practice.</p><p>Create a two-level authority table. On the international side, record consent, scope, relevant dates and reservations. On the domestic side, identify constitutional treatment, implementing legislation and the particular procedural remedy asserted. A conclusion may differ between the two columns without contradiction. Explain that difference precisely instead of treating either international commitment or domestic implementation as irrelevant merely because the other level raises an unresolved issue.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a policy brief asserts that a newly signed convention automatically overrides every local employment rule. The learner must verify status, dates, reservations and domestic effect. A state’s international commitment may be important even where a particular claimant’s domestic remedy requires further legal steps.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the UN Treaty Collection to check text, party status, reservations and dates for a selected treaty. Research the Liberian domestic basis separately; use current local sources for jurisdiction and recognition.</p><p><a href=\"https://treaties.un.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">United Nations Treaty Collection</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Treaties, Consent and Domestic Legal Effect”, start by separating ratification from reservation. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A treaty creates international obligations under the applicable international framework. Party status, scope and domestic implementation are separate questions; signing a document is not always equivalent to becoming bound by all of it.</li><li>Separate ratification from reservation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ratification</td><td>International act expressing consent to be bound where the treaty framework uses it</td></tr><tr><td>Reservation</td><td>Statement purporting to exclude or modify a treaty provision’s legal effect within the applicable rules</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic implementation</td><td>Measures giving an international obligation effect within the national legal system</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Ratification</summary><p>International act expressing consent to be bound where the treaty framework uses it</p></details><details><summary>Reservation</summary><p>Statement purporting to exclude or modify a treaty provision’s legal effect within the applicable rules</p></details><details><summary>Domestic implementation</summary><p>Measures giving an international obligation effect within the national legal system</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A state signs a convention. What must be checked before asserting its obligations?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the convention is popular</li><li>The instrument’s consent, entry-into-force and status records</li><li>Automatic application of every clause immediately</li><li>Only the ceremony photograph</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Treaty status depends on the applicable framework and records.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish international obligation from domestic cause of action?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>All international obligations are private contracts</li><li>Domestic procedure is always irrelevant</li><li>A national remedy may require a separate legal basis and route</li><li>Treaties can never matter locally</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The two levels can have different requirements.</p></details><p><strong>Which source best verifies party status and reservations for a UN-deposited treaty?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An anonymous social post</li><li>A learner’s memory</li><li>A generic foreign textbook alone</li><li>The relevant official depositary record</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The depositary records formal status and related actions.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Ratification and Reservation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of ratification and reservation. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned international law research task: Prepare a treaty-status dossier and 1,100-word memorandum separating international obligation, local implementation and the proposed remedy. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a treaty-status dossier and 1,100-word memorandum separating international obligation, local implementation and the proposed remedy.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M12:Conflict of Laws, Forum and Foreign Judgments": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 12</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONFLICT OF LAWS, FORUM AND FOREIGN JUDGMENTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>International Law</td><td>Conflict of Laws, Forum and Foreign Judgments</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain conflict of laws, forum and foreign judgments using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Conflict of laws addresses cross-border private disputes, including jurisdiction, applicable law and recognition of judgments. A governing-law clause does not necessarily select a court or guarantee enforcement.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Law selected or determined for a particular substantive issue</td></tr><tr><td>Forum</td><td>Body or court competent to hear the dispute</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition</td><td>Acceptance of a foreign judgment’s legal effect under the receiving system’s rules</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Separate three questions: which forum may hear the case, which law governs each issue and where a resulting judgment could be recognised. Contracts, property and procedure may raise different connecting factors. Identify the parties’ locations, transaction, assets and actual clause wording.</p><p>For a foreign judgment, obtain the complete authenticated decision, procedural history and evidence relevant to recognition. Investigate local requirements and defences rather than assuming a foreign seal makes enforcement automatic. Consider whether jurisdiction and notice were properly established and whether the judgment is final for the relevant purpose. A practical opinion evaluates asset location and available routes without guaranteeing recovery or inventing a reciprocal treaty.</p><p>Separate recognition from execution against an asset. Even where a judgment can receive legal effect, practical collection may require further competent steps and attention to ownership or competing interests. Identify where each proposed action would occur and which legal system governs it. A cross-border strategy should state its assumptions about the judgment’s status, notice, assets and local route, and explain what would change if one assumption fails.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a Liberian buyer and Ghanaian supplier choose a governing law but no forum. The supplier later sends a foreign judgment obtained without the buyer’s participation and seeks payment from Liberian assets. Analyse clause scope, notice, jurisdiction and the local recognition route separately.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the UN Treaty Collection to check text, party status, reservations and dates for a selected treaty. Research the Liberian domestic basis separately; use current local sources for jurisdiction and recognition.</p><p><a href=\"https://treaties.un.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">United Nations Treaty Collection</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Conflict of Laws, Forum and Foreign Judgments”, start by separating governing law from forum. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Conflict of laws addresses cross-border private disputes, including jurisdiction, applicable law and recognition of judgments. A governing-law clause does not necessarily select a court or guarantee enforcement.</li><li>Separate governing law from forum.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Law selected or determined for a particular substantive issue</td></tr><tr><td>Forum</td><td>Body or court competent to hear the dispute</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition</td><td>Acceptance of a foreign judgment’s legal effect under the receiving system’s rules</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Governing law</summary><p>Law selected or determined for a particular substantive issue</p></details><details><summary>Forum</summary><p>Body or court competent to hear the dispute</p></details><details><summary>Recognition</summary><p>Acceptance of a foreign judgment’s legal effect under the receiving system’s rules</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A clause selects governing law only. What remains unresolved?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the parties can disagree</li><li>Only the language of invoices</li><li>The competent forum and recognition or enforcement route</li><li>Whether the contract has any terms</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Choice of law does not necessarily settle forum or enforcement.</p></details><p><strong>A foreign judgment bears a seal. What must still be investigated?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic enforcement against every asset</li><li>Whether the seal is colourful</li><li>The claimant’s preferred exchange rate alone</li><li>Applicable recognition requirements, notice, jurisdiction and relevant defences</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Authenticity is not the whole recognition inquiry.</p></details><p><strong>Why map assets before recommending enforcement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A legally available route may still have practical recovery limits</li><li>Assets determine every substantive rule</li><li>No judgment can be enforced anywhere</li><li>Asset location replaces court procedure</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Practical strategy requires both legal and recovery analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Governing law and Forum.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of governing law and forum. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned international law research task: Write a cross-border litigation map and 1,100-word recognition opinion with a document checklist and conditional enforcement strategy. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a cross-border litigation map and 1,100-word recognition opinion with a document checklist and conditional enforcement strategy.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M12:Public Procurement and Cross-Border Commerce Dossier": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 12 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Public Procurement and Cross-Border Commerce Dossier</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional public tender involves a foreign supplier, a director’s interest and a disputed payment instrument. A later judgment is obtained abroad.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A procurement criteria audit using current instruments and relevant transition checks</li><li>A corporate conflict report and payment-chain diagram identifying each asserted claim</li><li>A 1,500-word cross-border strategy separating procurement review, contractual remedies and judgment recognition</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M13:Rights Analysis, State Duties and Justification": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 13</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: RIGHTS ANALYSIS, STATE DUTIES AND JUSTIFICATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights Law</td><td>Rights Analysis, State Duties and Justification</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain rights analysis, state duties and justification using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A rights analysis identifies the right holder, duty bearer, protected interest and challenged interference or omission. A policy objective and the legality of the means used to pursue it must be evaluated separately.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Right holder</td><td>Person or group protected by the relevant right</td></tr><tr><td>Duty bearer</td><td>Actor bearing the applicable legal obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Justification</td><td>Reasons and evidence assessed under the governing limits framework</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Locate the actual right in the relevant source and read its scope, qualifications and relationship with other provisions. Identify the public action or duty alleged, then connect the available facts to the protection. A statement that a decision is unfair does not by itself identify a legal violation.</p><p>Where the governing framework permits limitations, examine the prescribed justification structure rather than inventing a universal test. Compare the objective, means, evidence and alternatives. Include equality and practical access questions without assuming all different treatment is automatically unlawful. An advanced answer addresses both protection and implementation: what decision, record or remedy would actually respond to the alleged harm?</p><p>A rights matrix should include both the alleged interference and the justification actually advanced. Test the evidence supporting that justification and identify the relevant alternatives where the law calls for them. A legitimate objective cannot answer whether the chosen means meet the applicable requirements. Equally, the existence of a less convenient alternative does not automatically prove a violation; explain its legal relevance and factual feasibility.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a public training centre excludes applicants with a disability under a blanket safety rule without considering the activity or available adjustments. Investigate the governing rights, statutory obligations, evidence of risk and less restrictive measures where legally relevant. Do not treat a safety label as a complete justification or promise admission without assessing actual requirements.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read relevant constitutional rights with the applicable regional instrument. Use African Commission guidance to verify a proposed communication’s admissibility requirements and procedural steps.</p><p><a href=\"https://europe.ohchr.org/universal-declaration-human-rights\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Universal Declaration of Human Rights — UN Human Rights regional office</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Rights Analysis, State Duties and Justification”, start by separating right holder from duty bearer. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A rights analysis identifies the right holder, duty bearer, protected interest and challenged interference or omission. A policy objective and the legality of the means used to pursue it must be evaluated separately.</li><li>Separate right holder from duty bearer.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Right holder</td><td>Person or group protected by the relevant right</td></tr><tr><td>Duty bearer</td><td>Actor bearing the applicable legal obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Justification</td><td>Reasons and evidence assessed under the governing limits framework</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Right holder</summary><p>Person or group protected by the relevant right</p></details><details><summary>Duty bearer</summary><p>Actor bearing the applicable legal obligation</p></details><details><summary>Justification</summary><p>Reasons and evidence assessed under the governing limits framework</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A rule is labelled a safety measure. What remains necessary?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic invalidity of every safety rule</li><li>Only a publicity statement</li><li>Analysis of the applicable right, evidence and legally required justification</li><li>Automatic validity from the label</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> An objective does not by itself establish lawful means.</p></details><p><strong>Why identify the duty bearer precisely?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every private and public actor has identical duties under every instrument</li><li>Rights have no holders</li><li>A label replaces factual analysis</li><li>Different actors and sources may carry different obligations</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The claim must connect a protected interest to a relevant legal obligation.</p></details><p><strong>What should be checked before applying a proportionality formula?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether and how the governing legal framework uses that analysis</li><li>Whether it appears in a foreign video</li><li>Whether the formula is fashionable</li><li>Whether the learner likes the conclusion</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Limitation tests need an applicable legal basis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Right holder and Duty bearer.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of right holder and duty bearer. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned human rights law research task: Prepare a 1,100-word rights assessment with a source-and-duty matrix, competing arguments and a practical remedy proposal. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a 1,100-word rights assessment with a source-and-duty matrix, competing arguments and a practical remedy proposal.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M13:African Commission Communications and Admissibility": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 13</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: AFRICAN COMMISSION COMMUNICATIONS AND ADMISSIBILITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights Law</td><td>African Commission Communications and Admissibility</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain african commission communications and admissibility using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A regional communication is a complaint submitted through a defined human-rights procedure. Admissibility determines whether it may be considered; merits determine whether the substantive violation is established.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Admissibility</td><td>Requirements for considering a communication through the relevant process</td></tr><tr><td>Exhaustion</td><td>Use of applicable domestic remedies, subject to recognised qualifications</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>Substantive assessment of the alleged rights violation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read the current communication guidance and relevant instrument together. Identify complainant, respondent, allegations, supporting material and the steps taken through domestic remedies. Do not assume a regional body is a general appellate court for every disagreement with a local judgment.</p><p>Prepare a remedy chronology that distinguishes available, used and allegedly ineffective routes. If an exception is invoked, state its legal basis and supporting facts. Consider confidentiality and protection of affected persons; coursework must use fictional facts or properly handled public material. A carefully framed submission separates admissibility arguments, merits and requested measures, and makes clear which facts are alleged rather than established.</p><p>Prepare admissibility before writing the merits narrative. For every domestic step, record the application, response, date, present status and supporting document. If an exception is argued, identify the particular requirement and why the facts satisfy the recognised qualification. This structure allows the regional complaint to be assessed without assuming that dissatisfaction with a national process is itself enough or that a favourable admissibility decision proves the substantive claim.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a community group wishes to complain regionally after an unresolved national land-rights proceeding. It alleges delay but supplies no dates or orders. Build a domestic-remedies timeline and investigate the applicable admissibility requirements and any argued exception. Delay must be substantiated and legally analysed rather than asserted as a magic word.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read relevant constitutional rights with the applicable regional instrument. Use African Commission guidance to verify a proposed communication’s admissibility requirements and procedural steps.</p><p><a href=\"https://europe.ohchr.org/universal-declaration-human-rights\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Universal Declaration of Human Rights — UN Human Rights regional office</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “African Commission Communications and Admissibility”, start by separating admissibility from exhaustion. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A regional communication is a complaint submitted through a defined human-rights procedure. Admissibility determines whether it may be considered; merits determine whether the substantive violation is established.</li><li>Separate admissibility from exhaustion.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Admissibility</td><td>Requirements for considering a communication through the relevant process</td></tr><tr><td>Exhaustion</td><td>Use of applicable domestic remedies, subject to recognised qualifications</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>Substantive assessment of the alleged rights violation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Admissibility</summary><p>Requirements for considering a communication through the relevant process</p></details><details><summary>Exhaustion</summary><p>Use of applicable domestic remedies, subject to recognised qualifications</p></details><details><summary>Merits</summary><p>Substantive assessment of the alleged rights violation</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A group alleges prolonged delay without dates. What is needed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic exemption from every requirement</li><li>A fabricated court order</li><li>Only a general statement of frustration</li><li>A supported procedural chronology and the applicable admissibility analysis</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Exceptions need both legal and factual support.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate admissibility from merits?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Permission to consider a communication does not itself prove a violation</li><li>Admissibility guarantees compensation</li><li>Merits eliminate all procedural requirements</li><li>The stages are always identical</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Regional procedures distinguish access questions from substantive findings.</p></details><p><strong>How should a coursework communication handle affected persons’ details?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Assume educational use removes every ethical duty</li><li>Use fictional or appropriately public material and consider confidentiality</li><li>Publish private client records</li><li>Invent real people’s testimony</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Learning exercises should not expose confidential or unsupported personal claims.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Admissibility and Exhaustion.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of admissibility and exhaustion. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned human rights law research task: Draft a fictional communication outline, domestic-remedies chronology and 1,000-word admissibility note with pinpoint references to current guidance. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional communication outline, domestic-remedies chronology and 1,000-word admissibility note with pinpoint references to current guidance.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M14:Family Relationships, Child Welfare and Competing Interests": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 14</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: FAMILY RELATIONSHIPS, CHILD WELFARE AND COMPETING INTERESTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Family Law</td><td>Family Relationships, Child Welfare and Competing Interests</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain family relationships, child welfare and competing interests using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Family status, parental responsibility and a child-related decision are connected but distinct legal questions. A child’s welfare must be assessed under the applicable framework rather than treated as an automatic entitlement of the more powerful adult.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Family status</td><td>Legally recognised relationship relevant to the issue</td></tr><tr><td>Welfare assessment</td><td>Evaluation under the applicable child-related legal framework</td></tr><tr><td>Interim protection</td><td>Temporary response pending a fuller competent determination</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the legal relationship and decision sought before choosing a rule. Marriage, separation, support and care arrangements may invoke different sources and procedures. Separate evidence of financial means from evidence about care, safety and continuity. A classroom opinion should never presume misconduct from gender, poverty or family form.</p><p>Where the law requires an assessment of a child’s interests, examine the relevant circumstances and lawful participation safeguards. An adult’s account may need corroboration; a child’s information requires sensitive, appropriately authorised handling. Distinguish temporary protective arrangements from final status decisions. Consider workable orders and referral needs without offering unsupervised representation. Advanced analysis acknowledges conflicting interests and explains why a proposed arrangement responds to the evidence.</p><p>Use neutral descriptions of the care history and distinguish direct observation from allegations. A decision affecting a child should be capable of explanation in terms of the relevant legal factors and supported facts, not stereotypes about wealth or parental roles. Consider how an interim arrangement would operate in practice, who would carry it out and how unresolved safety concerns could be investigated through an appropriately authorised process.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: separated parents disagree over school attendance and care. One has higher income; the other has provided daily care. A relative alleges a safety concern but gives no dates. A balanced opinion identifies the governing provisions, examines each factor and proposes lawful fact-finding. Neither income alone nor an unsupported allegation should determine the outcome.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read applicable Domestic Relations Law and Decedents Estates Law provisions. Verify the relevant family relationship, child-related requirements and succession procedure in current Liberian authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=59:domestic-relations-law&amp;start=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Domestic Relations Law, Title 9 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=45:decedents-estates-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decedents Estates Law, Title 8 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Family Relationships, Child Welfare and Competing Interests”, start by separating family status from welfare assessment. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Family status, parental responsibility and a child-related decision are connected but distinct legal questions. A child’s welfare must be assessed under the applicable framework rather than treated as an automatic entitlement of the more powerful adult.</li><li>Separate family status from welfare assessment.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Family status</td><td>Legally recognised relationship relevant to the issue</td></tr><tr><td>Welfare assessment</td><td>Evaluation under the applicable child-related legal framework</td></tr><tr><td>Interim protection</td><td>Temporary response pending a fuller competent determination</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Family status</summary><p>Legally recognised relationship relevant to the issue</p></details><details><summary>Welfare assessment</summary><p>Evaluation under the applicable child-related legal framework</p></details><details><summary>Interim protection</summary><p>Temporary response pending a fuller competent determination</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>One parent earns more money. What is the sound analytical approach?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Treat income as the sole deciding factor</li><li>Ignore all financial circumstances</li><li>Assume daily care always resolves every issue</li><li>Assess income with the other legally relevant care and welfare evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The governing framework and full record determine relevance and weight.</p></details><p><strong>A relative reports a safety concern without dates. What should the plan do?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Seek appropriate particulars and lawful protection while avoiding unsupported findings</li><li>Declare the allegation established</li><li>Dismiss the concern because it is incomplete</li><li>Publicise the family’s details</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Safety and fairness require careful evidence handling.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish interim protection from final family status?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Interim arrangements cannot affect anyone</li><li>A temporary safety measure does not necessarily decide the ultimate legal relationship</li><li>Temporary orders permanently resolve all rights</li><li>Family status never requires legal authority</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Different decisions can have different tests and consequences.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Family status and Welfare assessment.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of family status and welfare assessment. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned family law research task: Write a 1,100-word fictional child-related decision memorandum with a neutral facts section, evidence gaps and an implementation plan protecting confidentiality. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,100-word fictional child-related decision memorandum with a neutral facts section, evidence gaps and an implementation plan protecting confidentiality.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M14:Intestacy, Estate Administration and Dependant Claims": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 14</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INTESTACY, ESTATE ADMINISTRATION AND DEPENDANT CLAIMS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Family Law</td><td>Intestacy, Estate Administration and Dependant Claims</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain intestacy, estate administration and dependant claims using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Intestacy concerns succession where property is not effectively disposed of by a valid will under the governing law. Estate administration manages the deceased person’s affairs; a beneficiary’s possible entitlement is not the same as authority to act for the estate.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intestacy</td><td>Succession governed by the applicable rules where testamentary disposition is absent or ineffective</td></tr><tr><td>Administrator</td><td>Person authorised through the relevant process to administer an estate</td></tr><tr><td>Estate inventory</td><td>Supported account of estate assets, liabilities and disputed items</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the deceased’s interests, debts, claimed relationships, any testamentary document and the relevant procedural record. Separate jointly held or third-party property from assets actually belonging to the estate. A list made by a relative is a starting allegation, not a final legal inventory.</p><p>Research who may administer and what court or process confers authority. Analyse competing succession claims using current Liberian provisions rather than foreign family-share tables. Keep administration expenses, creditor claims and beneficiary distribution distinct. An advanced report also explains disputed valuations, missing assets and documentary uncertainty. Do not distribute property on the assumption that the loudest family member represents everyone.</p><p>Build the estate analysis in layers: identify what belonged to the deceased, determine who is authorised to administer, verify liabilities and then analyse potential distribution. A dispute at one layer may prevent a confident conclusion at a later one. Explain how the unsigned document, supplier ownership claim and loan each affect a different part of the sequence rather than assuming that all family expectations are immediately distributable assets.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a trader dies leaving a shop, an equipment loan and an unsigned draft will. Two relatives claim authority to sell stock; another says some goods belong to a supplier. Investigate the will’s legal status, administration authority and ownership of the stock before discussing distribution. The loan and supplier claim cannot be ignored merely because beneficiaries need support.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read applicable Domestic Relations Law and Decedents Estates Law provisions. Verify the relevant family relationship, child-related requirements and succession procedure in current Liberian authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=59:domestic-relations-law&amp;start=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Domestic Relations Law, Title 9 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=45:decedents-estates-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decedents Estates Law, Title 8 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Intestacy, Estate Administration and Dependant Claims”, start by separating intestacy from administrator. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Intestacy concerns succession where property is not effectively disposed of by a valid will under the governing law. Estate administration manages the deceased person’s affairs; a beneficiary’s possible entitlement is not the same as authority to act for the estate.</li><li>Separate intestacy from administrator.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intestacy</td><td>Succession governed by the applicable rules where testamentary disposition is absent or ineffective</td></tr><tr><td>Administrator</td><td>Person authorised through the relevant process to administer an estate</td></tr><tr><td>Estate inventory</td><td>Supported account of estate assets, liabilities and disputed items</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Intestacy</summary><p>Succession governed by the applicable rules where testamentary disposition is absent or ineffective</p></details><details><summary>Administrator</summary><p>Person authorised through the relevant process to administer an estate</p></details><details><summary>Estate inventory</summary><p>Supported account of estate assets, liabilities and disputed items</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A relative expects to inherit. What does that not automatically establish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Authority to administer or sell estate property</li><li>A reason to investigate their claim</li><li>The existence of a family relationship</li><li>The relevance of the estate record</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Beneficiary entitlement and administration power are separate.</p></details><p><strong>A shop contains supplier-owned goods. What must the inventory distinguish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Ownership from any legal relevance</li><li>Estate assets from property owned by others</li><li>All physically present goods as estate property</li><li>Only the highest-value items</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Possession at death does not establish estate ownership of every item.</p></details><p><strong>An unsigned draft will is found. What should be done?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Destroy it because it is incomplete</li><li>Copy a foreign distribution table</li><li>Verify its legal status and applicable formal requirements before relying on it</li><li>Assume it always controls</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Testamentary validity requires the governing law and evidence.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Intestacy and Administrator.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of intestacy and administrator. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned family law research task: Prepare an estate inventory and 1,100-word administration-and-succession opinion, avoiding assumed inheritance shares or authority. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an estate inventory and 1,100-word administration-and-succession opinion, avoiding assumed inheritance shares or authority.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M15:Expert Testimony, Method and Evidential Weight": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 15</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: EXPERT TESTIMONY, METHOD AND EVIDENTIAL WEIGHT</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Expert Testimony, Method and Evidential Weight</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain expert testimony, method and evidential weight using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Expert evidence offers specialised assistance on a relevant issue. Qualification, reliability, admissibility and weight are different inquiries; expertise does not authorise deciding the legal question for the court.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Qualification</td><td>Relevant specialised knowledge or experience</td></tr><tr><td>Method</td><td>Reasoning process linking data to an opinion</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>Persuasive value assigned after evaluating the evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify what specialised issue needs explanation and what the witness is actually qualified to address. A medical qualification does not automatically establish expertise in digital forensics. Examine the data, method, assumptions and limitations supporting the opinion. Distinguish a measured observation from a conclusion drawn through specialised reasoning.</p><p>Test whether the opinion engages with alternative explanations and contrary data. A report should permit scrutiny rather than conceal the reasoning behind a confident title. Verify the local admissibility requirements before using a foreign expert-evidence formula. Once admitted, an opinion may still carry limited weight because of weak data or unsupported assumptions. An advanced cross-examination plan targets the reasoning and its limits without misrepresenting the witness’s actual conclusion.</p><p>Separate the expert’s ultimate conclusion from the premises necessary to reach it. Identify which premises are observed, supplied by another person or assumed for the report. Ask whether the conclusion remains defensible if one disputed premise changes. This sensitivity analysis makes the opinion’s limits visible and supports focused examination. It does not authorise the learner to substitute their own unsupported technical expertise for the witness’s field.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: an engineer attributes a clinic’s generator fire to defective wiring after reviewing one photograph, without inspecting the machine or maintenance records. The learner should identify what the photograph can show, what alternative causes remain and which additional records matter. The engineer’s credentials alone do not resolve causal reliability.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use applicable Liberian procedural evidence provisions and court rules, with the professional ethics code for privilege-related issues. Record the precise local basis for every admissibility objection.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Expert Testimony, Method and Evidential Weight”, start by separating qualification from method. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Expert evidence offers specialised assistance on a relevant issue. Qualification, reliability, admissibility and weight are different inquiries; expertise does not authorise deciding the legal question for the court.</li><li>Separate qualification from method.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Qualification</td><td>Relevant specialised knowledge or experience</td></tr><tr><td>Method</td><td>Reasoning process linking data to an opinion</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>Persuasive value assigned after evaluating the evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Qualification</summary><p>Relevant specialised knowledge or experience</p></details><details><summary>Method</summary><p>Reasoning process linking data to an opinion</p></details><details><summary>Weight</summary><p>Persuasive value assigned after evaluating the evidence</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>An expert has impressive credentials but inadequate data. What is the sound analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Assess data, method and limitations separately from qualification</li><li>Treat credentials as conclusive</li><li>Reject all expert evidence automatically</li><li>Assume admission guarantees maximum weight</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Expertise and support for the particular conclusion are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish admissibility from weight?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Excluded evidence always proves the opposite</li><li>Evidence permitted for consideration can still be weak</li><li>Admitted evidence must decide the case</li><li>Weight means physical mass only</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> The stages address different legal and evaluative questions.</p></details><p><strong>An engineer reviewed one photograph. Which question best tests the opinion?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Will you guarantee the claimant wins?</li><li>Is your title longer than the other witness’s?</li><li>What alternative causes cannot be assessed without inspection or maintenance records?</li><li>Why are all engineers unreliable?</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Focused questions examine the actual limits of reasoning.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Qualification and Method.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of qualification and method. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned evidence research task: Write an expert-evidence review with a method-and-assumptions table and ten focused cross-examination questions, each linked to a genuine limitation. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write an expert-evidence review with a method-and-assumptions table and ten focused cross-examination questions, each linked to a genuine limitation.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M15:Privilege, Confidentiality and Lawful Evidence Handling": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 15</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PRIVILEGE, CONFIDENTIALITY AND LAWFUL EVIDENCE HANDLING</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Privilege, Confidentiality and Lawful Evidence Handling</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain privilege, confidentiality and lawful evidence handling using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Confidentiality is a duty concerning information. Evidential privilege concerns protection from compelled disclosure where its legal requirements apply. A confidential label does not automatically establish privilege.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Duty to protect information within its applicable scope</td></tr><tr><td>Privilege</td><td>Legally recognised protection against disclosure where requirements are met</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-existing record</td><td>Document created independently of the later legal-advice exchange</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the communication, participants, purpose, timing and person entitled to invoke any protection. Separate pre-existing records from advice about them. Sharing a document with a lawyer does not automatically transform every underlying fact or record into protected material. Verify the applicable local basis and exceptions.</p><p>Preserve disputed material and use the competent procedure to resolve objections. Do not conceal or destroy records under the pretext of confidentiality. If disclosure is ordered or contested, analyse scope, redaction and lawful safeguards rather than assuming either complete secrecy or unrestricted publication. An advanced opinion distinguishes ethical handling, admissibility and evidential reliability; a document can raise more than one issue without those issues becoming interchangeable.</p><p>A review schedule should classify each item by origin, purpose, participants, asserted protection and disputed basis. Do not disclose substantive protected content unnecessarily merely to explain that a protection is claimed. At the same time, do not use a blanket label to conceal the existence of relevant records. Research the competent local process for resolving the dispute and distinguish that procedure from destruction, public release or alteration of the material.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a business sends an old ledger to counsel, marks it “privileged” and later refuses to produce any financial records. Another message contains actual legal advice. The learner must classify each item and investigate the governing protection and procedure. The ledger’s underlying status is not resolved simply by the email label.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use applicable Liberian procedural evidence provisions and court rules, with the professional ethics code for privilege-related issues. Record the precise local basis for every admissibility objection.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Privilege, Confidentiality and Lawful Evidence Handling”, start by separating confidentiality from privilege. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Confidentiality is a duty concerning information. Evidential privilege concerns protection from compelled disclosure where its legal requirements apply. A confidential label does not automatically establish privilege.</li><li>Separate confidentiality from privilege.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Duty to protect information within its applicable scope</td></tr><tr><td>Privilege</td><td>Legally recognised protection against disclosure where requirements are met</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-existing record</td><td>Document created independently of the later legal-advice exchange</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Confidentiality</summary><p>Duty to protect information within its applicable scope</p></details><details><summary>Privilege</summary><p>Legally recognised protection against disclosure where requirements are met</p></details><details><summary>Pre-existing record</summary><p>Document created independently of the later legal-advice exchange</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A ledger is emailed to a lawyer. What should not be assumed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That the governing rule should be checked</li><li>That the pre-existing record automatically becomes privileged in every respect</li><li>That legal advice may raise a separate issue</li><li>That the ledger’s origin matters</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Communication with counsel does not by itself resolve every document’s status.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish confidentiality from privilege?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Confidentiality abolishes court procedure</li><li>Privilege applies to every business record</li><li>They have different purposes, scope and legal consequences</li><li>They are always interchangeable</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> A careful analysis identifies the specific applicable protection.</p></details><p><strong>What is appropriate when protection is disputed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Destroy the disputed document</li><li>Rewrite its date</li><li>Publish all client records immediately</li><li>Preserve the material and seek a competent ruling through the applicable process</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Lawful handling preserves integrity while the issue is resolved.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Confidentiality and Privilege.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of confidentiality and privilege. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned evidence research task: Create a fictional privilege-review schedule and 1,000-word evidence-handling opinion distinguishing the ledger, legal advice and lawful disclosure process. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a fictional privilege-review schedule and 1,000-word evidence-handling opinion distinguishing the ledger, legal advice and lawful disclosure process.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M16:Doctrinal Research, Source Status and Comparative Method": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 16</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DOCTRINAL RESEARCH, SOURCE STATUS AND COMPARATIVE METHOD</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Research and Writing</td><td>Doctrinal Research, Source Status and Comparative Method</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain doctrinal research, source status and comparative method using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Doctrinal research develops an account of the law from legal sources and their authoritative relationships. Comparative research examines another system through an explicit question; it does not presume that similarly named doctrines have identical local effects.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Doctrinal proposition</td><td>Claim about law supported by appropriate legal authority</td></tr><tr><td>Empirical proposition</td><td>Claim about practice requiring suitable factual evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Source-status log</td><td>Record of version, authority, dates and verification limits</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Frame a research question narrow enough to answer and define its jurisdiction, period and legal problem. Construct a source hierarchy and search plan before drafting conclusions. Record versions, commencement, amendments and the status of judgments. A secondary explanation helps locate or interpret primary material but does not replace verification of the relied-on text.</p><p>Use a claim-to-source matrix: state the proposition, source, pinpoint, authority status and any contrary material. For comparison, justify the comparator and identify institutional differences that may limit transfer. A research gap is not proof that no rule exists. An advanced project explains its search limits and distinguishes doctrinal conclusions from empirical claims about what officials actually do.</p><p>Define a stopping rule for the search without overstating completeness. Record the repositories, search terms, date range and authority checks undertaken, then identify the material gaps that remain. A source being difficult to obtain is a limitation to disclose, not permission to invent its contents. Comparative material should be selected for a stated analytical purpose, and claims about institutional practice should be supported by evidence suitable for that claim.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a draft says online court filing is universally available because a neighbouring country introduced it. The researcher must identify the Liberian procedural source and actual institutional evidence separately. If the search finds no current official confirmation, the conclusion should report that limit, not invent a service or declare that none can exist.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use official Liberian legislation, court rules and published judgments. Keep a source-status log. Use the assigned research guide for method and distinguish it from controlling local authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://guides.loc.gov/law-secondary-resources\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal research: secondary resources — Library of Congress</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Doctrinal Research, Source Status and Comparative Method”, start by separating doctrinal proposition from empirical proposition. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Doctrinal research develops an account of the law from legal sources and their authoritative relationships. Comparative research examines another system through an explicit question; it does not presume that similarly named doctrines have identical local effects.</li><li>Separate doctrinal proposition from empirical proposition.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Doctrinal proposition</td><td>Claim about law supported by appropriate legal authority</td></tr><tr><td>Empirical proposition</td><td>Claim about practice requiring suitable factual evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Source-status log</td><td>Record of version, authority, dates and verification limits</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Doctrinal proposition</summary><p>Claim about law supported by appropriate legal authority</p></details><details><summary>Empirical proposition</summary><p>Claim about practice requiring suitable factual evidence</p></details><details><summary>Source-status log</summary><p>Record of version, authority, dates and verification limits</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A researcher finds no rule in one database. What is the sound conclusion?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Treat a foreign rule as automatically local</li><li>Report the search limit and investigate further rather than infer universal absence</li><li>Declare no rule exists anywhere</li><li>Invent a rule to complete the table</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> A limited search does not establish absence of law.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish doctrinal and empirical propositions?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Practice always amends statutes</li><li>Doctrinal research never uses judgments</li><li>What the law provides and what institutions do require different supporting evidence</li><li>Both are proved by any quotation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The proposition determines the appropriate type of support.</p></details><p><strong>What belongs in a claim-to-source matrix?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only attractive quotations</li><li>Only the author’s confidence level</li><li>Unverified case names</li><li>The proposition, pinpoint authority, status and relevant contrary material</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> A matrix makes the evidential and authoritative basis auditable.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Doctrinal proposition and Empirical proposition.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of doctrinal proposition and empirical proposition. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned legal research and writing research task: Develop a 1,200-word research proposal, ten-source annotated matrix and transparent search log for a narrow Liberian legal question. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Develop a 1,200-word research proposal, ten-source annotated matrix and transparent search log for a narrow Liberian legal question.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M16:Predictive Memoranda, Critical Argument and Citation Integrity": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 16</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PREDICTIVE MEMORANDA, CRITICAL ARGUMENT AND CITATION INTEGRITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Research and Writing</td><td>Predictive Memoranda, Critical Argument and Citation Integrity</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain predictive memoranda, critical argument and citation integrity using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A predictive memorandum evaluates likely outcomes for a reader, including adverse authority and factual uncertainty. A critical research argument advances and defends a thesis rather than merely arranging quotations.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Predictive advice</td><td>Balanced assessment of likely outcomes and limits</td></tr><tr><td>Counterargument</td><td>Strongest relevant objection to the proposed conclusion</td></tr><tr><td>Citation integrity</td><td>Accurate attribution to sources actually checked</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Organise a memorandum around the legal issues raised by material facts. State a concise answer only after testing each issue. Paragraphs should connect a proposition, applicable authority and reasoned application. Separate a binding holding from incidental discussion and explain why an analogy or distinction matters.</p><p>A research argument must engage with the strongest objection. Distinguish criticism of a rule from a claim that the rule is not currently law. Cite only sources actually checked and mark quotations accurately. Never invent a case, page or authority to fill a gap. Where a tool assists research or drafting, verify its claims and follow any applicable disclosure requirements. An advanced conclusion states what is established, what remains uncertain and what next inquiry would change the advice.</p><p>Audit each paragraph for a proposition, support and analytical step. A citation at the end of a page does not establish which source supports each claim. Distinguish what the authority holds from your inference about a new fact pattern. Where the contrary case is stronger than expected, revise the prediction rather than hiding it in a footnote. Clear writing serves the reasoning; polished language cannot repair unsupported premises.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a memo promises victory using a case citation that cannot be located and omits a contrary published judgment. The correct repair is to remove or verify the unsupported citation, analyse the contrary authority and revise the prediction. More polished wording does not cure a false source or one-sided reasoning.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use official Liberian legislation, court rules and published judgments. Keep a source-status log. Use the assigned research guide for method and distinguish it from controlling local authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://guides.loc.gov/law-secondary-resources\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal research: secondary resources — Library of Congress</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Predictive Memoranda, Critical Argument and Citation Integrity”, start by separating predictive advice from counterargument. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A predictive memorandum evaluates likely outcomes for a reader, including adverse authority and factual uncertainty. A critical research argument advances and defends a thesis rather than merely arranging quotations.</li><li>Separate predictive advice from counterargument.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Predictive advice</td><td>Balanced assessment of likely outcomes and limits</td></tr><tr><td>Counterargument</td><td>Strongest relevant objection to the proposed conclusion</td></tr><tr><td>Citation integrity</td><td>Accurate attribution to sources actually checked</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Predictive advice</summary><p>Balanced assessment of likely outcomes and limits</p></details><details><summary>Counterargument</summary><p>Strongest relevant objection to the proposed conclusion</p></details><details><summary>Citation integrity</summary><p>Accurate attribution to sources actually checked</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A cited case cannot be located. What should the writer do?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Keep it because the wording is persuasive</li><li>Assume all generated citations are real</li><li>Verify it or remove the unsupported reliance and disclose the gap</li><li>Invent a page number</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> A legal conclusion must not depend on fabricated authority.</p></details><p><strong>A contrary binding judgment is inconvenient. What improves the memorandum?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Delete it from the research log</li><li>Quote only favourable commentary</li><li>Guarantee success anyway</li><li>Analyse its relevance and effect on the proposed prediction</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Predictive advice requires candid treatment of adverse authority.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes critical argument from a quotation collection?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A defended thesis connecting authorities, reasoning and objections</li><li>A longer bibliography alone</li><li>Absence of conclusions</li><li>Use of unexplained foreign citations</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Critical writing develops reasons rather than merely accumulating text.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Predictive advice and Counterargument.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of predictive advice and counterargument. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned legal research and writing research task: Write a 1,500-word predictive memorandum with a short answer, adverse-authority section and a citation audit identifying every checked pinpoint. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,500-word predictive memorandum with a short answer, adverse-authority section and a citation audit identifying every checked pinpoint.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M16:Rights, Family and Evidence Research Portfolio": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 16 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Rights, Family and Evidence Research Portfolio</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional family disputes estate administration while a community organisation considers a regional rights complaint. An expert report and privileged email are contested.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>An estate inventory, administration-authority note and confidential-information handling plan</li><li>An expert-method review and separate privilege schedule with locally sourced objections</li><li>A 1,600-word research memorandum with a domestic-remedies chronology and citation audit</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M17:Confidentiality, Candour and Responsible Legal Assistance": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 17</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONFIDENTIALITY, CANDOUR AND RESPONSIBLE LEGAL ASSISTANCE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Ethics and Professional Responsibility</td><td>Confidentiality, Candour and Responsible Legal Assistance</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain confidentiality, candour and responsible legal assistance using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Professional responsibility concerns duties governing legal work and professional conduct. Confidentiality, candour and competence can raise different obligations; a client’s preferred outcome does not authorise misleading a court or fabricating a record.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Candour</td><td>Honesty toward the relevant tribunal or decision-maker under applicable duties</td></tr><tr><td>Competence</td><td>Ability and care appropriate to the authorised task</td></tr><tr><td>Role limitation</td><td>Boundary between an educational exercise and professional legal service</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the learner’s role and the applicable duty before giving assistance. A TIH certificate does not confer bar admission or authority to represent clients. In simulations, use fictional facts and explain the limits of the exercise. In professional analysis, distinguish information received from a client, verified documents and inferences.</p><p>Where confidentiality and a disclosure issue appear to conflict, research the actual rule and competent process rather than inventing an exception. Preserve records and seek appropriately qualified guidance. Competence includes recognising when the issue exceeds one’s knowledge or authority. An advanced ethical response identifies lawful options, their consequences and the information needed to choose, instead of simply stating that loyalty requires doing whatever the client requests.</p><p>An ethical options table should identify the proposed action, relevant duty, affected interest and lawful next step. Separate refusal to mislead from the different question of what information may lawfully be disclosed. Do not invent a universal duty either to publicise every client admission or to conceal every relevant fact. The correct response depends on the applicable professional rules and competent advice, while the learner’s own educational role remains limited.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a business asks a learner to draft a court statement from a ledger the business admits was altered. The learner must not present the alteration as genuine evidence or promise representation based on this course. Preserve the distinction between confidential discussion and misleading submission, identify the governing duties and refer the matter for authorised advice.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Liberian Code of Moral and Professional Ethics and relevant court rules. Distinguish course-based learning from the legal qualifications and authorisation required for professional practice.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/code-of-moral-and-professional-ethic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Code of Moral and Professional Ethics — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Confidentiality, Candour and Responsible Legal Assistance”, start by separating candour from competence. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Professional responsibility concerns duties governing legal work and professional conduct. Confidentiality, candour and competence can raise different obligations; a client’s preferred outcome does not authorise misleading a court or fabricating a record.</li><li>Separate candour from competence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Candour</td><td>Honesty toward the relevant tribunal or decision-maker under applicable duties</td></tr><tr><td>Competence</td><td>Ability and care appropriate to the authorised task</td></tr><tr><td>Role limitation</td><td>Boundary between an educational exercise and professional legal service</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Candour</summary><p>Honesty toward the relevant tribunal or decision-maker under applicable duties</p></details><details><summary>Competence</summary><p>Ability and care appropriate to the authorised task</p></details><details><summary>Role limitation</summary><p>Boundary between an educational exercise and professional legal service</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A client asks that an altered ledger be presented as genuine. What is the sound response?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Backdate the ledger again</li><li>Assume confidentiality makes false evidence lawful</li><li>Do not misrepresent it; analyse applicable duties and seek authorised guidance</li><li>Treat loyalty as permission to fabricate</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Client preference does not establish permission to mislead.</p></details><p><strong>What does completing this TIH programme establish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic admission to the Supreme Court bar</li><li>A university law degree</li><li>Unlimited authority to represent clients</li><li>Completion of an independent educational course, not permission to practise law</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Professional authorisation requires the applicable separate qualifications and process.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish verified facts from client instructions?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A client’s account is not automatically an established evidential finding</li><li>Client instructions are never relevant</li><li>A lawyer may invent corroboration</li><li>Only adverse facts need verification</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Responsible work makes the basis and limits of statements clear.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Candour and Competence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of candour and competence. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned legal ethics and professional responsibility research task: Write an ethical decision memorandum with a facts-to-duty matrix, lawful options and a referral explanation in plain language. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write an ethical decision memorandum with a facts-to-duty matrix, lawful options and a referral explanation in plain language.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M17:Conflicts, Informed Decisions and Protective File Controls": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 17</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONFLICTS, INFORMED DECISIONS AND PROTECTIVE FILE CONTROLS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Ethics and Professional Responsibility</td><td>Conflicts, Informed Decisions and Protective File Controls</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain conflicts, informed decisions and protective file controls using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A conflict of interest arises where relevant duties or interests may be incompatible under the governing rules. Disclosure, consent and withdrawal must be assessed within those rules; consent is not a universal cure.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conflict check</td><td>Structured inquiry into relationships, interests and relevant information</td></tr><tr><td>Informed decision</td><td>Choice based on sufficient relevant information within lawful disclosure limits</td></tr><tr><td>Protective control</td><td>Specific measure reducing an identified handling or access risk</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify current and former relationships, personal interests and information held before receiving unnecessary sensitive details. A conflict check should cover the matter, parties and related entities, not only the person who first makes contact. Separate a potential issue from a concluded legal prohibition and research the applicable standard.</p><p>Where consent is legally possible, examine whether the person has enough information to make the relevant decision without improperly disclosing another person’s protected information. If continued assistance is impermissible, plan a lawful transition that protects records and procedural interests. File controls should restrict access, maintain versions and preserve client or project property. An advanced answer explains why a proposed safeguard addresses the particular risk rather than using generic promises of fairness.</p><p>Test consent as a process rather than a signature. Identify who is entitled to decide, what information can lawfully be supplied and whether the conflict is one for which consent is permitted. If those conditions cannot be met, a waiver form is not a cure. A transition plan should protect records and lawful interests without assuming the conflicted person may continue substantive work simply because withdrawal would be inconvenient.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a practitioner previously advised a cooperative, then is asked by its director to act against it in a related land dispute. The director says everyone will consent. Investigate the prior matter, confidential information and governing conflict rule before assuming consent is available or meaningful. A separate digital folder alone does not resolve every duty conflict.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Liberian Code of Moral and Professional Ethics and relevant court rules. Distinguish course-based learning from the legal qualifications and authorisation required for professional practice.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/code-of-moral-and-professional-ethic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Code of Moral and Professional Ethics — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Conflicts, Informed Decisions and Protective File Controls”, start by separating conflict check from informed decision. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A conflict of interest arises where relevant duties or interests may be incompatible under the governing rules. Disclosure, consent and withdrawal must be assessed within those rules; consent is not a universal cure.</li><li>Separate conflict check from informed decision.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conflict check</td><td>Structured inquiry into relationships, interests and relevant information</td></tr><tr><td>Informed decision</td><td>Choice based on sufficient relevant information within lawful disclosure limits</td></tr><tr><td>Protective control</td><td>Specific measure reducing an identified handling or access risk</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Conflict check</summary><p>Structured inquiry into relationships, interests and relevant information</p></details><details><summary>Informed decision</summary><p>Choice based on sufficient relevant information within lawful disclosure limits</p></details><details><summary>Protective control</summary><p>Specific measure reducing an identified handling or access risk</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A former client’s director offers consent for everyone. What must be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic cure by any individual’s signature</li><li>Whether the director is persuasive</li><li>Whether separate folders always solve conflicts</li><li>Who holds the relevant interests and whether the rule permits and requirements support consent</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Authority and the legal possibility of consent require analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Why perform a conflict check before collecting extensive information?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It can reveal incompatible duties before unnecessary protected material is received</li><li>Information can never create ethical difficulty</li><li>Only payment details matter</li><li>Conflicts disappear if discovered late</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Early checking helps avoid preventable handling problems.</p></details><p><strong>What makes a file safeguard useful?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Shared passwords for all participants</li><li>A specific connection to an identified confidentiality or access risk</li><li>A generic statement that everyone is trustworthy</li><li>Deletion of audit history</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Controls should respond to concrete risks.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Conflict check and Informed decision.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of conflict check and informed decision. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned legal ethics and professional responsibility research task: Create a fictional conflict-check form and 1,100-word advice note evaluating consent, safeguards and lawful withdrawal options. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a fictional conflict-check form and 1,100-word advice note evaluating consent, safeguards and lawful withdrawal options.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M18:Wages, Working Time and Employment Classification": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 18</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: WAGES, WORKING TIME AND EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Labor and Employment Law</td><td>Wages, Working Time and Employment Classification</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain wages, working time and employment classification using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Employment classification determines which legal framework applies to a working relationship. A contract label is evidence but does not necessarily settle status. Pay reconciliation compares the legally relevant entitlement with actual records and payments.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Classification</td><td>Legal characterisation of the actual working relationship</td></tr><tr><td>Reconciliation</td><td>Comparison of entitlements, records and payments</td></tr><tr><td>Lawful deduction</td><td>Deduction supported by the applicable legal and factual basis</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the work, supervision, continuity, contractual terms and actual performance. Distinguish a genuine independent enterprise from a relationship merely labelled contracting. Verify the local status test and any sector-specific rules. Do not import a foreign employee definition automatically.</p><p>Build a pay table using verified periods, hours, agreed rates, lawful deductions and payments. Keep ordinary remuneration, overtime, leave and reimbursements separate where the law distinguishes them. A spreadsheet total is only as sound as its inputs and legal basis. Where records conflict, show alternative calculations and the evidence required to choose. An advanced opinion also considers the appropriate institution and procedure for resolving the dispute.</p><p>A pay reconciliation should be reproducible by another reader. State the source for each rate, hour total and deduction, distinguish agreed and disputed inputs and show the arithmetic. Where classification changes the legal entitlement, present the consequences of each legally plausible conclusion instead of hiding the classification issue inside the spreadsheet. The final figure should be a supported result, not the starting point around which evidence is selected.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a training centre calls a cleaner a contractor, sets daily hours and deducts unexplained amounts from monthly pay. The cleaner’s time log differs from the centre’s sheet. Investigate classification and applicable pay duties before calculating. Do not assume the label excludes all protection or that either spreadsheet is automatically correct.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read relevant Decent Work Act provisions and current labour-institution materials. Verify applicability, worker status, pay obligations, collective rights and the competent dispute route.</p><p><a href=\"https://csa.gov.lr/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Decent-Work-Act-2015.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decent Work Act 2015 — Civil Service Agency</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Wages, Working Time and Employment Classification”, start by separating classification from reconciliation. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Employment classification determines which legal framework applies to a working relationship. A contract label is evidence but does not necessarily settle status. Pay reconciliation compares the legally relevant entitlement with actual records and payments.</li><li>Separate classification from reconciliation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Classification</td><td>Legal characterisation of the actual working relationship</td></tr><tr><td>Reconciliation</td><td>Comparison of entitlements, records and payments</td></tr><tr><td>Lawful deduction</td><td>Deduction supported by the applicable legal and factual basis</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Classification</summary><p>Legal characterisation of the actual working relationship</p></details><details><summary>Reconciliation</summary><p>Comparison of entitlements, records and payments</p></details><details><summary>Lawful deduction</summary><p>Deduction supported by the applicable legal and factual basis</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A contract says independent contractor. What should the opinion examine?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The label as conclusive in every case</li><li>Only the worker’s preferred title</li><li>A foreign payroll rule as binding</li><li>The actual relationship and applicable local classification test</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Status depends on the governing framework and relevant facts.</p></details><p><strong>Two time records disagree. What is a transparent calculation method?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Show the competing inputs, legal basis and evidence needed to resolve them</li><li>Choose the larger total without explanation</li><li>Delete the employer’s sheet</li><li>Assume all deductions are lawful</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Calculations should reveal rather than hide uncertainty.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate reimbursements from wages in a reconciliation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The distinction removes all pay duties</li><li>Their treatment may differ under the applicable terms and law</li><li>They always have identical legal effects</li><li>Reimbursements are never recorded</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Different payment categories may require different analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Classification and Reconciliation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of classification and reconciliation. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned labor and employment law research task: Prepare an employment-status memorandum and auditable pay reconciliation with two alternative factual assumptions and a document-request list. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an employment-status memorandum and auditable pay reconciliation with two alternative factual assumptions and a document-request list.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M18:Collective Bargaining, Representation and Labour Disputes": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 18</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: COLLECTIVE BARGAINING, REPRESENTATION AND LABOUR DISPUTES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Labor and Employment Law</td><td>Collective Bargaining, Representation and Labour Disputes</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain collective bargaining, representation and labour disputes using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Collective bargaining is negotiation through the applicable worker and employer representation framework. Recognition, bargaining authority, agreement coverage and dispute resolution are separate issues.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Representation</td><td>Authority to act for the relevant workers or employer</td></tr><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Persons and issues governed by an agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory protection</td><td>Requirement not displaced merely by private preference where the law makes it compulsory</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the organisation, represented workers, employer and relevant statutory provisions. Distinguish an individual grievance from a collective claim and ask who is authorised to negotiate or conclude terms. An agreement’s heading does not establish that it covers every worker or issue.</p><p>Create an issues schedule with current terms, proposed changes, legal minima and evidence supporting each position. Bargaining strategy should distinguish legal entitlement from negotiable preference. Research the competent dispute and review institutions rather than assuming a foreign industrial court structure. An advanced settlement analysis addresses implementation, monitoring and unresolved individual rights, while avoiding promises that collective agreement can waive every mandatory protection.</p><p>Prepare a coverage matrix listing the represented group, issue, agreement provision and any mandatory legal constraint. A collective settlement can improve certainty while leaving a disputed individual entitlement unresolved. Explain that relationship rather than assuming a broad release answers every claim. A practical implementation plan identifies responsible persons, dates and records, while the enforceability opinion remains tied to verified authority and the actual bargaining mandate.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: staff representatives sign a pay settlement, but some workers deny authorising the representatives and argue that statutory minimum entitlements were waived. Evaluate representation, coverage, mandatory rules and the available dispute route separately. A signed settlement is important evidence, not an automatic answer to every enforceability issue.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read relevant Decent Work Act provisions and current labour-institution materials. Verify applicability, worker status, pay obligations, collective rights and the competent dispute route.</p><p><a href=\"https://csa.gov.lr/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Decent-Work-Act-2015.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decent Work Act 2015 — Civil Service Agency</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Collective Bargaining, Representation and Labour Disputes”, start by separating representation from coverage. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Collective bargaining is negotiation through the applicable worker and employer representation framework. Recognition, bargaining authority, agreement coverage and dispute resolution are separate issues.</li><li>Separate representation from coverage.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Representation</td><td>Authority to act for the relevant workers or employer</td></tr><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Persons and issues governed by an agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory protection</td><td>Requirement not displaced merely by private preference where the law makes it compulsory</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Representation</summary><p>Authority to act for the relevant workers or employer</p></details><details><summary>Coverage</summary><p>Persons and issues governed by an agreement</p></details><details><summary>Mandatory protection</summary><p>Requirement not displaced merely by private preference where the law makes it compulsory</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Staff representatives sign terms. What should be verified?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Their authority, agreement coverage and applicable mandatory rules</li><li>Automatic coverage of every worker everywhere</li><li>Only the meeting location</li><li>That any signature waives every statute</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Collective arrangements operate within the governing framework.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish legal entitlement from bargaining preference?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Negotiation replaces the statute entirely</li><li>A negotiable improvement is not the same as a mandatory minimum</li><li>All demands are automatically legal rights</li><li>No minimum can exist</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> The distinction clarifies what may be negotiated and what must be complied with.</p></details><p><strong>An agreement’s coverage is disputed. Which record matters?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A foreign union’s rulebook alone</li><li>The negotiator’s confidence</li><li>The relevant recognition, authorisation and agreement scope materials</li><li>Only a promotional announcement</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Coverage requires evidence and applicable legal analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Representation and Coverage.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of representation and coverage. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned labor and employment law research task: Draft a collective-dispute issues schedule and 1,100-word settlement opinion addressing representation, coverage, implementation and remaining rights. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a collective-dispute issues schedule and 1,100-word settlement opinion addressing representation, coverage, implementation and remaining rights.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M19:Environmental Duties, Permits and Regulatory Accountability": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 19</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ENVIRONMENTAL DUTIES, PERMITS AND REGULATORY ACCOUNTABILITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Environmental Law</td><td>Environmental Duties, Permits and Regulatory Accountability</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain environmental duties, permits and regulatory accountability using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Environmental regulation can impose planning, permitting, monitoring and remedial obligations. A permit authorises a defined activity subject to its legal scope and conditions; it does not necessarily eliminate other duties or civil claims.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Permit scope</td><td>The activity and conditions covered by a particular authorisation</td></tr><tr><td>Compliance evidence</td><td>Records showing whether a legal requirement was met</td></tr><tr><td>Regulatory duty</td><td>Obligation imposed through the applicable public-law framework</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the activity, site, affected interests and relevant statutory trigger. Separate an application from an issued authorisation and examine conditions, duration and amendments. A general business registration does not establish environmental permission. Distinguish compliance evidence from the operator’s assurance that nothing harmful will occur.</p><p>Map the responsible institutions and available legal routes. Environmental harm may raise regulatory, property, public-law and evidential questions, each with a separate foundation. Assess prevention and remedy using actual data, not an assumed right to operate because investment has occurred. An advanced opinion evaluates uncertainty and the evidence needed for monitoring while recognising that the exact legal duties come from current local instruments.</p><p>Create separate columns for authorisation, compliance and causation. An operator may face an authorisation issue even before a particular loss is proved, while a civil claim about downstream damage needs its own causal and remedial analysis. Identify what each inspection, permit or sample can actually establish. Avoid treating either a permit or a complaint as conclusive proof of every issue; the evidential task depends on the legal proposition being advanced.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a waste-processing operator holds a business certificate but cannot produce an EPA authorisation. Residents report discoloured water downstream. The learner should verify permit requirements, obtain site and water records, and separate causation of the reported harm from the independent question of regulatory compliance.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Environmental Protection and Management Law and current EPA materials for the chosen activity. Verify permit, assessment, public-participation and enforcement requirements locally.</p><p><a href=\"https://epa.gov.lr/epa-documents/the-environmental-protection-and-management-law-of-liberia/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Environmental Protection and Management Law — EPA Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Environmental Duties, Permits and Regulatory Accountability”, start by separating permit scope from compliance evidence. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Environmental regulation can impose planning, permitting, monitoring and remedial obligations. A permit authorises a defined activity subject to its legal scope and conditions; it does not necessarily eliminate other duties or civil claims.</li><li>Separate permit scope from compliance evidence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Permit scope</td><td>The activity and conditions covered by a particular authorisation</td></tr><tr><td>Compliance evidence</td><td>Records showing whether a legal requirement was met</td></tr><tr><td>Regulatory duty</td><td>Obligation imposed through the applicable public-law framework</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Permit scope</summary><p>The activity and conditions covered by a particular authorisation</p></details><details><summary>Compliance evidence</summary><p>Records showing whether a legal requirement was met</p></details><details><summary>Regulatory duty</summary><p>Obligation imposed through the applicable public-law framework</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>An operator has business registration. What environmental issue remains?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the specific activity has the required environmental authority and compliance record</li><li>Automatic permission for every environmental impact</li><li>Proof that no harm can occur</li><li>A universal exemption from monitoring</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Different authorisations serve different legal purposes.</p></details><p><strong>Residents report discoloured water. What should be separated?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The permit from any conditions</li><li>Evidence of harm and causation from the operator’s regulatory compliance duties</li><li>Every complaint from legal relevance</li><li>Water testing from all factual inquiry</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> A compliance breach and a causal damages claim are not identical.</p></details><p><strong>Why read the actual permit conditions?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Conditions are only suggestions everywhere</li><li>A certificate title proves full compliance</li><li>Authorisation may be limited by activity, location, time and obligations</li><li>Permits always cover every future activity</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The instrument’s scope determines what it authorises.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Permit scope and Compliance evidence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of permit scope and compliance evidence. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned environmental law research task: Prepare an environmental compliance matrix and 1,000-word opinion distinguishing authorisation, monitoring, causation and possible remedies. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an environmental compliance matrix and 1,000-word opinion distinguishing authorisation, monitoring, causation and possible remedies.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M19:Environmental Impact Assessment and Meaningful Participation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 19</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT ASSESSMENT AND MEANINGFUL PARTICIPATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Environmental Law</td><td>Environmental Impact Assessment and Meaningful Participation</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain environmental impact assessment and meaningful participation using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Environmental impact assessment evaluates proposed effects through the applicable process. Public participation concerns informed involvement in that process; a meeting alone does not necessarily establish that the legally required steps were completed.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Baseline</td><td>Documented conditions against which effects are assessed</td></tr><tr><td>Mitigation</td><td>Measures intended to avoid, reduce or address relevant effects</td></tr><tr><td>Residual risk</td><td>Risk remaining after proposed controls</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the proposal version, baseline information, potential effects, alternatives and mitigation plan. Separate an assessment report from the decision authorising the project. A report may be technically detailed yet omit the interests most affected by a design change. Verify the local triggers and procedural requirements.</p><p>Evaluate participation through notice, accessible information, opportunity to respond and treatment of material concerns where the law requires them. Record how comments influence or fail to influence the decision. Mitigation should identify an action, responsible actor, monitoring measure and response if it fails. An advanced appraisal compares alternatives and residual risk rather than treating approval as proof that no harmful effects are possible.</p><p>Evaluate alternatives on comparable assumptions. State the activity, affected area, expected impacts, uncertainties and proposed controls for each option. A preferred design should not appear harmless merely because its risks were omitted from the table. Distinguish mitigation promised from mitigation implemented and monitored. A final decision can be examined for its legal and evidential basis without assuming that assessment alone proves all future operational compliance.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: an assessment describes a small recycling site, but the final design adds a discharge channel near a community water point. Residents attended an earlier meeting but never saw the change. The learner must examine whether the assessment and decision address the actual project and what current law requires for the revised proposal.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Environmental Protection and Management Law and current EPA materials for the chosen activity. Verify permit, assessment, public-participation and enforcement requirements locally.</p><p><a href=\"https://epa.gov.lr/epa-documents/the-environmental-protection-and-management-law-of-liberia/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Environmental Protection and Management Law — EPA Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Environmental Impact Assessment and Meaningful Participation”, start by separating baseline from mitigation. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Environmental impact assessment evaluates proposed effects through the applicable process. Public participation concerns informed involvement in that process; a meeting alone does not necessarily establish that the legally required steps were completed.</li><li>Separate baseline from mitigation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Baseline</td><td>Documented conditions against which effects are assessed</td></tr><tr><td>Mitigation</td><td>Measures intended to avoid, reduce or address relevant effects</td></tr><tr><td>Residual risk</td><td>Risk remaining after proposed controls</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Baseline</summary><p>Documented conditions against which effects are assessed</p></details><details><summary>Mitigation</summary><p>Measures intended to avoid, reduce or address relevant effects</p></details><details><summary>Residual risk</summary><p>Risk remaining after proposed controls</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>The final project differs materially from the assessed proposal. What must be examined?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the developer prefers the new version</li><li>Whether the applicable process and decision cover the actual changed project</li><li>Automatic approval of all later designs</li><li>Only the report’s page count</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Assessment must relate to the activity actually proposed or authorised.</p></details><p><strong>What makes mitigation more than a general promise?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only a reassuring photograph</li><li>A guarantee that no uncertainty exists</li><li>Defined action, responsibility, monitoring and response to failure</li><li>A statement that everyone will be careful</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Operational detail enables evaluation and accountability.</p></details><p><strong>Why examine how comments were treated?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>All comments must automatically prevail</li><li>Meetings alone always prove compliance</li><li>Only supportive comments are relevant</li><li>Participation can require more than attendance and may affect the decision process</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Meaningful participation depends on the applicable process and record.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Baseline and Mitigation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of baseline and mitigation. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned environmental law research task: Write a 1,200-word assessment critique with an alternatives table, participation record review and measurable mitigation-and-monitoring plan. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,200-word assessment critique with an alternatives table, participation record review and measurable mitigation-and-monitoring plan.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M20:Tax Statutory Interpretation and Temporal Application": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 20</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TAX STATUTORY INTERPRETATION AND TEMPORAL APPLICATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Taxation Law</td><td>Tax Statutory Interpretation and Temporal Application</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain tax statutory interpretation and temporal application using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A tax liability requires a legal basis connecting a taxable person, event, measure and period. Statutory interpretation identifies the rule actually enacted; administrative guidance assists but must be assessed against its legal status.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Taxable event</td><td>Event or transaction to which the charge legally attaches</td></tr><tr><td>Tax base</td><td>Measure to which the applicable calculation applies</td></tr><tr><td>Temporal application</td><td>Which version of the law governs the relevant period or event</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Break the provision into charge, scope, definitions, exemptions and calculation rules. Read cross-references and verify commencement and amendments for the relevant period. Do not apply today’s rate retrospectively merely because it appears first in a search result. A tax label on an invoice is not conclusive evidence that the particular transaction falls within the charge.</p><p>Distinguish ambiguity about law from missing facts about the transaction. For a disputed exemption, identify every condition and the evidence supporting it. Develop competing interpretations using the full statutory context, not a preferred commercial result. An advanced opinion states assumptions and separates legal analysis from arithmetic; a correct multiplication cannot cure use of the wrong taxable base or period.</p><p>Draft the tax analysis before calculating. Identify the charging provision, person, event, measure, period and any asserted exemption, with a source for each. If an amendment changes one component, explain its temporal operation rather than applying the newest number to the whole file. Present alternative calculations only where the underlying assumptions are genuinely disputed, and make clear which legal or factual inquiry would resolve the difference.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a training business invoices services spanning an amendment date and assumes the latest online rate applies to every payment. The learner must identify the relevant taxable event, transition provisions and factual periods. The course does not supply an unverified current rate; the research task is to establish and cite the controlling instrument.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Liberia Revenue Authority’s current tax laws and amendments. Verify the taxable event, person, period, rate, procedure and temporal effect before calculating or advising.</p><p><a href=\"https://revenue.lra.gov.lr/tax-laws-and-amendments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tax laws and amendments — Liberia Revenue Authority</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Tax Statutory Interpretation and Temporal Application”, start by separating taxable event from tax base. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A tax liability requires a legal basis connecting a taxable person, event, measure and period. Statutory interpretation identifies the rule actually enacted; administrative guidance assists but must be assessed against its legal status.</li><li>Separate taxable event from tax base.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Taxable event</td><td>Event or transaction to which the charge legally attaches</td></tr><tr><td>Tax base</td><td>Measure to which the applicable calculation applies</td></tr><tr><td>Temporal application</td><td>Which version of the law governs the relevant period or event</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Taxable event</summary><p>Event or transaction to which the charge legally attaches</p></details><details><summary>Tax base</summary><p>Measure to which the applicable calculation applies</p></details><details><summary>Temporal application</summary><p>Which version of the law governs the relevant period or event</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A new rate appears online after a transaction. What must be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the business prefers the lower amount</li><li>Commencement, transition and the relevant taxable event</li><li>Automatic retrospective use of the newest number</li><li>Only the invoice’s colour</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Temporal application determines the governing version.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate the legal tax base from arithmetic?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every receipt is taxed identically</li><li>Exemptions never need evidence</li><li>Accurate multiplication can still use the wrong legally taxable measure</li><li>Arithmetic creates the statutory charge</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The calculation depends on a correct legal classification.</p></details><p><strong>A business claims an exemption. What should the analysis identify?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only the word exempt in a brochure</li><li>A general belief that education is always untaxed</li><li>A foreign exemption as automatically binding</li><li>Each statutory condition and the supporting transaction evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> An exemption claim needs the applicable conditions and facts.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Taxable event and Tax base.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of taxable event and tax base. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned taxation law research task: Write a 1,100-word tax interpretation opinion and period-by-period calculation table using verified rates and explicit assumptions. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,100-word tax interpretation opinion and period-by-period calculation table using verified rates and explicit assumptions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M20:Assessments, Objections and Tax Dispute Records": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 20</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ASSESSMENTS, OBJECTIONS AND TAX DISPUTE RECORDS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Taxation Law</td><td>Assessments, Objections and Tax Dispute Records</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain assessments, objections and tax dispute records using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An assessment determines or asserts liability through the applicable tax process. An objection and an appeal are distinct review mechanisms whose availability, scope and timing must be verified in current local law.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assessment</td><td>Determination or assertion of liability under the governing process</td></tr><tr><td>Objection</td><td>Defined initial challenge where the local framework provides it</td></tr><tr><td>Issue schedule</td><td>Organised account of contested items, evidence and requested changes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Obtain the assessment, stated basis, service record, return, working papers and payment history. Identify each contested item and distinguish legal classification, factual disagreement and arithmetic error. A general claim that the amount is unfair does not isolate an issue capable of reasoned determination.</p><p>Prepare an objections schedule with proposition, evidence, legal basis and requested adjustment. Verify the competent route, timing, payment or suspension requirements and further review steps rather than copying a foreign revenue authority’s procedure. Maintain accurate records even while disputing liability. An advanced strategy evaluates collection risk and settlement possibilities within the applicable rules without promising that filing an objection automatically suspends every enforcement step.</p><p>Divide the objection file into undisputed facts, contested facts, legal grounds and arithmetic corrections. This helps the reader distinguish a duplicated payment from a disputed classification without conceding either inadvertently. Record the actual procedural steps and current status. A recommendation should explain which issue a proposed settlement or adjustment resolves and what remains outstanding, rather than promising that one successful ground eliminates every part of the assessment.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: an assessment includes a payment twice and classifies a disputed grant as trading income. The business wants to ignore the notice. Separate the duplication from the classification issue, preserve proof of service and payments, and research the current local objection route. A strong challenge remains specific and timely rather than relying on a broad accusation.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Liberia Revenue Authority’s current tax laws and amendments. Verify the taxable event, person, period, rate, procedure and temporal effect before calculating or advising.</p><p><a href=\"https://revenue.lra.gov.lr/tax-laws-and-amendments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tax laws and amendments — Liberia Revenue Authority</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Assessments, Objections and Tax Dispute Records”, start by separating assessment from objection. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An assessment determines or asserts liability through the applicable tax process. An objection and an appeal are distinct review mechanisms whose availability, scope and timing must be verified in current local law.</li><li>Separate assessment from objection.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assessment</td><td>Determination or assertion of liability under the governing process</td></tr><tr><td>Objection</td><td>Defined initial challenge where the local framework provides it</td></tr><tr><td>Issue schedule</td><td>Organised account of contested items, evidence and requested changes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Assessment</summary><p>Determination or assertion of liability under the governing process</p></details><details><summary>Objection</summary><p>Defined initial challenge where the local framework provides it</p></details><details><summary>Issue schedule</summary><p>Organised account of contested items, evidence and requested changes</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>An assessment duplicates a payment and disputes an exemption. What is the best structure?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Ignore the notice</li><li>Use only a general fairness argument</li><li>Separate the arithmetic issue from the legal classification issue</li><li>Call both issues the same error without explanation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Different grounds require different evidence and legal reasoning.</p></details><p><strong>Why preserve proof of service of the assessment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Service proves the tax is correct</li><li>A receipt automatically cancels liability</li><li>Dates never matter to tax procedure</li><li>The procedural chronology may affect available challenge steps</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Accurate timing supports the applicable review analysis.</p></details><p><strong>What must be verified before saying an objection stops collection?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The applicable local rules on payment, suspension and enforcement</li><li>A foreign video’s general description</li><li>The taxpayer’s preference</li><li>The mere existence of a complaint letter</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Review and collection effects are governed by the relevant procedure.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Assessment and Objection.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of assessment and objection. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned taxation law research task: Draft a fictional tax objection, evidence index and 1,000-word route-and-risk memorandum with locally verified procedural references. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional tax objection, evidence index and 1,000-word route-and-risk memorandum with locally verified procedural references.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M20:Employment, Environmental and Tax Compliance Opinion": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 20 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Employment, Environmental and Tax Compliance Opinion</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional training enterprise expands its facility, changes staff arrangements and receives a disputed tax assessment. Residents raise a water-access concern.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>An employment-status and pay reconciliation with auditable assumptions</li><li>An environmental authorisation and participation matrix tied to the actual revised design</li><li>A 1,600-word integrated opinion with a tax objection schedule, lawful options and source-based procedural questions</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M21:Trust Creation, Certainty and Beneficial Interests": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 21</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TRUST CREATION, CERTAINTY AND BENEFICIAL INTERESTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equity and Trusts</td><td>Trust Creation, Certainty and Beneficial Interests</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain trust creation, certainty and beneficial interests using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A trust separates administration or legal holding from beneficial interests under a legally recognised framework. A gift, personal promise and trust are not interchangeable. Comparative certainty requirements help frame research but must be tested against applicable local authority.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Settlor</td><td>Person creating or purporting to create the relevant trust arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Beneficial interest</td><td>Entitlement held for a beneficiary under a recognised arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Certainty</td><td>Sufficient legal clarity about the arrangement’s relevant components</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the alleged settlor, trustee, property, beneficiaries or purpose and words or conduct said to create the arrangement. Ask whether the property and obligations are sufficiently identifiable under the governing law. An expression of hope may differ from an enforceable obligation. Do not assume that using the word trust proves valid creation or that omitting it prevents every recognised arrangement.</p><p>Distinguish creation from transfer or constitution of the relevant assets. A person may intend an arrangement but fail to complete a required legal step. Examine capacity, formality and any purpose restriction through local sources. An advanced analysis presents alternative classifications and their consequences: outright gift, contractual obligation, ineffective disposition or a valid trust may lead to different rights and remedies.</p><p>Compare the intended obligation with the practical control of the assets. Who may use the money, for what purpose, and who can require an account? These questions help distinguish a personal wish from an enforceable arrangement without deciding the local legal result by intuition. Identify any formal transfer still required and explain what changes under the alternative gift, contract or trust classification. The comparative terminology must remain anchored to verified local authority.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a donor tells a relative to “keep some of my shop money for the children” but does not identify the fund, children or control arrangements. The learner should examine intention, identifiable property and beneficiaries, then research applicable local requirements. It is premature either to declare a perfect trust or to assume the donor intended an unconditional gift.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use applicable Liberian estate, property and fiduciary authorities. The three-certainties framework and comparative fiduciary lectures are research models; verify local reception and application rather than treating English or Australian doctrine as automatically binding.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=45:decedents-estates-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decedents Estates Law, Title 8 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.fedcourt.gov.au/digital-law-library/judges-speeches/justice-osullivan/osullivan-j-20260221\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Fiduciary duties — Federal Court of Australia judicial lecture (comparative)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Trust Creation, Certainty and Beneficial Interests”, start by separating settlor from beneficial interest. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A trust separates administration or legal holding from beneficial interests under a legally recognised framework. A gift, personal promise and trust are not interchangeable. Comparative certainty requirements help frame research but must be tested against applicable local authority.</li><li>Separate settlor from beneficial interest.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Settlor</td><td>Person creating or purporting to create the relevant trust arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Beneficial interest</td><td>Entitlement held for a beneficiary under a recognised arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Certainty</td><td>Sufficient legal clarity about the arrangement’s relevant components</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Settlor</summary><p>Person creating or purporting to create the relevant trust arrangement</p></details><details><summary>Beneficial interest</summary><p>Entitlement held for a beneficiary under a recognised arrangement</p></details><details><summary>Certainty</summary><p>Sufficient legal clarity about the arrangement’s relevant components</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A donor uses the word trust. What should be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic ownership by the recipient</li><li>Only the donor’s handwriting</li><li>The actual intention, property, beneficiaries and applicable creation requirements</li><li>Automatic validity from the label</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> A label cannot replace the legal requirements of the arrangement.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish intention from constitution of assets?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Intention always transfers every asset</li><li>Assets are irrelevant to trusts</li><li>Constitution means only the national Constitution</li><li>An intended arrangement may still lack a required transfer or formal step</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Creation and effective disposition may involve separate requirements.</p></details><p><strong>A comparative lecture discusses three certainties. How should it be used?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>As an analytical framework whose Liberian applicability must be researched</li><li>As an automatic local statute</li><li>As proof that all foreign cases bind Liberia</li><li>As a replacement for identifying the assets</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Comparative doctrines require a reasoned local authority check.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Settlor and Beneficial interest.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of settlor and beneficial interest. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned equity and trusts research task: Write a 1,200-word trust-classification opinion and draft a clearer fictional declaration, marking every local formality or rule requiring verification. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,200-word trust-classification opinion and draft a clearer fictional declaration, marking every local formality or rule requiring verification.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M21:Fiduciary Administration, Accounts and Beneficiary Protection": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 21</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: FIDUCIARY ADMINISTRATION, ACCOUNTS AND BENEFICIARY PROTECTION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equity and Trusts</td><td>Fiduciary Administration, Accounts and Beneficiary Protection</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain fiduciary administration, accounts and beneficiary protection using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Fiduciary administration concerns entrusted powers and the obligations attached to their exercise. Loyalty, care, accounting and remedies must be distinguished rather than treated as one undifferentiated fairness duty.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fiduciary power</td><td>Entrusted authority whose exercise is constrained by applicable duties</td></tr><tr><td>Account</td><td>Supported explanation of assets, transactions and balances</td></tr><tr><td>Unauthorised gain</td><td>Benefit whose recovery requires the governing duty and remedial analysis</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the source and scope of the office or entrusted function. Examine the instrument, relevant law, assets and permitted purposes. A power to invest is not necessarily a power to make an undisclosed loan to the administrator’s own business. Conversely, investment loss does not by itself establish disloyalty or every form of fault.</p><p>Reconcile opening assets, receipts, payments, investments and closing balances using actual records. Separate a loss claim from recovery of an unauthorised gain and research the local remedial basis for each. Preserve third-party and insolvency questions where relevant. An advanced protection plan considers accounts, disclosure, replacement or other competent relief without assuming beneficiaries may simply seize assets or that consent from one person binds everyone.</p><p>Use a transaction ledger to connect duty and remedy. For each disputed transfer, identify the entrusted power, authorisation, recipient, alleged gain or loss and available tracing evidence. A positive investment return does not by itself answer a loyalty issue, while a loss does not prove every pleaded breach. The proposed remedy should specify what it restores or recovers and avoid unexplained duplication between accounting, compensation and gain-based relief.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a fund administrator lends money to their own business, omits the loan from accounts and later reports an investment loss. The analysis must investigate authority, disclosure, loyalty, asset tracing and the accuracy of the accounts separately. A successful investment return would not necessarily cure an undisclosed conflict, while a loss alone does not prove every alleged breach.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use applicable Liberian estate, property and fiduciary authorities. The three-certainties framework and comparative fiduciary lectures are research models; verify local reception and application rather than treating English or Australian doctrine as automatically binding.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=45:decedents-estates-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decedents Estates Law, Title 8 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.fedcourt.gov.au/digital-law-library/judges-speeches/justice-osullivan/osullivan-j-20260221\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Fiduciary duties — Federal Court of Australia judicial lecture (comparative)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Fiduciary Administration, Accounts and Beneficiary Protection”, start by separating fiduciary power from account. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Fiduciary administration concerns entrusted powers and the obligations attached to their exercise. Loyalty, care, accounting and remedies must be distinguished rather than treated as one undifferentiated fairness duty.</li><li>Separate fiduciary power from account.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fiduciary power</td><td>Entrusted authority whose exercise is constrained by applicable duties</td></tr><tr><td>Account</td><td>Supported explanation of assets, transactions and balances</td></tr><tr><td>Unauthorised gain</td><td>Benefit whose recovery requires the governing duty and remedial analysis</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Fiduciary power</summary><p>Entrusted authority whose exercise is constrained by applicable duties</p></details><details><summary>Account</summary><p>Supported explanation of assets, transactions and balances</p></details><details><summary>Unauthorised gain</summary><p>Benefit whose recovery requires the governing duty and remedial analysis</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>An administrator profits from an undisclosed self-loan. What should be analysed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only whether the final return was positive</li><li>Automatic permission from possession of funds</li><li>A universal right to seize all assets</li><li>Authority, conflict, accounting and the available local remedies</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Different duty and remedy questions require separate foundations.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish an investment loss from disloyalty?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A poor outcome does not by itself establish every fiduciary breach</li><li>Losses are always irrelevant</li><li>Loyalty means guaranteed profits</li><li>No accounts are needed if investments fail</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The nature of the duty and conduct must be established.</p></details><p><strong>What makes a fund reconciliation auditable?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A statement that records are private</li><li>Opening assets, sourced transactions and explained closing balances</li><li>Only an asserted final number</li><li>Deletion of disputed transfers</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Transparent records allow the relevant transactions to be tested.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Fiduciary power and Account.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of fiduciary power and account. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned equity and trusts research task: Prepare a fund reconciliation and 1,200-word beneficiary-protection opinion distinguishing duty, loss, gain and alternative relief. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fund reconciliation and 1,200-word beneficiary-protection opinion distinguishing duty, loss, gain and alternative relief.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M22:Natural Law, Moral Critique and Legal Obligation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 22</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: NATURAL LAW, MORAL CRITIQUE AND LEGAL OBLIGATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisprudence</td><td>Natural Law, Moral Critique and Legal Obligation</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain natural law, moral critique and legal obligation using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Natural-law theories connect law and practical or moral reasoning in differing ways. A moral criticism of a rule, a claim about its legal validity and a claim about a person’s obligation to obey it are distinct propositions.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Moral critique</td><td>Evaluation of a rule against an ethical standard</td></tr><tr><td>Legal validity</td><td>Status as law under the relevant account or legal system</td></tr><tr><td>Normative argument</td><td>Reasoning about what ought to be done or recognised</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>State the theory fairly before applying it. Avoid the caricature that every natural-law account treats personal dislike as proof of invalidity. Identify the moral criterion being proposed and explain how it relates to legal sources and institutional authority. Distinguish the theorist’s description of law from their prescription for reform.</p><p>Test the theory against a hard case where legality and justice appear to diverge. Consider predictability, equal treatment, official power and the risks of unchecked personal moral judgment. An advanced essay defends an argument while confronting the strongest objection. It should not present philosophy as permission to disregard court orders or practise law without authority; practical legal consequences still require the relevant local sources.</p><p>Steelman the rival account before responding. A theory that emphasises legal sources may still condemn injustice, while a theory connecting law and morality may still value institutional certainty. Explain the point of disagreement precisely: criteria of validity, reasons for obedience or standards for reform. The fictional exclusion problem becomes analytically useful when the essay shows which question each theory answers and where a further institutional argument is needed.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a formally issued rule excludes a community from a public service. A learner argues that injustice means no one needs to investigate its legal status. A stronger essay separates moral critique, constitutional validity and lawful challenge, then explains what a natural-law account contributes and what institutional questions remain.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Liberian Constitution, a published judgment and a documented account of the relevant customary framework. The philosophical readings and videos are analytical perspectives, not substitutes for local legal authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Natural Law, Moral Critique and Legal Obligation”, start by separating moral critique from legal validity. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Natural-law theories connect law and practical or moral reasoning in differing ways. A moral criticism of a rule, a claim about its legal validity and a claim about a person’s obligation to obey it are distinct propositions.</li><li>Separate moral critique from legal validity.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Moral critique</td><td>Evaluation of a rule against an ethical standard</td></tr><tr><td>Legal validity</td><td>Status as law under the relevant account or legal system</td></tr><tr><td>Normative argument</td><td>Reasoning about what ought to be done or recognised</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Moral critique</summary><p>Evaluation of a rule against an ethical standard</p></details><details><summary>Legal validity</summary><p>Status as law under the relevant account or legal system</p></details><details><summary>Normative argument</summary><p>Reasoning about what ought to be done or recognised</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A rule is morally criticised. What should not be assumed without further argument?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That ethical analysis may matter</li><li>That constitutional review may be relevant</li><li>That the theory should be explained</li><li>That moral criticism alone resolves its current legal status and procedural consequences</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Validity, morality and lawful response are distinct questions.</p></details><p><strong>What makes a jurisprudence essay critical rather than descriptive?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A defended thesis engaging with a strong objection</li><li>A list of theorists only</li><li>An assumption that all theories agree</li><li>A conclusion based on popularity</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Critical work evaluates reasons and competing explanations.</p></details><p><strong>Why avoid treating personal dislike as a complete natural-law theory?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Legal sources are always irrelevant</li><li>The philosophical claim requires an articulated account of moral and legal reasoning</li><li>Moral standards never matter</li><li>All natural-law theories are identical slogans</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> A theory should be stated accurately before evaluation.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Moral critique and Legal validity.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of moral critique and legal validity. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned jurisprudence research task: Write a 1,300-word critical essay distinguishing validity, obligation and justice in the fictional exclusion case, with a developed objection and reply. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,300-word critical essay distinguishing validity, obligation and justice in the fictional exclusion case, with a developed objection and reply.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M22:Legal Positivism, Pluralism and Customary Authority": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 22</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LEGAL POSITIVISM, PLURALISM AND CUSTOMARY AUTHORITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisprudence</td><td>Legal Positivism, Pluralism and Customary Authority</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain legal positivism, pluralism and customary authority using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Legal positivism examines law through socially recognised sources and criteria, without necessarily endorsing every valid rule morally. Legal pluralism studies interacting normative orders; social practice and legally recognised customary authority must not be automatically equated.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Positivism</td><td>Family of accounts connecting legal status to recognised social sources or criteria</td></tr><tr><td>Pluralism</td><td>Interaction of more than one normative order</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition</td><td>Basis on which a claimed practice or authority receives legal effect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the proposed criterion of legal recognition and distinguish it from a claim about justice. A positivist can condemn a law while describing its status within a system. Conversely, an institution’s assertion of authority does not prove that the relevant legal criteria are satisfied.</p><p>In a plural setting, map statutory, constitutional, customary and practical decision-making claims. Ask who participates, whose interests are affected and how conflicts are resolved under the governing framework. Do not describe a community as having one timeless, uncontested custom without evidence. An advanced analysis addresses change, internal disagreement and constitutional limits, while distinguishing an empirical account of practice from a doctrinal conclusion about enforceability.</p><p>A plural-authority map should include relationships rather than merely names. Show how a claimed customary rule interacts with statutory recognition, constitutional limits and actual local decision-making. Record internal disagreement and the evidence supporting each account. Distinguish the researcher’s description of a practice from a legal claim that it is enforceable. This prevents both romanticising custom as uncontested and dismissing it simply because it differs from an urban documentary model.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a community council asserts an exclusionary land custom, while some residents dispute its history and constitutional consistency. The learner must investigate both the evidence of practice and its legal recognition. Calling something customary does not settle the authority, participation or constitutional questions, and describing the practice does not mean endorsing it.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Liberian Constitution, a published judgment and a documented account of the relevant customary framework. The philosophical readings and videos are analytical perspectives, not substitutes for local legal authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Legal Positivism, Pluralism and Customary Authority”, start by separating positivism from pluralism. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Legal positivism examines law through socially recognised sources and criteria, without necessarily endorsing every valid rule morally. Legal pluralism studies interacting normative orders; social practice and legally recognised customary authority must not be automatically equated.</li><li>Separate positivism from pluralism.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Positivism</td><td>Family of accounts connecting legal status to recognised social sources or criteria</td></tr><tr><td>Pluralism</td><td>Interaction of more than one normative order</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition</td><td>Basis on which a claimed practice or authority receives legal effect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Positivism</summary><p>Family of accounts connecting legal status to recognised social sources or criteria</p></details><details><summary>Pluralism</summary><p>Interaction of more than one normative order</p></details><details><summary>Recognition</summary><p>Basis on which a claimed practice or authority receives legal effect</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A positivist describes a rule as valid law. What does that not necessarily imply?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Moral approval of the rule</li><li>Recognition that sources matter</li><li>A need to explain the criteria used</li><li>Possible criticism of the rule</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Descriptive legal status and ethical endorsement are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>A council calls a rule customary. What should be investigated?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only the council’s preferred conclusion</li><li>Evidence of practice, internal disagreement, legal recognition and constitutional limits</li><li>Automatic universal acceptance</li><li>The assumption that customs never change</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Customary authority requires careful factual and legal analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish empirical pluralism from enforceability?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every observed norm is binding statute</li><li>Courts cannot consider local facts</li><li>The existence of a social practice does not itself establish its legal effect</li><li>Social practice can never be relevant</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Practice and legal recognition need different supporting arguments.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Positivism and Pluralism.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of positivism and pluralism. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned jurisprudence research task: Prepare a plural-authority map and 1,300-word essay comparing positivist and pluralist explanations, with an explicit evidence-and-validity distinction. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a plural-authority map and 1,300-word essay comparing positivist and pluralist explanations, with an explicit evidence-and-validity distinction.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M23:Banking Regulation, Capital, Liquidity and Supervisory Power": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 23</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: BANKING REGULATION, CAPITAL, LIQUIDITY AND SUPERVISORY POWER</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Banking and Financial Law</td><td>Banking Regulation, Capital, Liquidity and Supervisory Power</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain banking regulation, capital, liquidity and supervisory power using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Capital absorbs losses; liquidity concerns ability to meet obligations when due. Prudential regulation addresses financial soundness through legally authorised requirements and supervision. International standards are not automatically self-executing domestic law.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>Resources measured under the applicable framework for absorbing losses</td></tr><tr><td>Liquidity</td><td>Capacity to meet relevant payment obligations as they fall due</td></tr><tr><td>Supervision</td><td>Oversight exercised under legally conferred regulatory powers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the institution and regulated activity before choosing the governing instrument. A bank, payment provider and non-bank lender may have different authorisation and supervisory requirements. Use the CBL’s current repository, checking the 2025 legislation’s operative and transitional provisions and later directives. Do not assume the old financial institutions framework remains the only controlling source.</p><p>Separate economic explanation from legal obligation. Maturity transformation can create liquidity risk even where assets exceed liabilities; a capital requirement serves a different function from a stock of immediately available funds. The comparative Yale lecture illustrates concepts and historical foreign arrangements, not current Liberian ratios or deposit guarantees. An advanced compliance opinion identifies the actual legal source, relevant metric, reporting obligation and competent supervisory response without inventing numerical thresholds.</p><p>Build an instrument-status table before preparing a compliance opinion. Record the regulated activity, governing Act, commencement or transition question and relevant current regulation or directive. Explain the legal effect of the source rather than treating every international benchmark as a local obligation. A bank’s economic risk profile can guide the inquiry, but the claimed regulatory breach and supervisory response still require an operative legal rule and supporting institutional facts.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a lender has profitable long-term loans but cannot meet immediate withdrawals and claims profitability proves compliance. Another adviser imports an old foreign capital ratio. The learner must distinguish liquidity from solvency, identify the regulated entity and verify current CBL requirements and powers before recommending any legal response.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the CBL regulatory framework, including the Bank-Financial Institutions and Bank-Financial Holding Companies Act of 2025 listed in 2026. Check commencement, transitional provisions and current directives. Use the Collateral Registry and applicable commercial law for security.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.cbl.org.lr/publications/document-type/regulatory-framework-all-acts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Current financial institutions legislation — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.cbl.org.lr/publications/document-type/regulations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Current regulations — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.registry.cbl.org.lr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Collateral Registry — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://oyc.yale.edu/economics/econ-252-11/lecture-13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Banks — Yale conceptual lecture (2011; not current Liberian rules)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Banking Regulation, Capital, Liquidity and Supervisory Power”, start by separating capital from liquidity. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Capital absorbs losses; liquidity concerns ability to meet obligations when due. Prudential regulation addresses financial soundness through legally authorised requirements and supervision. International standards are not automatically self-executing domestic law.</li><li>Separate capital from liquidity.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>Resources measured under the applicable framework for absorbing losses</td></tr><tr><td>Liquidity</td><td>Capacity to meet relevant payment obligations as they fall due</td></tr><tr><td>Supervision</td><td>Oversight exercised under legally conferred regulatory powers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Capital</summary><p>Resources measured under the applicable framework for absorbing losses</p></details><details><summary>Liquidity</summary><p>Capacity to meet relevant payment obligations as they fall due</p></details><details><summary>Supervision</summary><p>Oversight exercised under legally conferred regulatory powers</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A lender has valuable long-term assets but cannot meet withdrawals. What distinction matters?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Liquidity versus solvency or capital adequacy</li><li>Profitability automatically proves immediate cash availability</li><li>All financial risks are identical</li><li>A foreign ratio resolves every local issue</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Timing of obligations and loss-absorbing capacity are different concerns.</p></details><p><strong>Why check the current CBL repository and transition provisions?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A website title alone settles all temporal questions</li><li>The applicable framework may include new legislation and later directives</li><li>The oldest available summary always governs</li><li>International recommendations automatically repeal local Acts</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Legal compliance requires the operative local instruments.</p></details><p><strong>A comparative lecture describes foreign deposit insurance. What should the learner avoid?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Checking local sources</li><li>Distinguishing theory from legal obligation</li><li>Assuming the same guarantee and limits apply in Liberia</li><li>Using it to understand the concept</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Comparative examples do not establish local guarantees.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Capital and Liquidity.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of capital and liquidity. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned banking and financial law research task: Write a 1,200-word prudential-law memorandum with an instrument-status table, capital-versus-liquidity explanation and verified compliance inquiries. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,200-word prudential-law memorandum with an instrument-status table, capital-versus-liquidity explanation and verified compliance inquiries.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M23:Secured Credit, Priority and Enforcement Risk": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 23</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: SECURED CREDIT, PRIORITY AND ENFORCEMENT RISK</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Banking and Financial Law</td><td>Secured Credit, Priority and Enforcement Risk</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain secured credit, priority and enforcement risk using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A security interest links an obligation to identified collateral under the governing framework. Creation, third-party effectiveness, priority and enforcement are separate stages; registration is not a substitute for the underlying legal requirements.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collateral</td><td>Identified property supporting an obligation under the applicable arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Priority</td><td>Ranking of competing claims under the governing rules</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement</td><td>Legally available steps following the relevant default or trigger</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify debtor, creditor, obligation, collateral and authority to grant security. Read the agreement and verify the relevant registry information. A notice may identify an asserted interest without proving every contractual fact. Distinguish movable collateral from land-security regimes and check the applicable law for each.</p><p>Build a chronology of competing interests and relevant acts. Priority cannot be inferred solely from which creditor first demanded payment. Analyse default and the lawful enforcement route separately, including restrictions and affected third parties. An advanced lending opinion considers valuation, asset identification and insolvency interaction without promising guaranteed recovery. Preserve the distinction between a valid claim and practically realisable collateral.</p><p>A security opinion should track separate gates: the debtor’s rights, creation of the interest, effectiveness against others, ranking and lawful realisation. Passing one gate does not establish the next. Identify the fact or document relevant to each and explain what happens if the claimed asset belongs to a third party. The recommendation should distinguish legal priority from valuation and recoverability rather than using a registry search as a guarantee of payment.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: two lenders claim the same training-centre computers. One has an earlier agreement; the other points to a registry notice, and some computers were leased from a third party. Examine creation, effectiveness, priority and actual asset ownership under the local framework. Neither possession nor a registry screenshot resolves every issue.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the CBL regulatory framework, including the Bank-Financial Institutions and Bank-Financial Holding Companies Act of 2025 listed in 2026. Check commencement, transitional provisions and current directives. Use the Collateral Registry and applicable commercial law for security.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.cbl.org.lr/publications/document-type/regulatory-framework-all-acts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Current financial institutions legislation — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.cbl.org.lr/publications/document-type/regulations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Current regulations — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.registry.cbl.org.lr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Collateral Registry — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://oyc.yale.edu/economics/econ-252-11/lecture-13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Banks — Yale conceptual lecture (2011; not current Liberian rules)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Secured Credit, Priority and Enforcement Risk”, start by separating collateral from priority. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A security interest links an obligation to identified collateral under the governing framework. Creation, third-party effectiveness, priority and enforcement are separate stages; registration is not a substitute for the underlying legal requirements.</li><li>Separate collateral from priority.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collateral</td><td>Identified property supporting an obligation under the applicable arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Priority</td><td>Ranking of competing claims under the governing rules</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement</td><td>Legally available steps following the relevant default or trigger</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Collateral</summary><p>Identified property supporting an obligation under the applicable arrangement</p></details><details><summary>Priority</summary><p>Ranking of competing claims under the governing rules</p></details><details><summary>Enforcement</summary><p>Legally available steps following the relevant default or trigger</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A registry notice is found. What remains to be established?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A guarantee that the debt is valid</li><li>The underlying interest, asset description and applicable priority requirements</li><li>Automatic ownership of every debtor asset</li><li>Unlimited self-help enforcement</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Registration is one part of a broader legal analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Some computers are leased from a third party. Why does that matter?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Leases remove every creditor right</li><li>Only the computer brand matters</li><li>The debtor may not own or be authorised to encumber the assumed interest</li><li>Every possessed asset is automatically owned</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Collateral analysis begins with the rights actually available.</p></details><p><strong>Two creditors claim priority. What is needed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The order in which they shouted for payment</li><li>Automatic preference for the larger lender</li><li>A foreign rule without local verification</li><li>A sourced analysis of competing interests and relevant chronology</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Priority follows the applicable rules and legally relevant acts.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Collateral and Priority.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of collateral and priority. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned banking and financial law research task: Produce a secured-credit due-diligence memorandum with an asset inventory, competing-interests timeline and conditional enforcement plan. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Produce a secured-credit due-diligence memorandum with an asset inventory, competing-interests timeline and conditional enforcement plan.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M24:Copyright, Authorship and Digital Reuse": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 24</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: COPYRIGHT, AUTHORSHIP AND DIGITAL REUSE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intellectual Property Law</td><td>Copyright, Authorship and Digital Reuse</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain copyright, authorship and digital reuse using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Copyright concerns legally protected expression and relevant rights under the applicable framework. It differs from ownership of a physical copy, an idea and permission to use a work for a particular purpose.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expression</td><td>Form of a work distinguished from its underlying idea or facts where applicable</td></tr><tr><td>Licence</td><td>Permission within defined conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Attribution</td><td>Identification of authorship, distinct from automatic permission</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the work, authorship or ownership basis, relevant act and territory. A person who possesses an image file does not necessarily own the copyright. Employment, commissioning and licensing questions require the actual agreement and local law. Separate protected expression from unprotected ideas or facts where the framework makes that distinction.</p><p>Analyse permission by scope: medium, purpose, duration, territory and conditions. A credit line may be required but does not automatically grant a licence. Investigate any claimed exception in the applicable Act rather than assuming that educational or online use is always free. An advanced opinion assesses both infringement and lawful alternatives, including original creation, properly licensed material and a narrower use supported by an actual exception.</p><p>A digital-use audit should record the work, source, owner or licensor, intended act and permission evidence. Analyse each use separately: viewing, reproducing, adapting and advertising can raise different questions under the relevant framework. A general educational purpose is not a complete exception analysis. If the proposed use lacks a verified basis, identify lawful alternatives and their practical effect instead of assuming that attribution or low revenue cures the missing permission.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a training business downloads a photographer’s image, adds attribution and uses it in paid advertising. It argues that anything online is free for education. The learner should identify the relevant use, rights holder, licence evidence and applicable exceptions. Attribution alone does not answer the permission question.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Liberia Intellectual Property Act 2016 through WIPO Lex and check current amendments and administrative materials. Distinguish local rights from foreign registration and comparative examples.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.wipo.int/wipolex/en/legislation/details/16994\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberia Intellectual Property Act 2016 — WIPO Lex (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Copyright, Authorship and Digital Reuse”, start by separating expression from licence. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Copyright concerns legally protected expression and relevant rights under the applicable framework. It differs from ownership of a physical copy, an idea and permission to use a work for a particular purpose.</li><li>Separate expression from licence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expression</td><td>Form of a work distinguished from its underlying idea or facts where applicable</td></tr><tr><td>Licence</td><td>Permission within defined conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Attribution</td><td>Identification of authorship, distinct from automatic permission</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Expression</summary><p>Form of a work distinguished from its underlying idea or facts where applicable</p></details><details><summary>Licence</summary><p>Permission within defined conditions</p></details><details><summary>Attribution</summary><p>Identification of authorship, distinct from automatic permission</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>An image is publicly viewable online. What cannot be assumed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That a licence may exist</li><li>Unrestricted permission to reuse it in advertising</li><li>That it can be viewed</li><li>That its source should be recorded</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Public accessibility and permission are different matters.</p></details><p><strong>A user credits the photographer. What issue remains?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether attribution contains enough emojis</li><li>The elimination of every ownership question</li><li>Whether the use is licensed or falls within an applicable legal exception</li><li>Automatic transfer of all copyright</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Credit does not itself establish permission.</p></details><p><strong>Why read the commissioning agreement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Commissioning always transfers every right everywhere</li><li>A physical copy determines all rights</li><li>Agreements can never matter to copyright</li><li>Ownership and permitted uses may depend on its terms and applicable law</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The actual arrangement must be examined.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Expression and Licence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of expression and licence. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned intellectual property law research task: Write a 1,100-word copyright-use opinion and a licence checklist for a fictional educational media project. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,100-word copyright-use opinion and a licence checklist for a fictional educational media project.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M24:Trademarks, Patents and Commercial IP Strategy": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 24</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TRADEMARKS, PATENTS AND COMMERCIAL IP STRATEGY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intellectual Property Law</td><td>Trademarks, Patents and Commercial IP Strategy</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain trademarks, patents and commercial ip strategy using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Trademarks identify commercial origin under the applicable framework; patents concern qualifying inventions through a defined system. Copyright, trademark and patent protection have different objects, requirements and territorial consequences.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trademark</td><td>Sign whose legal protection relates to relevant commercial origin and statutory requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Patent</td><td>Right granted for an invention meeting the applicable requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Territorial scope</td><td>Geographical reach of the particular legal protection</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Classify the proposed asset before recommending a filing. A business name, logo, technical invention and training manual may invoke different rights. Search the relevant official records and investigate prior rights rather than treating an internet domain as proof of trademark ownership. A patent application is not the same as a granted enforceable patent.</p><p>For each proposed right, identify owner, territory, status, scope, duration and any licence or competing claim. A foreign registration does not automatically prove a Liberian entitlement. Consider confidentiality and disclosure before discussing an invention publicly, subject to the applicable patent rules. An advanced strategy balances legal availability, business objective, evidence and cost without guaranteeing that registration prevents all disputes.</p><p>Prioritise protection by asset and commercial objective. A brand strategy concerns distinguishing origin; an invention strategy concerns qualifying technical subject matter and disclosure; a content licence concerns permitted use of expression. One filing does not answer all three. Record territorial status and competing rights, and distinguish an application, registration and enforceable claim. A useful strategy explains what each proposed step can achieve and what uncertainty remains.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a startup registers a domain and assumes it owns a similar existing brand; it also posts a technical invention online before seeking advice. Separate the trademark conflict, domain position and patent-disclosure questions. Investigate the local law and records before promising exclusive rights.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Liberia Intellectual Property Act 2016 through WIPO Lex and check current amendments and administrative materials. Distinguish local rights from foreign registration and comparative examples.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.wipo.int/wipolex/en/legislation/details/16994\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberia Intellectual Property Act 2016 — WIPO Lex (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Trademarks, Patents and Commercial IP Strategy”, start by separating trademark from patent. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Trademarks identify commercial origin under the applicable framework; patents concern qualifying inventions through a defined system. Copyright, trademark and patent protection have different objects, requirements and territorial consequences.</li><li>Separate trademark from patent.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trademark</td><td>Sign whose legal protection relates to relevant commercial origin and statutory requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Patent</td><td>Right granted for an invention meeting the applicable requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Territorial scope</td><td>Geographical reach of the particular legal protection</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Trademark</summary><p>Sign whose legal protection relates to relevant commercial origin and statutory requirements</p></details><details><summary>Patent</summary><p>Right granted for an invention meeting the applicable requirements</p></details><details><summary>Territorial scope</summary><p>Geographical reach of the particular legal protection</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A startup owns a domain. What remains to be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Patentability of the domain name alone</li><li>The absence of all competing rights</li><li>Trademark availability, prior rights and the applicable local registration framework</li><li>Automatic ownership of every similar brand</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> A domain registration and trademark entitlement are different.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish a patent application from a grant?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every application guarantees exclusivity</li><li>The distinction only affects document colour</li><li>A grant removes territorial limits everywhere</li><li>Filing status does not necessarily establish a granted enforceable right</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Rights depend on the applicable status and scope.</p></details><p><strong>A foreign trademark registration is produced. What should local advice analyse?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its relevance and the basis for protection in the applicable territory</li><li>Automatic worldwide exclusivity</li><li>No possible relevance in any case</li><li>Only the registration’s visual design</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Territorial effect must be established.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Trademark and Patent.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of trademark and patent. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned intellectual property law research task: Prepare an IP asset-and-status register and 1,200-word protection strategy distinguishing brand, invention, content and unresolved prior rights. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an IP asset-and-status register and 1,200-word protection strategy distinguishing brand, invention, content and unresolved prior rights.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M24:Fiduciary Finance and Intellectual Property Case Study": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 24 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Fiduciary Finance and Intellectual Property Case Study</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional education fund finances a startup and licenses course media. Its administrator has an undisclosed business interest and a lender claims the startup’s equipment.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A trust-classification and fund-reconciliation report distinguishing authority, loss and gain</li><li>A current CBL instrument-status table and collateral-priority timeline identifying third-party property</li><li>A 1,700-word fiduciary-and-IP opinion with a rights-and-licences register and a reasoned counterargument</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M25:Negotiation, Mediation and Settlement Authority": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 25</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: NEGOTIATION, MEDIATION AND SETTLEMENT AUTHORITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Alternative Dispute Resolution</td><td>Negotiation, Mediation and Settlement Authority</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain negotiation, mediation and settlement authority using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Negotiation seeks agreement through party interaction. Mediation uses a neutral facilitator without necessarily conferring power to impose a decision. Settlement authority determines who may commit the relevant party.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interest</td><td>Underlying concern distinct from the stated legal position</td></tr><tr><td>Settlement authority</td><td>Power to commit a party to the relevant agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Release</td><td>Term discharging specified claims within its lawful scope</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Separate legal entitlement, underlying interests and practical options. A settlement range should be informed by evidence, legal risk, costs and alternatives, not a fabricated prediction of certain victory. Identify who can negotiate and who must approve the final terms. A mediator’s suggestion is not automatically a binding award.</p><p>Draft settlement terms identifying parties, obligations, timing, releases, confidentiality and a lawful response to nonperformance. Clarify which claims are resolved and which remain. Consider power imbalances and informed participation without assuming that agreement alone proves every legal requirement was met. An advanced analysis also addresses enforceability and implementation: a carefully negotiated promise still needs a competent legal basis and practical means of performance.</p><p>Test settlement terms from the perspective of later performance. Identify who must act, what counts as completion and which claims are released at which point. A broad promise can conceal disagreement about authority, quality or timing. Compare the negotiated outcome with lawful alternatives without presenting an unsupported trial prediction as certainty. A balanced agreement should expose unresolved issues rather than describing every concession as a final solution.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a school’s procurement officer agrees at mediation to release all claims against a supplier, but the officer’s authority is limited and the goods remain disputed. Investigate actual settlement authority and the proposed release. A handshake in front of a mediator does not answer whether the school was validly bound or what performance is owed.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read applicable Liberian commercial and civil-procedure provisions for negotiation, settlement and arbitration. Use UNCITRAL texts for comparison and verify any relevant treaty status through an official record.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://legal.un.org/avl/ha/crefaa/crefaa.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Foreign arbitral awards: convention and source materials — UN legal library (verify status and local application)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Negotiation, Mediation and Settlement Authority”, start by separating interest from settlement authority. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Negotiation seeks agreement through party interaction. Mediation uses a neutral facilitator without necessarily conferring power to impose a decision. Settlement authority determines who may commit the relevant party.</li><li>Separate interest from settlement authority.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interest</td><td>Underlying concern distinct from the stated legal position</td></tr><tr><td>Settlement authority</td><td>Power to commit a party to the relevant agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Release</td><td>Term discharging specified claims within its lawful scope</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Interest</summary><p>Underlying concern distinct from the stated legal position</p></details><details><summary>Settlement authority</summary><p>Power to commit a party to the relevant agreement</p></details><details><summary>Release</summary><p>Term discharging specified claims within its lawful scope</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A mediator proposes a solution. What should not be assumed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That authority still matters</li><li>That settlement terms should be recorded</li><li>That it is automatically binding like an adjudicated award</li><li>That parties may consider it</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Facilitation and decision-making power are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>A representative signs a broad release. What must be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic power from attendance alone</li><li>That every future claim worldwide disappears</li><li>Only the mediator’s confidence</li><li>Authority and the lawful scope of claims resolved</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Settlement depends on competent parties and defined terms.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes interests from positions?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Interests explain underlying needs while positions state proposed outcomes</li><li>Interests are always legally binding</li><li>Positions never matter</li><li>Every disagreement has identical interests</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The distinction can reveal workable settlement options.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Interest and Settlement authority.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of interest and settlement authority. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned alternative dispute resolution research task: Draft a negotiation plan and settlement agreement with a precise claims schedule, authority check and implementation timetable. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a negotiation plan and settlement agreement with a precise claims schedule, authority check and implementation timetable.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M25:Arbitration Agreements, Awards and Enforcement Defences": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 25</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ARBITRATION AGREEMENTS, AWARDS AND ENFORCEMENT DEFENCES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Alternative Dispute Resolution</td><td>Arbitration Agreements, Awards and Enforcement Defences</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain arbitration agreements, awards and enforcement defences using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Arbitration rests on a legally effective submission to a decision-making process. Agreement scope, tribunal authority, procedural fairness and award enforcement are separate issues governed by applicable law and any relevant convention.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Seat</td><td>Legal location whose framework is relevant to the arbitration</td></tr><tr><td>Tribunal jurisdiction</td><td>Authority to decide the submitted dispute</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement defence</td><td>Ground recognised by applicable law for resisting enforcement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read the exact clause and identify parties, disputes covered, seat, rules and appointment mechanism. Do not treat a reference to amicable discussion as automatically an arbitration agreement. Verify the local framework and any treaty basis, including status and scope, before promising cross-border enforcement.</p><p>Distinguish a merits disagreement from a recognised challenge or enforcement ground. Obtain the complete award, agreement, procedural record and evidence of notice. A party’s absence may raise different questions depending on proper notice and opportunity to participate. An advanced opinion evaluates available defences and competent routes without assuming either that every award is immune from review or that a court will simply rehear all facts.</p><p>An enforcement file should separate the agreement, tribunal process, award status and receiving court’s role. A challenge to consent is different from an allegation that the tribunal reached the wrong factual conclusion. State the legal basis for each objection and the evidence needed to support it. Verify the applicable convention and local framework before discussing cross-border effect, and explain why a recognised ground does or does not apply to the actual record.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a contract calls for “discussion by respected persons” but names no binding process. One party obtains a document called an award without notifying the other and seeks local enforcement. Analyse agreement, authority, notice and the actual legal route. The heading “award” does not establish enforceability.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read applicable Liberian commercial and civil-procedure provisions for negotiation, settlement and arbitration. Use UNCITRAL texts for comparison and verify any relevant treaty status through an official record.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://legal.un.org/avl/ha/crefaa/crefaa.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Foreign arbitral awards: convention and source materials — UN legal library (verify status and local application)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Arbitration Agreements, Awards and Enforcement Defences”, start by separating seat from tribunal jurisdiction. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Arbitration rests on a legally effective submission to a decision-making process. Agreement scope, tribunal authority, procedural fairness and award enforcement are separate issues governed by applicable law and any relevant convention.</li><li>Separate seat from tribunal jurisdiction.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Seat</td><td>Legal location whose framework is relevant to the arbitration</td></tr><tr><td>Tribunal jurisdiction</td><td>Authority to decide the submitted dispute</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement defence</td><td>Ground recognised by applicable law for resisting enforcement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Seat</summary><p>Legal location whose framework is relevant to the arbitration</p></details><details><summary>Tribunal jurisdiction</summary><p>Authority to decide the submitted dispute</p></details><details><summary>Enforcement defence</summary><p>Ground recognised by applicable law for resisting enforcement</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A contract mentions amicable discussion. What is required before asserting arbitration?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic conversion of every discussion clause into arbitration</li><li>Only a title saying award</li><li>The assumption that all mediation is binding</li><li>Analysis of whether a legally effective submission to binding arbitration exists</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The agreement’s meaning and governing requirements matter.</p></details><p><strong>An award was issued without participation by one party. What should be investigated?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Notice, opportunity to participate and applicable challenge or enforcement rules</li><li>Automatic invalidity regardless of notice</li><li>Automatic validity regardless of process</li><li>Only the award’s length</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Absence and procedural fairness require a factual and legal analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish merits disagreement from an enforcement defence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>All factual disputes are irrelevant everywhere</li><li>The receiving court’s role may be limited by the governing framework</li><li>Every enforcement process is a complete new trial</li><li>Awards never face any legal scrutiny</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Available review depends on recognised grounds and competent procedure.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Seat and Tribunal jurisdiction.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of seat and tribunal jurisdiction. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned alternative dispute resolution research task: Write a 1,200-word arbitration-and-enforcement opinion with a clause audit, procedural chronology and verified local or treaty authorities. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,200-word arbitration-and-enforcement opinion with a clause audit, procedural chronology and verified local or treaty authorities.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M26:Moot Court, Issue Selection and Responsive Oral Advocacy": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 26</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: MOOT COURT, ISSUE SELECTION AND RESPONSIVE ORAL ADVOCACY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trial Advocacy and Moot Court</td><td>Moot Court, Issue Selection and Responsive Oral Advocacy</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain moot court, issue selection and responsive oral advocacy using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Oral advocacy presents a structured legal argument responsive to the decision-maker’s questions. A case theory links law, material facts and requested relief; it must remain faithful to the record.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Case theory</td><td>Coherent explanation connecting material facts, law and relief</td></tr><tr><td>Concession</td><td>Candid acceptance of a point within a defined scope</td></tr><tr><td>Responsiveness</td><td>Direct engagement with the question actually asked</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the issue the court must decide and the precise order sought. Organise submissions around propositions that can be supported by authorities and record references. A long chronology is not a substitute for an argument. Distinguish concessions about weak points from abandonment of the entire position.</p><p>Practise answering the question asked before returning to the structure of the submission. If a premise is disputed, explain why with a record reference. Do not invent a fact to escape a difficult question or claim certainty where the authority is unsettled. An advanced advocate can state the strongest adverse case fairly and explain the relevant distinction. The classroom assessment rewards accuracy, responsiveness and reasoned limits rather than dramatic performance.</p><p>Rehearse a difficult question by preparing three components: a direct answer, the best supporting authority or record reference and a candid limit. If the question reveals a fatal gap in the main argument, identify any narrower lawful position rather than inventing a fact. The purpose of the exercise is disciplined persuasion under scrutiny. Assess the submission by accuracy and responsiveness as well as structure, pace and time management.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: a bench asks why a disputed invoice term bound the buyer, but counsel repeats the contract history. A better response addresses incorporation directly, identifies the supporting evidence and accepts what remains uncertain. If the record lacks assent, the advocate should consider a narrower argument rather than inventing acceptance.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the relevant Liberian court rules, professional ethics provisions and selected published judgment. Use a wholly fictional record for oral and written advocacy; this is a simulation, not supervised legal practice.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Moot Court, Issue Selection and Responsive Oral Advocacy”, start by separating case theory from concession. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Oral advocacy presents a structured legal argument responsive to the decision-maker’s questions. A case theory links law, material facts and requested relief; it must remain faithful to the record.</li><li>Separate case theory from concession.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Case theory</td><td>Coherent explanation connecting material facts, law and relief</td></tr><tr><td>Concession</td><td>Candid acceptance of a point within a defined scope</td></tr><tr><td>Responsiveness</td><td>Direct engagement with the question actually asked</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Case theory</summary><p>Coherent explanation connecting material facts, law and relief</p></details><details><summary>Concession</summary><p>Candid acceptance of a point within a defined scope</p></details><details><summary>Responsiveness</summary><p>Direct engagement with the question actually asked</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A bench asks about incorporation of a term. What is the best response?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Repeat the full chronology without addressing it</li><li>Invent a missing acceptance email</li><li>Refuse all questions</li><li>Answer that legal and evidential question directly before returning to the outline</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Responsive advocacy engages with the decision-maker’s actual concern.</p></details><p><strong>The record does not support a desired fact. What should counsel do?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Acknowledge the limit and develop a lawful argument on the actual record</li><li>Create a plausible new fact</li><li>Present speculation as testimony</li><li>Delete the contrary document</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Advocacy requires fidelity to the record.</p></details><p><strong>Why state adverse authority accurately?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Misquotation is acceptable if persuasive</li><li>Credible distinction requires engaging with what the authority actually decides</li><li>Opposing authority can never matter</li><li>Accuracy is optional in a simulation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Sound advocacy confronts the real contrary case.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Case theory and Concession.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of case theory and concession. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned trial advocacy and moot court research task: Prepare two seven-minute arguments for opposing sides of a fictional contract dispute, with a one-page issue map and answers to six likely bench questions. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare two seven-minute arguments for opposing sides of a fictional contract dispute, with a one-page issue map and answers to six likely bench questions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M26:Appellate Submissions, Standards of Review and Reasoned Opinions": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Advanced Liberian Legal Studies Programme • Module 26</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: APPELLATE SUBMISSIONS, STANDARDS OF REVIEW AND REASONED OPINIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trial Advocacy and Moot Court</td><td>Appellate Submissions, Standards of Review and Reasoned Opinions</td><td>Advanced independent legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain appellate submissions, standards of review and reasoned opinions using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Appellate advocacy challenges or defends a decision through the applicable review framework. A standard of review concerns the reviewing body’s approach to the identified issue; it must be established from local authority rather than assumed from a foreign appeal.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ground of appeal</td><td>Defined alleged error connected to the decision under review</td></tr><tr><td>Record reference</td><td>Pinpoint to material actually in the relevant proceedings</td></tr><tr><td>Disposition</td><td>Precise legal outcome or order reached on review</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the decision, appeal route, preserved issue, alleged error and remedy. Separate a factual disagreement from an error of law or procedure where the local framework distinguishes them. Construct a record index and tie every material assertion to it. An appellate court cannot be assumed to hear new evidence or retry every issue.</p><p>Draft grounds that identify the legal error and its effect on the disposition. Explain the strongest response from the other side and why the requested order follows if the error is established. For a reasoned opinion, state the issue, governing authority, findings or review conclusions and precise disposition. An advanced final project argues both sides before deciding, demonstrating the difference between advocacy and impartial adjudicative reasoning.</p><p>A reasoned disposition should follow the analysis of error and consequence. If the reviewing body accepts one ground but rejects another, explain which findings remain and what further competent process, if any, is required. The opinion should allow the parties to understand exactly what was decided. Writing both sides first helps expose assumptions that partisan drafting can conceal and makes the final adjudicative reasoning more transparent.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional problem: an appeal says the trial judge was unfair but identifies no ruling, preserved objection or effect on the result. The learner should locate the actual decision, relevant record and governing review approach. If a procedural error affected only part of the case, the proposed disposition should explain why a full retrial or narrower response is justified.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the relevant Liberian court rules, professional ethics provisions and selected published judgment. Use a wholly fictional record for oral and written advocacy; this is a simulation, not supervised legal practice.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 15 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>For “Appellate Submissions, Standards of Review and Reasoned Opinions”, start by separating ground of appeal from record reference. Apply a verified local rule to the disputed facts, develop the strongest contrary interpretation and justify the scope of the conclusion. A label or foreign quotation alone does not establish the required legal basis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Appellate advocacy challenges or defends a decision through the applicable review framework. A standard of review concerns the reviewing body’s approach to the identified issue; it must be established from local authority rather than assumed from a foreign appeal.</li><li>Separate ground of appeal from record reference.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ground of appeal</td><td>Defined alleged error connected to the decision under review</td></tr><tr><td>Record reference</td><td>Pinpoint to material actually in the relevant proceedings</td></tr><tr><td>Disposition</td><td>Precise legal outcome or order reached on review</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Ground of appeal</summary><p>Defined alleged error connected to the decision under review</p></details><details><summary>Record reference</summary><p>Pinpoint to material actually in the relevant proceedings</p></details><details><summary>Disposition</summary><p>Precise legal outcome or order reached on review</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>An appeal alleges unfairness without identifying a ruling. What is needed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A defined error, supporting record, applicable review approach and effect on the result</li><li>More forceful adjectives</li><li>An assumption that every appeal retries all facts</li><li>An invented preserved objection</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Appellate grounds must connect to a reviewable decision and legal consequence.</p></details><p><strong>Why verify the standard of review locally?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only the case title matters</li><li>The reviewing role and approach depend on the governing framework</li><li>All foreign appeal formulas are identical</li><li>The standard is chosen by the appellant</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Review authority and method require an applicable legal basis.</p></details><p><strong>A procedural defect affected one issue only. What should remedial argument address?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A guaranteed damages award</li><li>The removal of all record references</li><li>Why the proposed scope of relief follows from the defect’s effect</li><li>Automatic reversal of every unrelated finding</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> A disposition should be justified by the established error and its significance.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Ground of appeal and Record reference.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Using the fictional problem above, prepare opposing analyses of ground of appeal and record reference. Each side must identify a record gap capable of changing the result.</li><li>Complete the assigned trial advocacy and moot court research task: Write opposing 1,500-word appellate submissions and a 1,200-word reasoned opinion using one fictional record, with a verified local review framework. Exchange a source-and-reasoning audit with a study partner, or perform a written self-audit.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write opposing 1,500-word appellate submissions and a 1,200-word reasoned opinion using one fictional record, with a verified local review framework.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M27:Independent Research Capstone": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 27 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Independent Research Capstone</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>Choose a narrow Liberian legal question arising from the course. Use published sources and fictional or properly public facts; do not collect private client data or claim to conduct supervised legal practice.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A 700-word proposal with question, method, scope, ethics and source plan</li><li>A 3,500–4,500-word critical research paper using at least twelve checked sources, including relevant primary authorities, and an annotated bibliography</li><li>A claim-to-source matrix, adverse-authority discussion, limitations statement and 500-word revision reflection</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M27:Professional Moot and Reasoned Opinion": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 27 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Professional Moot and Reasoned Opinion</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>Use one fictional appellate record about a disputed supply agreement, board authority, community access and evidence. Establish the applicable local review framework; argue both sides before deciding.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>Two opposing 1,500-word memorials with authorities, record pinpoints and precise requested relief</li><li>Two eight-minute oral submissions and responses to six bench questions per side; self-record or rehearse privately</li><li>A 1,500-word reasoned opinion, twenty-six-subject portfolio index and rubric-based self-assessment</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>"};
if(typeof document!=="undefined"&&!document.getElementById("advanced-law-note-style")){var s=document.createElement("style");s.id="advanced-law-note-style";s.textContent=".law-note{font-size:1rem;line-height:1.8;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.law-note h3{color:#002868;font-size:1.4rem;line-height:1.4}.law-note h4{color:#002868;margin:1.8rem 0 .6rem}.law-banner{background:linear-gradient(120deg,#002868,#087a9e);color:white;border-radius:12px;padding:1rem;font-weight:700}.law-table-wrap{overflow-x:auto;margin:1rem 0}.law-note table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;min-width:280px}.law-note th,.law-note td{padding:.75rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}.law-note th{background:#eff6ff}.law-note details{border:1px solid #bfdbfe;border-radius:10px;padding:.8rem;margin:.7rem 0;background:#f8fbff}.law-note summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#002868;min-height:28px}.law-note a{color:#075985;text-decoration:underline}.law-callout{border-left:4px solid #e31e24;background:#fff7ed;padding:1rem;margin:1.5rem 0}.law-footnote{font-size:.85rem;color:#475569}@media(max-width:480px){.law-note h3{font-size:1.15rem}.law-note td,.law-note th{padding:.5rem}}@media print{.law-note details>*{display:block}.law-note{font-size:11pt}.law-callout{break-inside:avoid}} .law-note>h3+.law-table-wrap table{min-width:520px}";document.head.appendChild(s);}})();
